\noindent $\bullet$
\textbf{First commercial private network measurements focused on indoor neutral-host deployments (\S\ref{sec:deployment}):} We believe our measurement campaign is the first to comprehensively measure a private neutral-host deployment. These measurements are carried out indoors and outdoors to quantify building and environmental losses.

\noindent $\bullet$
\textbf{Comparison of outdoor and indoor characterization of a neutral-host and mid-band 5G deployments (\S\ref{sec:coverage}):} We present a comprehensive analysis that includes deployment configurations, as well as heatmaps and distributions of the received signal strengths. By comparing indoor signal strengths from outdoor 5G deployments and outdoor reception of indoor CBRS deployments, we offer insights into signal propagation characteristics and provide a foundation for further investigation of building/environment loss.

% \noindent $\bullet$
% \textbf{Indoor/outdoor isolation (\S\ref{sec:indoor_outdoor_rsrp}):} Using indoor and outdoor data at varying locations, we demonstrate the high outdoor-to-indoor and indoor-to-outdoor losses. These high losses due to penetration loss indicate good signal isolation between indoor and outdoor environments.

\noindent $\bullet$
\textbf{Detailed insight into the performance of a neutral-host deployment (\S\ref{sec:dl_analysis},\S\ref{sec:ul_analysis}):} We show that the neutral-host network provides better indoor performance compared to MNO deployed 5G mid-band, in both downlink and uplink, due to the proximity and density of CBRS devices (CBSDs) deployed indoors. Moreover, the UE reports lower TX power indoors when connected to the neutral-host, and use of the neutral-host by indoor users creates additional capacity for the MNO to serve other customers by freeing up resource blocks.

% \noindent $\bullet$
% \textbf{Comparison of signal strength between CBRS and 5~GHz Wi-Fi (\S\ref{sec:rssi})}: While the 3.55~GHz CBRS and 5~GHz Wi-Fi bands are separated by a significant frequency gap and have different maximum transmit power levels, we present a comparative analysis of their signal reception and building loss in the same locations for a preliminary first look to understand mid-band network characteristics. This is particularly relevant for exploring the feasibility of low-power, non-AFC (Automatic Frequency Control) sharing schemes.  Our measurements reveal similar CBRS and Wi-Fi signal reception, both indoors and outdoors, under both full-buffer downlink and ping traffic conditions.

These findings from real-world deployments will inform regulators as they make decisions regarding the future use of mid-band spectrum, including the 3.1 - 3.45~GHz, upper 6~GHz, and 7.125 - 8.4~GHz bands.


