\subsection{Hyperparameters}
\label{sec:hyperparameters}

All models were evaluated under a unified inference setup to ensure fairness and reproducibility. Experiments were run on an NVIDIA A100 GPU (40GB VRAM) using a batch size of 32. Visual inputs were resized to \(224 \times 224\) pixels, and text inputs were tokenized using model-specific tokenizers—BERT for LXMERT and VisualBERT, and byte-level BPE for CLIP and BLIP. The maximum text sequence length was capped at 40 tokens, with truncation and padding applied. Inference used mixed-precision (FP16) for efficiency, with 10 warm-up iterations before measurement. Dropout (rate 0.1) was retained but models were run in evaluation mode. No test-time augmentation or ensembling was applied. Implementations used PyTorch 2.0.1 and HuggingFace Transformers 4.31, with preprocessing via OpenCV 4.8.0 and SpaCy 3.6.1. Visual features were normalized per-channel, and all pretrained weights were sourced from official repositories to ensure fidelity. \textit{\textbf{Note:}} Code will be released post-acceptance.


\section{Results and Analysis}

\subsection{Image-Text Retrieval}

Image-text retrieval evaluates a VLM's ability to align visual inputs with textual descriptions. We benchmarked CLIP, BLIP, and LXMERT on COCO, Flickr30k, and Visual Genome using standard metrics—Recall@K (\(K = 1, 5, 10\)), MRR, and MedR (see Table~\ref{tab:retrieval-results}). BLIP outperformed others across COCO and Flickr30k. On COCO, BLIP achieved R@1 of 78.9\%, surpassing CLIP (74.0\%) and LXMERT (71.2\%), with consistent gains in R@5/10, MRR (0.84), and MedR (1). On Flickr30k, BLIP reached R@1 of 76.5\%, ahead of CLIP by 4.4\% and LXMERT by 7.6\%, reflecting its robust alignment and ranking capabilities. CLIP showed strong generalization due to its large-scale contrastive training, but lagged in fine-grained alignment. LXMERT, while weaker on COCO/Flickr30k, showed relative strength on Visual Genome’s text-to-image retrieval (R@1 = 70.4\%), likely due to its structured reasoning modules. BLIP followed closely (68.3\%), and CLIP trailed (66.8\%) with higher MedR (3). BLIP’s consistent MRR and low MedR values suggest superior retrieval fidelity, attributed to its fusion of generative modeling and vision-language pretraining. In contrast, LXMERT’s inconsistent scores highlight its sensitivity to visual complexity and distractors.

\subsection{Visual Question Answering}

We evaluate VLMs on CLEVR and VCR (see Table~\ref{tab:vqa-results}), covering both compositional reasoning and commonsense inference. On CLEVR, LXMERT achieves 96.3\% accuracy, outperforming BLIP (89.7\%) and CLIP (84.5\%), thanks to its explicit relational reasoning modules that effectively handle structured, logic-driven queries. The synthetic nature of CLEVR benefits LXMERT’s architecture, which incorporates object-level attention and spatial reasoning. For VCR, which tests real-world commonsense reasoning, BLIP leads in the \texttt{Q→A} task with 74.8\%, driven by its strong vision-language pretraining. In the \texttt{QA→R} subtask, LXMERT again excels (71.5\%), showcasing its ability to model cross-modal rationale generation. CLIP scores lowest in both (\texttt{Q→A}: 62.3\%, \texttt{QA→R}: 58.9\%), indicating its limited reasoning beyond contrastive alignment. Overall, LXMERT dominates structured reasoning tasks, while BLIP shows strength in semantic alignment and commonsense QA.

