\subsection{Caption Generation}

Table~\ref{tab:caption-results} compares captioning performance on COCO and Flickr30k using BLEU-4, METEOR, CIDEr, and SPICE. BLIP outperforms LXMERT and CLIP across all metrics, achieving top CIDEr scores (134.5 on COCO, 95.2 on Flickr30k) and SPICE scores (22.4, 20.8), indicating strong alignment with human-like and semantically structured captions. Its high METEOR values (29.7, 27.3) highlight robust synonym and paraphrase handling—critical for natural-sounding output. LXMERT delivers competitive BLEU-4 results (34.1\%, 31.5\%) but falls short in METEOR and CIDEr, reflecting limited semantic diversity despite syntactic correctness. CLIP performs worst overall, with BLEU-4 scores of 22.3\% (COCO) and 19.7\% (Flickr30k), underlining its unsuitability for generation without task-specific fine-tuning. 

\subsection{Computational Efficiency}

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:efficiency-multidataset-vertical}, CLIP is the most efficient model across all benchmarks, offering the lowest latency (28--35~ms), minimal memory usage (0.8--1.1~GB), and the fewest FLOPs (33--38~GFLOPs). This efficiency stems from its streamlined architecture and contrastive training paradigm. In contrast, BLIP and LXMERT incur significantly higher costs. LXMERT reaches up to 180~ms latency and 4.5~GB memory usage on Visual Genome, due to its heavy multimodal fusion and cross-attention modules. BLIP, while more efficient than LXMERT, still demands 3.2--4.1~GB memory and over 120~GFLOPs on average. Throughput metrics confirm this trend: CLIP handles 29--35 samples/s, BLIP manages 11--15, and LXMERT trails with 8--11, especially struggling with reasoning-heavy datasets like VCR. These results underscore CLIP’s practicality for real-time and resource-constrained deployments.

\textit{\textbf{Note:}} All computational efficiency results were obtained using NVIDIA A100 GPUs to ensure consistent benchmarking across large-scale models. Edge devices such as Jetson Nano or Raspberry Pi, while relevant for deployment, lack the memory and compute capacity to run models like BLIP and LXMERT in full precision without aggressive quantization or architectural pruning. Evaluating on A100 offers a reliable upper-bound analysis of efficiency and throughput in realistic server-side or cloud-inference scenarios, aligning with industry practices for scalable vision-language applications.


