\subsection{Cross-Dataset Generalization}

Table~\ref{tab:cdc-results} reports the CDC scores across five benchmarks to assess generalization. CLIP achieves the highest CDC score of 0.92, reflecting strong robustness across both curated (e.g., COCO) and synthetic (e.g., CLEVR) datasets. This stems from its contrastive pretraining on diverse web-scale data and shared embedding space architecture. BLIP shows moderate generalization with a CDC score of 0.76, performing well on datasets like Flickr30k and VCR but varying on less curated domains. LXMERT scores lowest (0.64), indicating weaker cross-domain consistency—effective on reasoning tasks but less adaptable to broader contexts due to its specialized architecture and narrower pretraining scope. These results highlight CLIP's suitability for general-purpose applications, while BLIP and LXMERT are better aligned with task-specific deployments. CDC thus serves as a practical metric for evaluating robustness in real-world scenarios.

