\begin{abstract}


We introduce a model of controlled sequential social learning in which a planner may pay a cost to adjust the private information structure of agents. The planner may seek to induce correct actions that are consistent with an unknown true state of the world (altruistic planner) or to induce a specific action the planner prefers (biased planner). Our framework presents a new optimization problem for social learning that combines dynamic programming with decentralized action choices and Bayesian belief updates. This sheds light on practical policy questions, such as how the socially optimal level of ad personalization changes according to current beliefs or how a political campaign may selectively illuminate or obfuscate the winning potential of its candidate among voters. We then prove the convexity of the value function and characterize the optimal policies of altruistic and biased planners, which attain desired tradeoffs between the costs they incur and the payoffs they earn from the choices they induce in the agents. Even for a planner who has equivalent knowledge to an individual, cannot lie or cherry-pick information, and is fully observable, we demonstrate that it is possible to dramatically influence social welfare in both positive and negative directions. 
\end{abstract}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{INTRODUCTION}
\label{sec:intro}


Social learning involves individuals (referred to as agents) estimating an unknown state of the world by observing each other's actions and from their private information, which is specific for each agent. This estimate affects the agent's observable action, which in turn further influences the beliefs and, subsequently, actions of others. Social learning has been shown to significantly influence the adoption of vaccines (e.g., \cite{rao2007social, bauch2012evolutionary}), new technology (e.g. \cite{gillingham2021social, weber2012social}), and evolution of political and moral opinions (e.g., \cite{brady2021social,guilbeault2018social}). For example, \cite{rao2007social} shows that individuals who interact with vaccinated peers are more likely to opt for vaccines themselves.

Pioneering works in economic theory have provided theoretical foundations for sequential social learning in which agents act in a predetermined sequence \cite{economics1992, Bikhchandani1992}. The classical model demonstrates the phenomenon of \emph{information cascades} -- \emph{absorbing states} in which agents' beliefs about the state of the world become so strong that they overpower all private information and social learning halts. This can lead to regimes where nearly all agents make suboptimal choices. Generalizations and variations of the classical model include characterization of asymptotic behavior and conditions for complete learning (e.g. \cite{Smith2000, Arieli2021}), considerations of different channels of information transmission (e.g., \cite{Dasaratha2022, Le2016}), alternate decision processes (e.g., \cite{Bistritz2022}), and agents with various kinds of biases and misperceptions (e.g., \cite{Eyster2010, golub2010naive, bohren2018}).

We augment the classical sequential social learning model by introducing a central planner who chooses the private signal precision of each agent, with different choices differently influencing the learning and actions of the agents and incurring different costs for the planner. For example, a more precise private signal may be more expensive but helps the agent better learn the true state of the world. This opens up several intriguing questions. Can a planner capitalize upon social learning to steer it toward a given objective? How should the planner choose the precisions to accomplish the above? How do the social learning dynamics depend upon the planner’s investment?

Importantly, the answers to all such questions and their ramifications depend upon the objective of the planner. An altruistic planner may seek to guide agents toward acting consistent with the true state of the world. In contrast, a biased one may wish to guide agents toward one particular action, regardless of whether that action is consistent with the true state of the world. The optimal policies and the outcomes of social learning dynamics would differ in the two cases. We formulate the social learning problem for both types of planners and provide frameworks to obtain the choices of the agents and the planner that optimize their respective objectives and characterize how much a planner can help or harm social welfare via steering social learning.


\subsection{Related Works}


The literature on the \emph{control} of social learning is relatively limited. \cite{wei2022} introduces a self-interested coordinator who offers additional information to agents in exchange for payment and seeks to maximize its income from the agents. In contrast, our planners' utility depends on the actions taken by the agents and the social learning process, as well as the costs incurred in realizing desired precisions for the private signals of the agents. Besides, our model only assumes one-sided interactions between the planner and the agents.

A planner has also been considered for information fusion problems among networks of social sensors \cite{krishnamurthySocialLearningBayesian2013, krishnamurthyQuickestDetectionPOMDPs2012, bhattControlledSequentialInformation2021}. The planner in \cite{krishnamurthySocialLearningBayesian2013} and \cite{krishnamurthyQuickestDetectionPOMDPs2012} determines when sufficient information has been gathered to detect a change in the process being monitored and stops the social learning process accordingly but does not influence the actions of the agents otherwise. Furthermore, the planner's action space in these works is finite, whereas it is uncountably infinite in ours, leading to additional technical challenges. \cite{bhattControlledSequentialInformation2021} is closer to us in that the planner influences the agents' actions but with objectives and control mechanisms different from ours. The planner in \cite{bhattControlledSequentialInformation2021}
incentivizes the agents to adopt actions consistent with their private information, thereby revealing their private information. In contrast, our planner wants the agents to adopt a specific action (if the planner is biased) or actions consistent with the world's true state (if the planner is altruistic). Also, our planner influences the agents by changing their information structure, specifically by choosing the precisions of their private signals rather than by directly paying them as in \cite{bhattControlledSequentialInformation2021}.


\subsection{Applications}\label{sec:applications}


We further motivate our work with two topical examples: targeted digital ads and persuasive large language models (LLMs). The crux of our planner's power is his control over information provision. Both targeted digital ads and LLMs have engendered dramatic increases in the prevalence and potential of such power. We will consider altruistic and biased planners in each example.


\subsubsection{Targeted Digital Ads}\label{sec:targeted_ads} 


Consider a sequence of individuals (agents in our terminology) deciding whether to patronize a business or service provider (e.g., a restaurant, contractor, realtor, etc.) and a recommendation system (the planner) serving the agents information about the business. The true state of the world, assumed to be binary, is whether the business is good, which is unknown. Both the recommendation system and the agents want to be consistent with the unknown true state. That is, if and only if the business is good, agents want to patronize it, and the recommendation system wants to induce the agents to do so. The recommendation system is an \emph{altruistic planner} in this sense. 

The recommendation system can show an agent a highly targeted (\emph{precise} in our terminology) ad that showcases the strengths and weaknesses of the business in contexts that she can relate to, given her background and characteristics, or it can show her a generic or confusing ad
that would not help inform her action. The precise signal will be more informative, comprehensible, and relatable for the agent. As such, it is more likely to drive her to the correct conclusion, i.e., patronize the business if and only if it is good. Social welfare increases as more agents arrive at correct decisions. However, changing the precision of an ad also incurs cost, as it involves tailoring to the agent’s specific background. The recommendation system must then choose the precisions so as to maximize social welfare minus the costs. 

We now provide a real-life example of a \emph{biased planner.} Consider a group of voters (agents) motivated to support the candidate most likely to win in their community. The motivation to "back a winner" has been shown to influence electoral outcomes, e.g., this is why US states with early primary elections have an outsized impact on election results (see \cite{bartels_presidential_1988}). The planner is a specific candidate's campaign and, therefore, seeks to motivate agents to back the candidate. The binary true state of the world indicates if the candidate is winning or otherwise. Thus, if an agent knew the true state, she would back the candidate if and only if he were winning. 

Each agent understands her community well and would know if the candidate is winning (i.e., the true state of the world) if she knows his stances and policies. She would know the latter correctly if those are provided to her in a manner that she digests information best. For example, some agents understand audio-visuals best, some long-form articles backed by facts, figures, and citations, some only brief and focused contents, and some only their native language, etc.

The campaign sends digital ads (i.e., private signals) to agents in varying degrees of precision. The precision represents how much the content is tailored to the agent’s taste. Note that a precise signal accurately conveys the true state of the world to an agent by helping her clearly understand the candidate’s policies, track record, and character, which enables her to correctly infer if the candidate will win. A precise signal does not necessarily mean that the agent backs the planner's candidate, though. For example, if the candidate loses in the agent’s community as per the true state of the world, a highly precise signal would induce the agent to oppose him. However, an imprecise signal is more likely to induce the agent into backing him since it obfuscates the true state of the world from the agent (possibly by being vague or confusing), thus increasing the chance that she thinks he is winning. Thus, the biased planner may be incentivized to decrease an agent's signal precision if he thinks that his candidate is losing. To tailor the ad to an agent, the planner must research how the agent best understands any content, which incurs costs for the planner. Even rendering the signal to be really imprecise is costly, as it still requires tailoring to the specific agent, e.g., the planner needs to know that an agent best understands focused and brief messages to be able to decrease precision by confusing her with long-form verbose, detailed articles. The planner selects the precisions so as to maximize the expected number of backers minus the cost incurred in generating the precisions. 


\subsubsection{Persuasive LLMs}\label{sec:llms}


The use of LLMs, while still nascent, is growing rapidly, with recent surveys indicating that over $75\%$ of large businesses and over $50\%$ of their respective C-level executives are regularly using generative AI at work, with the most popular variety being LLM chat-bots \cite{StateAIGlobal}. Furthermore, several works have demonstrated that state-of-the-art LLMs possess persuasive ability on par with humans, including on technology adoption, political choice, and opinion building \cite{breumPersuasivePowerLarge2024,potterHiddenPersuadersLLMs2024}.

Here, consider the provider of an LLM to be the planner and the users of the LLM to be agents. An agent's private signal corresponds to the LLM's responses to her relevant queries, e.g., on the pros and cons of a technology or a candidate's policies and track record in an election. A high-precision (low-precision, respectively) signal might correspond to a detailed and specific (vague) response. To change signal precision from a default value, the planner must spend resources researching the agent so that he can tailor the response. The planner can either be altruistic or biased. The former seeks to maximize the number of agents arriving at correct decisions (i.e., social welfare) minus the cost of control. The latter seeks to maximize the number of agents who adopt the planner’s preferred technology, candidate, or opinion minus the cost of control. 


\subsection{Contributions and Outline}


We introduce a formal model of sequential social learning with precision investment control motivated by a diverse range of applications. As in classical sequential social learning, in this model, a predetermined sequence of decentralized agents seek to choose actions that align with an unknown binary true state of the world, which they must learn from their private signals and observation of each others' actions. Our innovation has been to combine a dynamic program with this classical version to allow a centralized planner to control this social learning by choosing the precisions of the private signals of the agents in accordance with his objectives. We consider two different objectives of the planner: inducing the agents to 1) act consistent with the true state (altruistic planner, Section~\ref{sec:alt_planner_prob}) or 2) choose a specific action desired by the planner (biased planner, Section \ref{sec:bias_planner_prob}).

We are able to fully specify the optimal choices of the altruistic planner as a function of the system state, which is an evolving public belief about the true state of the world (Section \ref{sec:altruistic_results}). The result is founded upon the convexity of the value function which we establish. The proof for convexity is technically involved and does not follow from existing results on the convexity of value functions. We fully characterize the optimal choice of the biased planner for certain ranges of public belief and bound the optimal choice in other regions (Section \ref{sec:biased_results}). The policy characterizations in Sections~\ref{sec:altruistic_results} and \ref{sec:biased_results} go beyond common intuitions about the system and illuminate the dynamics of interactions between the planner and agents and among agents. These differ greatly from the type of analytical results, such as asymptotic convergence and stability results, typically obtained in fully decentralized social learning (i.e., without a planner). Due to space constraints, proofs have only been outlined in this paper, and the details deferred to \cite{tech_report}. 

Numerical evaluations reveal that, despite having no additional knowledge relative to agents, being required to provide truthful and symmetric information, and all control actions being fully disclosed, the planners still possess significant capacity to steer social learning, for better or for worse (Section~\ref{sec:social_welfare}). 

Summarily, the introduction of a planner who chooses signal precisions is novel for social learning to the best of our knowledge. This addition dramatically shifts the types of applications that can be incorporated, questions that arise, the models one may deploy, the nature of the policies that emerge, the guarantees that can be proven for them, and the social learning dynamics and outcomes.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{SOCIAL LEARNING MODEL}
\label{sec:model}


We consider a countable sequence of Bayes rational agents $i\in\mathbb{N}_{>0}$. At time $i=0$, nature determines a fixed exogenous state of the world $\omega\in\Omega:=\{G,B\}$ such that $\P(\omega=G)=b_1$. In the recommendation system (election campaign, respectively) example of Section \ref{sec:applications}, $G$ is realized when the business (preferred candidate, respectively) is good (winning, respectively). At time $i$, agent $i$ chooses an action $a_i\in\Omega$. She knows $b_1$ but must estimate $\omega$ from her private signal and the choices of agents $j < i$ (i.e., those who act before her). She receives utility $0$ if her action matches the true state $a_i=\omega$ (e.g., patronizing a business or candidate if and only if $\omega = G$), and $-C$ otherwise, with $C>0$. 


\subsection{System Overview}
\label{sec:overview}


At time $i$, agent $i$ receives a binary private signal $s_i\in\Omega$ which matches the state of the world $\omega$ with probability $q_i\in[0.5,1]$ i.e. $\P(s_i=\omega)=q_i$. We refer to $q_i$ as agent $i$'s signal precision. Each agent's signal is independent of those of other agents conditioned upon $\omega$. Essentially, $\omega$ is communicated to agent $i$ via a binary symmetric channel with error probability $1-q_i$. Thus, if the private signal has high precision, the agent infers $\omega$ from it with high accuracy. Note then that $s_i$ depends only upon $\omega$ and $q_i.$

Each agent observes the actions of all her predecessors and their respective signal precisions. This history is denoted $\mathcal{H}_i:=\left(b_1,(q_j,a_j)_{j<i}\right)$. Informed by $\mathcal{H}_i$ and $q_i$, agent $i$ chooses action $a_i\in\Omega$ so as to maximize her utility. If both actions fetch the same utility, she chooses the action that matches $s_i$. 

Based on $\mathcal{H}_i$, there is a shared public belief about $\omega$, which is updated after each agent acts. The public belief $b_i$ just before agent $i$ acts is $\P(\omega=G|\mathcal{H}_i)$, that before any agent acts is the a priori distribution over $\Omega$, $b_1$. As in the classic model of \cite{economics1992} and \cite{Bikhchandani1992}, $(b_i)_{i\in\mathbb{N}}$ is a Markov process.


\subsection{Agents' Decision Problems}
\label{sec:agents}


Agent $i$ obtains a \emph{private} belief, $\tilde{b}_i$, using $\mathcal{H}_i$ and her private signal $s_i$ of precision $q_i$. In \cite{tech_report}, we have derived the following via Bayes' Theorem: 
\begin{align}
\label{private_update}
    \tilde{b}_i=\P(\omega=G|\mathcal{H}_i,q_i,s_i)=\begin{cases}
        \frac{q_i}{1+2b_iq_i-b_i-q_i}b_i&s_i=G\\
        \frac{1-q_i}{b_i+q_i-2b_iq_i}b_i&s_i=B
    \end{cases}
\end{align}

Since agent $i$'s payoff is higher if $a_i=\omega$ than otherwise, she chooses the action corresponding to the state of the world that is more likely as per her posterior belief $\tilde{b}_i$: 
\begin{align}
\label{actions_prelim}
    a_i=\begin{cases}
        G&\tilde{b}_i>0.5\\
        B&\tilde{b}_i<0.5\\
        s_i&\tilde{b}_i=0.5
    \end{cases}
\end{align}

From (\ref{private_update}) and (\ref{actions_prelim}), $a_i$ can be expressed in terms of $b_i, q_i$:
\begin{align}
\label{actions}
    a_i=\begin{cases}
        s_i&1-q_i\leq b_i\leq q_i\\
        G&q_i<b_i\\
        B&q_i<1-b_i
    \end{cases}
\end{align}

Agent $i$'s action is then observed by all other agents and incorporated into the updated public belief $b_{i+1}$, which is:
\begin{align}
\label{public_update}
    b_{i+1}=f(b_i,q_i)=\begin{cases}
        \tilde{b}_i&1-q_i\leq b_i\leq q_i\\
        b_i&\text{o.w.}
    \end{cases}
\end{align}

(\ref{public_update}) has been derived in \cite{tech_report}. The intuition is as follows. When $1-q_i\leq b_i\leq q_i$, agent $i$'s action perfectly reveals her private signal via (\ref{actions}). Thus, the updated public belief is identical to the private belief (\ref{private_update}) of agent $i$. Otherwise, i.e., if $q_i < \max(b_i,1-b_i)$, then from (\ref{actions}), agent $i$'s private signal has no effect on her action, which renders her action uninformative. Thus, the public belief is unchanged, and an absorbing state, referred to as \emph{information cascade} or \emph{herding} in prior works, is reached. 

Agent $i$'s expected utility is $-C \P(a_i\neq\omega|b_i,q_i).$ From (\ref{actions}),
\begin{align}
    \label{mistake_probability}
    \P(a_i\neq\omega|b_i,q_i) = \min(b_i, 1-b_i, 1-q_i).
\end{align}


\subsection{The Planner's Problem}
\label{sec:planners}


We consider two types of planners distinguished by their respective objectives: (1) an altruistic planner who wishes to induce agents to take the correct action ($a_i=\omega$) and (2) a biased planner who wishes to induce a specific action, say $G$, in agents regardless of $\omega$. We denote these different planners with subscripts $A$ and $B$, respectively. In each case, the planner determines the precision of the private signal of each agent. A function $\beta(\cdot)$, which is non-negative, (strictly) increasing, continuous, and concave in its argument, will denote the cost associated with the chosen precisions. 

The planner has an information set $\mathcal{H}_i$ identical to those of the agents. Note that, therefore, the planner does not know the true state of the world $\omega$.\footnote{The planner does not have access to any additional information relative to agents in several real-life applications. For example, consider agents deciding whether to take a new vaccine. At the very early stages, knowledge about vaccine efficacy is limited to clinical trials, and clinical trial data is widely available. Thus, at the onset of the campaign, both the planner and agents may have the same prior belief (based on the clinical trial data) about the efficacy of the vaccine.}


\subsubsection{Altruistic Planner}
\label{sec:alt_planner_prob} 


At time $i$, the altruistic planner chooses the precision $q_i$ for agent $i$ and incurs a cost of $\beta(q_i)$ where $\beta(p) = 0$, $p\in[0.5,1]$. Thus, the planner incurs additional cost only if it increases the precision above a baseline value of $p$, and the additional cost increases with further increase in the precision, with decreasing marginal costs. The agents know $p$ and the function $\beta(\cdot)$. 

The planner seeks to maximize social welfare minus the cost of precision investment, where social welfare is the expected total utility of the agents. This overall objective motivates the following definition of the instantaneous reward for the altruistic planner, $r_A(b_i, q_i)$ when beginning at public belief $b_i$ and choosing signal precision $q_i$ for agent $i$, and recalling that agent $i$'s expected utility is $C \P(a_i\neq\omega|b_i,q_i)$, 
\begin{align}
\label{alt_reward}
    r_A(b_i,q_i)=-\beta(q_i) -C\P(a_i\neq\omega|b_i,q_i).
\end{align}

The planner starting from a public belief $b_1$ chooses a sequence of policies $\pi=(\pi_i)_{i=1}^{\infty}$ such that $\pi_i(\mathcal{H}_i)=q_i$, and attains the following expected total discounted utility, for a discount factor $\delta\in[0,1)$: 
\begin{align*}
    V^{\pi}_A(b_1)=\sum_{i=1}^\infty \delta^{i-1}r_A(b_i,\pi(b_i))
\end{align*}
Let $\Pi$ be the set of all possible policies of the planner. 
The optimal utility and policy of the planner can be defined as follows: 
\begin{defn}{Altruistic Precision Investment Control}
\label{alt_problem}
    \begin{align*}
        V^*_A(b) = \sup_{\pi\in\Pi}V^{\pi}_A(b)\ \forall b\in[0,1], \\\nonumber
    \end{align*} An optimal altruistic policy $\pi^*_A$ is any policy that attains this supremum. 
\end{defn}

The planner's utility maximization problem constitutes an infinite horizon discounted stationary Markov Decision Process (MDP) with state $b_i\in[0,1]$, control $q_i\in[0.5,1]$, and transition function defined by (\ref{public_update}) \cite{PUTERMAN1990331}. Thus, there exists a unique optimal value function and a (not necessarily unique) deterministic Markov optimal policy (Theorem 3.6 and Corollary 3.3, \cite{kallenberg2011markov}). Henceforth, we will restrict our focus to deterministic Markov policies, namely those of the form $\pi:[0,1]\rightarrow[0.5,1]$ such that $\pi(b_i)=q_i$, and restrict $\Pi$ to only such policies. 


\subsubsection{Biased Planner} 
\label{sec:bias_planner_prob}


The difference between the biased planner's problem and the altruistic planner's is in their objectives and, therefore, in the cost and reward functions. Refer to the example for a political campaign in Section~\ref{sec:applications} for elucidation of a biased planner. The biased planner seeks to induce action $G$ from each agent regardless of $\omega.$ When an agent chooses action $G$ (respectively, $B$), the planner, incurs cost $0$ (respectively, $C>0$), regardless of $\omega$. The biased planner can make a private signal either more or less precise than the baseline value of $p$, both of which incur additional costs relative to choosing $p$. Any choice of precision other than $p$ incurs a cost for the biased planner as it requires him to tailor the ad to an agent, which in turn needs research on how the agent best understands any content. The biased planner incurs cost $\beta(|q_i-p|)$ for choosing signal precision $q_i$, with $\beta(0)=0$. 

The biased planner's expected instantaneous reward at time $i$ is then defined as follows:
\begin{align}
\label{biased_reward}
    r_B(b_i,q_i)=-\beta(|q_i-p|)-C\P(a_i=B|b_i,q_i)
\end{align}

Using (\ref{biased_reward}), $V^{\pi}_B(\cdot)$, $V^*_B(\cdot)$, and $\pi^*_B(\cdot)$ can now be defined for the biased planner, as $V^{\pi}_A(\cdot)$, $V^*_A(\cdot)$, and $\pi^*_A(\cdot)$ were defined for the altruistic planner using (\ref{alt_reward}). 

As noted in the first paragraph of Section \ref{sec:model}, each agent still receives a higher utility by choosing an action that matches $\omega$. Thus, if $\omega = B$, the biased planner’s success with an agent lowers the agent's utility. In contrast, since the altruistic planner seeks to have each agent's action match $\omega$, regardless of what $\omega$ is, his success increases the agent's utility. Thus, the altruistic planner's objective is always aligned with that of each agent, while for the biased planner, this is only true when $\omega = G.$ Also, the altruistic planner never lowers the precision below $p$ because he wants each agent to act in accordance with $\omega$ if that does not increase his cost, and $p$ incurs $0$ cost (cost is always nonnegative). 


