\section{OPTIMAL BIASED POLICIES}
\label{sec:biased_results}


We will begin with the myopic optimal policy as we did for the altruistic planner in Section~\ref{sec:altruistic_results}. This, along with Lemma~\ref{biased_mono}, will underpin our analysis of the optimal biased policy. Similar to Section~\ref{sec:altruistic_results}, the myopic biased optimal policy, denoted as $\pi^0_B(\cdot)$, can be defined as
\begin{align}
\label{myopic_opt_policy_biased}
    \pi^0_B(b)\in \arg\sup_{q\in[0.5,1]}\ r_B(b,q)\ \forall b\in[0,1], 
\end{align}

$\pi^0_B(\cdot)$ can be characterized as follows: 
\begin{thm}{Myopic Biased Policy}
\label{myopic_biased_policy}

    There exist $t_1,\dots,t_5\in(0,p]$ with $t_1<1-p<t_2\leq t_3<0.5\leq t_4\leq t_5< p$ such that, 
    \begin{align*}
        \pi^0_B(b)=\begin{cases}
            p&b\leq t_1\\
            1-b&b\in(t_1,1-p]\\
            p&b\in(1-p,t_2)\\
            1-b&b\in[t_2,t_3)\\
            p&b\in[t_3,t_4]\\
            1&b\in(t_4,t_5)\\
            b-\epsilon&[t_5,p)\\
            p&b\geq p
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
\end{thm}

The optimal biased policy has a similar structure and intuition presented later, which also applies to the myopic policy. 

Next, a higher public belief renders agents more likely to choose the biased planner's favored action $G$. Hence: 

\begin{lem}{\hfill}
\label{biased_mono}

    For any $b_1,b_2\in[0,1]$ such that $b_1\leq b_2$, 
    $$V^*_B(b_1)\leq V^*_B(b_2)$$
\end{lem}

The optimal biased policy can be characterized using Lemma~\ref{biased_mono} and the myopic policy stated in Theorem~\ref{myopic_biased_policy}. 

\begin{thm}{Optimal Biased Policy Characterization}
\label{biased_policy}

    For optimal biased policy $\pi^*_B(\cdot)$, there exist $t_1,t_2\in[0,p]$ with $t_1<1-p\leq0.5\leq t_2< p$ such that the following holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(A)] If $b\leq t_1$, then $\pi^*_B(b)=p$.
        \item[(B)] If $b\in(t_1,1-p]$, then $\pi^*_B(b)= 1-b$.
        \item[(C)] If $b\in(1-p,0.5)$, then $\pi^*_B(b)\geq 1-b$
        \item[(D)] If $b\in[0.5,t_2)$, then $\pi^*_B(b)\geq p$.
        \item[(E)] If $b\in(t_2,p)$, then $\pi^*_B(b)=b-\epsilon$.
        \item[(F)] If $b\geq p$, then $\pi^*_B(b)=p$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{thm}

The optimal biased policy exhibits six potential phases.

When public belief is sufficiently low (i.e., condition (A)), the cost required to steer the system may be too great, despite the negative consequences for the planner's utility. In this range, the chosen precision $p$ is less than $\max(b,1-b)$. Therefore, from (\ref{actions}), the agent will act in accordance with the public belief $b<0.5$ and choose action $B$ regardless of her private signal. Since this action is uninformative, it does not change the public belief. Thus, because the policy is Markovian, this process repeats ad infinitum with all future agents selecting action $B$. This corresponds to an unfavorable cascade for the planner.

When public belief is close to an unfavorable cascade, as in (B), the planner increases signal precision so that it is high enough to affect the agent's action despite the fact that, in expectation, the resulting signal will be $B$. When precision is chosen to be $\geq\max(b,1-b)$, the agent will act in accordance with her private signal, which has some non-zero chance of leading to a favorable action for the planner. Essentially, in this phase, the planner invests in a last-ditch effort to steer away from the unfavorable cascade.

For belief values slightly higher (i.e., (C)), the planner may decrease signal precision below $p$. In these ranges, $b<0.5$; therefore, more precise signals are more likely to yield signal realizations of $B$. Thus, the planner will maintain a precision that is strong enough to influence the agent's action ($q\geq\max(b,1-b)$) in the hopes of moving to a more favorable public belief but will do so with the least precise signal possible to reduce the cost incurred.

When public belief weakly favors the planner's desired action (i.e., (D)), the planner adopts precision at least $p$. Since $b>0.5$, an increase in precision makes the agent more likely to infer that $\omega = G$. Investment in this regime is the planner's attempt to bolster public belief.

When public belief is still higher (i.e., (E)), the planner decreases signal precision just below $\max(b,1-b)$ ($b-\epsilon$). Thus, agents ignore private signals and take action $G$. Here, the risk of a private signal overturning the favorable public belief outweighs both the cost of decreasing precision and the potential for public belief to increase further.

Finally, once public belief surpasses $p$ (i.e. (F)), as per (\ref{actions}), the agents take action $G$ (the action preferred by the planner), if the planner selects precision $p$, which incurs the planner $0$ cost. Thus, this is exactly what the planner does. % 


