\section{EVALUATION OF SOCIAL WELFARE}
\label{sec:social_welfare}


We define social welfare $W^{\pi}(b)$ as the total expected discounted utility of all agents under precision policy $\pi$ and beginning at public belief $b$. From (\ref{mistake_probability}), 
\begin{align}
\label{welfare}
    W^{\pi}(b_1)&=\sum_{i=1}^\infty\delta^{i-1}\P(a_i=\omega)\nonumber\\
    &=\sum_{i=1}^\infty\delta^{i-1}\max(b_i,1-b_i,\pi(b_i))
\end{align}

This naturally leads to the fact that increasing signal precision increases social welfare. Put simply, better information never harms \emph{agents}, regardless of its impact on the planner.

\begin{thm}
\label{welfare_mono}{Precision Monotonicity of Social Welfare}

If $\pi_1(b)\leq\pi_2(b)\ \forall b\in[0,1]$, for policies $\pi_1(\cdot), \pi_2(\cdot)$, then 
    $$W^{\pi_1}(b)\leq W^{\pi_2}(b)\ \forall b\in[0,1]$$
\end{thm}

Since the altruistic policies (both myopic and optimal) always choose precision of $p$ or higher, Theorem~\ref{welfare_mono} informs us that their social welfare is higher than that of the baseline case of always choosing precision $p$. To assess the amount of increase, we evaluate social welfare under all four planners (myopic and optimal for both altruistic and biased) by numerically solving\footnote{We utilize JuliaPOMDP to numerically evaluate the optimal policies and trajectories of the MDPs \cite{egorov2017pomdps}. } the respective MDPs. We assume a linear $\beta(\cdot)$, $\beta(q)=k|q-p|$, and vary $k$, baseline precision $p$, and discount factor $\delta$, with the cost of an unfavorable action $C$ fixed at $1$. For each combination of these parameters, we simulate 1000 trajectories for a total of 125,000 trajectories per policy. Figure~\ref{fig:welfare_comp} depicts the average expenditure by the planner and social welfare, with values normalized by the expected utility when the precision is $p$ throughout. Even the myopic planners led to social welfare changes of $-11.49\%$ and $17.91\%$, respectively, for the biased and altruistic cases, while the optimal policies of each changed the social welfare more substantially ($-26.34\%$ and $96.97\%$, respectively).

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{images/bar_chart.pdf}
    \caption{Here, we depict planner expenditure (total cost of precision investment) and social welfare under the four policies. Values are shown as percentages of the expected welfare when the control policy is null.}
    \label{fig:welfare_comp}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[!ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{images/policy_comparison.pdf}
    \caption{Here, we depict examples of the optimal and myopic policies under altruistic and biased planners when $C=1, p=0.7, \delta=0.75, k = 0.5.$ }
    \label{fig:policy_comp}
\end{figure}

Recall that the myopic altruistic policy can be interpreted as a decentralized scheme where each agent is optimizing her own precision (see Section~\ref{sec:altruistic_results}). Thus, the difference in social welfare when implementing the myopic and optimal altruistic policies ($\sim80\%$) is the price of anarchy i.e., how much is lost when agents are not coordinated.

We elucidate the optimal policies considering an example depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:policy_comp}. Note that the altruistic planner invests significantly more in signal precision. The optimal policy of the biased planner decreases signal precision in large regimes (e.g., phases (C) and (E) of Theorem~\ref{biased_policy}) and, subsequently, adversely affects social welfare. 

The extent of influence that the planner can have on social welfare is even more surprising considering several noteworthy limitations on the planner's power in our model. We discuss these limitations below:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \emph{Equivalent Information:}
   The information set of the planner is identical to those of the agents (barring private signals). In particular, the planner has no additional knowledge about the true state of the world. Realistically, planners (e.g., government agencies, large corporations, etc.) often do have access to information unavailable to the general populace. 

    \item \emph{Symmetric and Informative Signals:}

     In our setting, for a precision $q$ chosen by the planner, an agent's signal realization matches the true state of the world with probability $q$, regardless of what the true state is. Thus our planners are allowed to obfuscate (by choosing low $q$), but not falsify, the state of the world to the agents. The planner is also prohibited from cherry-picking information as this would lead to signals with asymmetric match probability. 

    \item \emph{Observable Control:}

    All agents know the precision chosen by the planner at each time step. Thus, the planner can not influence agents surreptitiously -- their actions are fully transparent. This restriction is made even stronger because all agents are assumed to be Bayes rational. This guarantees that agents never lose expected utility because of their private signal; at worst, the private signal is uninformative (i.e., precision $0.5$). If given a signal $s$ has precision $q<0.5$, a rational agent simply negates the signal to achieve precision $1-q$.
\end{enumerate}

Given these restrictions, one might believe that such a limited planner would be ineffectual in steering social learning. However, we have shown that even so constricted, the planner can significantly impact social welfare, for better or worse.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{FUTURE DIRECTIONS}
\label{sec:conclusion}


We highlight two notable directions for future research: (1) generalization to agents with diverse preferences and (2) mitigation of the negative welfare effects of biased planners. 

The problem of the altruistic planner can be easily generalized to allow for agents with different preferences, i.e., agents whose utility is maximized by different actions for the same true state (see \cite{tech_report}). However, the biased planner's problem is inherently asymmetric and, thus, does not allow for the same generalization. The question of how a biased planner interacts with individuals of diverse preferences remains open. Would the planner concentrate efforts on agents whose preferences align with the planner's, or rather focus on swaying agents who may be predisposed otherwise? 

Another important area for future exploration is how one might prevent the decrease in welfare caused by biased planners. Here, one might explore regulations or mechanisms that seek to align the incentives of planners and agents to avoid detrimental impacts on welfare. 

Both directions are motivated by our current work and carry significant social importance.



