\label{sec:heterogeneous_agents}
We sketch here the argument that the altruistic planner’s optimal control problem remains essentially identical if there are two types of agents: one that seeks to match their action to the true state of the world and another that seeks to mismatch their action and the true state. One interpretation of the utility structure is that each agent is rewarded for making a choice that is right (or good) for her and penalized for a poor choice. Further, assume that all agents know their own type and the types of all other agents.

First, note that the private belief update remains exactly as (\ref{private_update}) because it is only dependent upon the signal structure. If agent $i$ gets a higher reward when her action matches $\omega$ she acts exactly as in (\ref{actions}). If, instead, she gets a higher reward when her action does not match $\omega$, then her actions are the opposite of those in (\ref{actions}). Because we assume the type of each agent is known to all, when $q_i\geq\max(b_i,1-b_i)$, all agents can perfectly infer the private signal of agent $i$. If $q_i<\max(b,1-b_i)$, the action of agent $i$ is uninformative. Thus, after observing agent $i$'s type, precision $q_i$, and action $a_i$, the public belief is updated exactly as (\ref{public_update}). 

If agent $i$ gets a higher reward when her action matches $\omega$, then an altruistic planner seeks to induce her to take action $\omega$. Otherwise, the altruistic planner seeks to induce her to act opposite to $\omega$. Thus, for the first type of agents, the instantaneous reward function of the planner is exactly as (\ref{alt_reward}). For the second type of agent, the planner's reward is obtained by replacing the second term in (\ref{alt_reward}) by $C\P(a_i=\omega)$. But, since the actions of the two types of agents are negations of each other, $P(a_i\neq\omega)$ for one type has the same expression as $P(a_i=\omega)$ of the other (in terms of $q_i, b_i$). Thus, the instantaneous reward function becomes equal to (\ref{alt_reward}) for both types of agents. Thus, the planner seeks to solve the same optimization problem when there are two types of agents as that described for the previous altruistic planner (Section~\ref{sec:altruistic_results}). Refer to \cite{tech_report} for further detail. 

While this argument can be made for the altruistic planner, the biased planner's problem is inherently asymmetric and, thus, does not allow for the same generalization. The study of the interplay between a biased planner and individuals with diverse preferences is left for future work.

