\subsection{Proof of Theorem~\ref{myopic_biased_policy}}

We will let $z(b,q)=b+q-2bq$ be the probability of an agent receiving signal $B$ with precision $q$ and prior public belief $b$. Thus, applying the action rule from (\ref{actions}), the instantaneous reward can be written as follows:
\begin{align}
\label{biased_reward_expanded}
    r_B(b,q)=\begin{cases}
        -\beta(|q-p|)-Cz(b,q)&q\geq\max(b,1-b)\\
        -\beta(|q-p|)-C&b<1-q\\
        -\beta(|q-p|)&b>q
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
First, note that at $b=0.5$, $z(b,q)=z(0.5,q)=0.5$ regardless of the precision chosen. Thus, at $b=0.5$, the optimal policy will be to choose precision $p$ at cost $0$. 

We can write the derivative of the reward with respect to the chosen precision as follows:
\begin{align}
\label{biased_reward_derivative}
    \frac{\partial r_B(b,q)}{\partial q}=\begin{cases}
        -\sign(q-p)\dot\beta(|q-p|)\\
        \ \ -C(1-2b))&q\geq\max(b,1-b)\\
        -\sign(q-p)\dot\beta(|q-p|)&\text{o.w.}
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
In the latter case of (\ref{biased_reward_derivative}), the reward is maximized when $|q-p|$ is minimized. 

In the former case, we can write the second derivative of the reward with respect to $q$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\partial^2}{\partial q^2}r_B(b,q)=-\ddot\beta(|q-p|)
\end{align*}

Because $\beta(\cdot)$ is concave, this expression is non-negative, and $r_B(b,q)$ is convex with respect to $q$. Thus, the reward is maximized at one of the extreme points $\{\max(b,1-b),1\}$ or $p$ because $r_B(b,q)$ is not differentiable at $q=p$.
Furthermore, $z(b,q)$ increases with respect to $q$ when $b<0.5$ and decreases with respect to $q$ when $b>0.5$.

For the remainder of the proof, we will consider the myopic biased policy in 4 cases: $b\in[0,1-p)$, $b\in[1-p,0.5)$, $b\in(0.5,p]$, and $b\in(p,1]$, making use of the fact that $p\geq0.5$. Based on our reasoning above, these cases have the following candidate optimal policies: $\{p,1-b\}$, $\{1-b,p\}$, $\{1,p,b-\epsilon\}$, and $\{p,1\}$, respectively, where $\epsilon>0$ is an arbitrarily small and positive real number.

\emph{\underline{Case 1}: }$b\in[0,1-p)$, $\pi^0_B(b)\in\{p,1-b\}$

We can express the reward under the two candidate policies as follows by applying (\ref{biased_reward_expanded}):
\begin{align*}
    r_B(b,p)&=-C\\
    r_B(b,1-b)&=-\beta(1-b-p)-C(1-2b(1-b))
\end{align*}
The derivative of $r_B(b,1-b)$ with respect to $b$ is
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial b}r_B(b,1-b)&=\dot\beta(1-p-b)+2C(1-2b)
\end{align*}
which is positive as $\beta(\cdot)$ is increasing and $b<1-p\leq0.5$.
Furthermore, when evaluated at $b=0$ and $b=1-p$ we obtain
\begin{align*}
    r_B(0,1)&=-\beta(1-p)-C<r_B(b,p)\\
    r_B(1-p,p)&=-C(1-2p(1-p))>r_B(b,p)
\end{align*}

Thus, $r_B(b,1-b)$ is increasing on $[0,1-p)$ with $r_B(b,1-b)<r_B(b,p)$ for $b=0$ and vice versa for $b=1-p$. Because $\beta(\cdot)$ is continuous, so are both reward functions. Thus, there exists $t_1\in(0,1-p)$ such that $\pi^0_B(b)=p$ for $b\in[0,t_1]$ and $\pi^0_B(b)=1-b$ for $b\in(t_1,1-p)$.

\emph{\underline{Case 2}: }$b\in[1-p,0.5)$, $\pi^0_B(b)\in\{1-b,p,1-b-\epsilon\}$

We can express the reward under the candidate policies as follows by applying (\ref{biased_reward_expanded}):
\begin{align*}
    r_B(b,1-b)&=-\beta(p-1+b)-Cz(b,1-b)\\
    r_B(b,p)&=-Cz(b,p)\\
    r_B(b,1-b-\epsilon)&=-\beta(p-1+b+\epsilon)-C
\end{align*}

Because $\beta(\cdot)$ is increasing and $z(b,1-b)<1\ \forall b\in[0,1]$, we can immediately rule out choosing precision $1-b-\epsilon$ as it is dominated by choosing precision $1-b$. 

At $b=0.5$, $r_B(b,p)>r_B(b,1-b)$, thus there exists a $t_3\in[1-p,0.5)$ such that $\pi^0_B(b)=p$ for $b\in[t_3,0.5)$.

At $b=1-p$, $r_B(b,1-b)=r_B(b,p)$. We now consider the partial derivatives of these two expressions above:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial b}r_B(b,1-b)&=-\dot\beta(p-1+b)+2C(1-2b)\\
    \frac{\partial}{\partial b}r_B(b,p)&=C(2p-1)
\end{align*}
Evaluating these derivatives at $b=1-p$ yields:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial b}r_B(b,1-b)\bigg|_{b=1-p}&=-\dot\beta(0)+2C(p-1)\\&=-\dot\beta(0)+2Cp-2C\\
    \frac{\partial}{\partial b}r_B(b,p)\bigg|_{b=1-p}&=C(2p-1)=2Cp-C
\end{align*}
Thus, at $b=1-p$, $r_B(b,p)$ is increasing more quickly than $r_B(b,1-b)$. Because they take the same value at $1-p$, this implies the existence of an interval $[1-p,t_2]$ such that $\pi^0_B(b)=p$.

\emph{\underline{Case 3}: }$b\in(0.5,p]$, $\pi^0_B(b)\in\{1,p,b-\epsilon\}$

We can express the reward under the candidate policies as follows by applying (\ref{biased_reward_expanded}):
\begin{align*}
    r_B(b,1)&=-\beta(1-p)-C(1-b)\\
    r_B(b,p)&=-Cz(b,p)\\
    r_B(b,b-\epsilon)&=-\beta(p-b+\epsilon)
\end{align*}

First, note that the first two expressions above are linear functions in $b$ while the third is convex in $b$. 

When evaluated at $b=p$, $r_B(b,b-\epsilon)$ is infinitesimal and therefore $b-\epsilon$ is the optimal precision on some interval $(t_3,p]$ for $0.5\leq t_3<p$. Furthermore, because $r_B(b,b-\epsilon)$ is convex, its level sets must be convex. Therefore, it cannot be optimal on any other subset of $(0.5,p]$. 

What remains is to compare precisions $1$ and $p$ on the interval $(0.5,t_4]$

\emph{\underline{Case 4}: }$b\in(p,1]$, $\pi^0_B(b)\in\{p,1\}$

In this case, even without investment, the expected reward of the planner is $0$. Since the reward is non-negative, this is optimal. Thus, for $b>p$, $\pi^0_B(b)=p$.

Combining the results from each of the cases completes the proof.
\hfill\QED

\subsection{Proof of Lemma~\ref{biased_mono}}

We begin by showing that, for fixed signal precision $q$, the biased planner's instantaneous reward is increasing in public belief. Applying (\ref{actions}), we can rewrite the instantaneous reward as follows:
\begin{align}
    r_B(b,q)=\begin{cases}
        -\beta(|q-p|)-Cz(b,q)&q\geq\max(b,1-b)\\
        -\beta(|q-p|)-C\mathbf{1}(b<0.5)&\text{o.w.}
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
where $z(b,q)=b+q-2bq$ is the probability of receiving signal $s_i=B$ with precision $q$ and public belief $b$, and $\mathbf{1}$ is the indicator function.
It immediately follows that the reward is increasing in $b$ as shown below, noting that $q\geq0.5$.
\begin{align}
    \frac{\partial}{\partial b}r_B(b,q)=-C(1-2q)\geq0.
\end{align}

Next, note that the Bayesian nature of belief updates implies that the belief process is a martingale. That is, for any fixed $q_i$, $\E[b_{i+1}]=b_i$. To demonstrate this consider two cases: (1) $q_i<\max(b_i,1-b_i)$, and (2) $q_i\geq\max(b_i,1-b_i)$. 

In the former case, it follows from (\ref{public_update}) that $b_{i+1}=b_i$, satisfying the claim. 

In the latter case, (\ref{private_update}) gives the distribution of $b_{i+1}$ which yields the same. 

The monotonicity of $V^*_B(\cdot)$ then follows from Proposition 5 of \cite{smithStructuralPropertiesStochastic2002}.
\hfill\QED





\subsection{Proof of Theorem~\ref{welfare_mono}}
\begin{proof}

    It is clear that the one-step welfare $\max(b_i,1-b_i,\pi(b_i))$ is increasing in $\pi(b_i)$. The fact that future welfare is also increasing follows from the convexity of $\max(b_i,1-b_i,\pi(b_i))$ with respect to $b_i$ and the nature of the belief update in (\ref{private_update}) and (\ref{public_update}). This follows the same argument as our convexity argument in Theorem~\ref{alt_convexity}.
\end{proof}

