\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:intro-problem}

We consider the problem of generating an arbitrary
sequence $(X_1, X_2, \dots)$ of discrete random variables,
using an input sequence of
i.i.d.~unbiased coin tosses
$\bm{\omega} \defeq (\omega_i)_{i=1}^{\infty} \in \set{0,1}^\infty$
from an entropy source.
%
Efficiency in this context is measured in terms of the space, runtime, and
entropy complexity properties of the algorithm.
%
No particular assumptions are made on the joint distribution of the output
sequence, which can be subject to any stochastic process such as i.i.d.,
exchangeable, stationary, Markov, etc.
%
In particular, the distribution of the output sequence is specified in
terms of the conditional distributions
$P_i(x_i \mid X_1=x_1, \dots, X_{i-1}=x_{i-1})$ of each $X_i$.
%
This distribution is assumed to be available at every step $i \ge 1$ as either an array
of probabilities or as a cumulative distribution function,
and its probabilities are rational numbers with denominator at most $d \ge 1$.

For any sampling algorithm $S$ and integer $k$, let $\tosses{S}{k}$ denote the \textit{entropy cost},
which is the (random) number of input coin tosses consumed from the
entropy source to produce the output $(X_1,\dots,X_k)$.
%
As a random variable, the entropy cost is itself a deterministic function
$\tosses{S}{k}(\bm{\omega})$ of the coin tosses themselves,
where $\bm{\omega} \sim \Uniform(\set{0,1}^\infty)$.
%
By the source coding theorem of \citet{shannon1948}, a lower bound
on the expected entropy cost $\expect{T_S}$
of any algorithm is given by the \citeauthor{shannon1948} entropy:
\begin{equation}
H(X_1,\dots,X_k) \defeq -\sum_{x_1}\dots\sum_{x_k} \log\left(P(X_1=x_1,\dots,X_k=x_k)\right) P(X_1=x_1,\dots,X_k=x_k).
\end{equation}
The least possible expected \textit{amortized} entropy cost
$\expect{\tosses{S}{k}/k}$ is therefore $H(X_1,\dots,X_k)/k$ bits per sample.
%
The corresponding expected \textit{entropy loss} of any sampler is given by
\begin{equation}
\expect{\tosses{S}{k} - H(X_1, \dots, X_k)}.
\end{equation}
%
We are interested in space- and time-efficient algorithms that minimize this
expected entropy loss.

\subsection{Existing Approaches}
\label{sec:intro-existing}

To motivate our main result, we first describe how existing
algorithms for efficiently sampling a \textit{single} random variate $X \sim P$
can be adapted to the setting of sampling a sequence of random
variates, as well as the advantages and limitations of these approaches.

\paragraph{Entropy-Optimal Sampling}

For generating a single discrete random variable $X \sim P$,
\citet{knuth1976} describe an ``entropy-optimal'' algorithm $S$ whose
entropy cost $\expect{T_S} \in [H(X), H(X)+2)$ is the best possible~\citep[Theorem 2.2]{knuth1976}.
%
This result shows that the \citeauthor{shannon1948} rate of $H(X)$ bits per
sample is in general not achievable when drawing a single sample.

In an ``offline'' setting, where $k$ is a fixed and prespecified value,
we could use the entropy-optimal \citeauthor{knuth1976} method
to sample the joint probability distribution $P_{1:k}$
of $(X_1,\dots,X_k)$, i.e.,
\begin{align}
(X_1, \dots, X_k) \overset{\ky}{\sim} P_{1:k}(x_1,\dots,x_k) \defeq
  \prod_{i=1}^{k}P_1(X_i=x_i\mid \cap_{j=1}^{i-1}\set{X_j=x_j}),
\label{eq:baseline-ky-joint-dist-gen}
\end{align}
where $X \overset{\ky}{\sim} P$ means that ``$X$ is generated from $P$
using the \citeauthor{knuth1976} method''.
%
Since $P_{1:k}$ is a bona fide discrete distribution
over $[d]^k$, the expected entropy cost is
\begin{align}
\expect{\tosses{S}{k}} < H(X_1,\dots,X_k) + 2.
\label{eq:baseline-ky-joint-dist-entropy}
\end{align}
This method achieves the best possible rate that tends to the optimal
expected amortized cost of $H(X_1,\dots,X_k)/k$ bits per sample.
%
Its disadvantage is that the joint distribution $P_{1:k}$ requires up to $d^k$
space and its probabilities can have a large denominator, up to $d^k$.
%
Moreover, the method requires $k$ to be prespecified, making it unsuitable
for online random sampling.

\paragraph{Sequential Entropy-Optimal Sampling}

It is possible to generate $(X_i)_{i\ge1}$
by using sequence of entropy-optimal \citeauthor{knuth1976}
samplers that target each individual conditional distribution, i.e.,
\begin{align}
X_1 &\overset{\ky}{\sim} P_1,
&& X_2 \overset{\ky}{\sim} P_2(x_2 \mid X_1),
&&\dots
&&X_k \overset{\ky}{\sim} P_k(x_k \mid X_1, \dots, X_{k-1}),
\label{eq:baseline-ky-chain-rule-gen}
\end{align}
The expected entropy cost of generating
$(X_1, \dots, X_k)$ using this sequential
sampler $S$ is therefore
\begin{equation}
\expect{\tosses{S}{k}}
< \sum_{i=1}^{k}\left[H(X_i \mid X_1,\dots,X_{i-1})+2\right]
= H(X_1,\dots,X_k) + 2k.
\label{eq:baseline-ky-chain-rule-entropy}
\end{equation}
%
The space complexity of sequential sampling remains
bounded as $k$ grows large, since at each step the conditional
distribution $P_i(x_i \mid X_1=x_1, \dots, X_{i-1}=x_{i-1})$
is finitely encodable using at most $d\log{d}$ bits.
%
The space complexity of generating $X_i \overset{\ky}{\sim} P$
at each step may be exponential or linear, depending on the amount of
preprocessing performed prior to sampling.
%
The usual approach of explicitly constructing a \citeauthor{knuth1976} DDG tree
sampler for $P$ during preprocessing
(e.g., \citet[Algorithm~1]{roy2013}; \citet[Algorithms 5 and 6]{saad2020popl})
can require exponential space in the number of bits needed
to encode $P$ \citep[Theorem 3.6]{saad2020popl}.
%
Alternatively, by using the cumulative distribution function of $P$,
\citet[Algorithm 1]{saad2025} give a linear space implementation
of entropy-optimal sampling that avoids preprocessing a DDG tree.
%
However, this method incurs a runtime overhead of computing the binary
expansions of the target probabilities during the sampling phase.
%
In either case, the space complexity of \cref{eq:baseline-ky-chain-rule-gen}
remains bounded as $k$ grows.

The expected amortized entropy cost bound of $H(X_1,\dots,X_k)/k + 2$
bits per sample does not tend to the optimal rate, however.
%
The term $2k$ in \cref{eq:baseline-ky-chain-rule-entropy} indicates a
significant waste of random bits.
%
\Citet[\S2.3]{devroye2020} describe a ``randomness extraction''
procedure that recycles the random bits in this sequential approach,
which achieves an asymptotically optimal rate
\begin{equation}
\expect{\tosses{S}{k}} < H(X_1,\dots,X_k) + 2 + o(k).
\end{equation}
However, the space complexity is no longer bounded as the algorithm
requires arbitrary-precision arithmetic.
%
Randomness extraction uses operations with big integers of unbounded size
as $k \to \infty$ \textit{even if} the rational probabilities have bounded
denominator $d$, compromising performance.

\input{fig-compare}

\paragraph{Interval Method (Arithmetic Coding)}

The interval algorithm of \citet{han1997} generates samples from an
arbitrary discrete probability distribution by recursively subdividing the
unit interval, exactly as in arithmetic coding \citep[\S6.3]{mackay2003}.
%
It naturally extends to sampling i.i.d.~random variables,
a stationary homogeneous Markov process, or an arbitrary process \citep[\S{V}]{han1997}
%
In particular, when the entropy source is a fair coin,
\citet[Theorem 3]{han1997}
show that generating $(X_1, \dots, X_k)$ using the interval sampler has
\begin{align}
\expect{\tosses{S}{k}} < H(X_1,\dots,X_k) + 3.
\label{eq:baseline-interval-entropy}
\end{align}
%
The interval method does not require the full joint distribution to be
known in advance, making it suitable for online sampling.
%
Its expected amortized entropy cost bound
$H(X_1,\dots,X_k)/k + 3/k$ also converges to the asymptotically
optimal Shannon rate \citep[Eq.~(5.2)]{han1997}.
%
The disadvantage is that it requires arbitrary-precision arithmetic,
as with the method of \citet{devroye2020}.
%
\Citet{uyematsu2003} give a finite memory implementation of the
interval method that uses the usual arithmetic coding technique of rounding
cumulative probabilities, but this approximate
version no longer produces exact samples.

\subsection{Main Result}
\label{sec:intro-result}

The main contribution of this article is an efficient algorithm
for generating a sequence $(X_i)_{i\ge1}$ of discrete random
variables whose expected amortized entropy consumption, can be
made arbitrarily close to the Shannon rate of $H(X_1,\dots,X_k)/k$ bits per
sample as $k \to \infty$.
%
\Cref{table:compare} shows how the complexity properties of our method
(labeled Randomness Recycling) compare to those of existing algorithms.
%
In particular, the space complexity of our method does not grow arbitrarily
large with $k$.
%
The precise result on the entropy and space complexity is as follows.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{EntropyCost}
\label{theorem:entropy-cost}
Let $(X_i)_{i \ge 1} \defeq (X_1, X_2, X_3, \dots)$
be a sequence of discrete random variables.
%
Assume that all the conditional probabilities
$P_i(x_i \mid X_1=x_1, \dots, X_{i-1}=x_{i-1})$
are rational numbers with denominator at most $d \ge 1$.
%
For any $\varepsilon > 0$,
there exists a random sampling algorithm $S$ that generates $(X_i)_{i \ge 1}$
whose expected entropy cost for generating $(X_1,\dots,X_k)$ satisfies
\begin{align}
\expect{\tosses{S}{k}} < H(X_1,\dots,X_k) + \varepsilon{k} + W(d,\varepsilon) && (k\ge1),
\label{eq:main-thm-cost}
\end{align}
where $W(d,\varepsilon) \sim \log(d/\varepsilon)$ as $d/\varepsilon \to \infty$.
%
In addition, the algorithm maintains a state of at most $2W$ bits
and uses auxiliary space of $O(W)$ bits.
\end{restatable}

In the special case of sampling an i.i.d.~sequence with common distribution
$X$, \cref{theorem:entropy-cost} gives an amortized entropy cost that is
asymptotically less than $H(X)+\varepsilon$ bits per sample.

\paragraph{Approach}

The entropy bound in \cref{theorem:entropy-cost} is enabled
by \textit{randomness recycling}.
%
The key idea is as follows.
%
When sampling $X_i \sim P_{i}(x_i\mid X_1=x_1,\dots,X_{i-1}=x_{i-1})$
from its conditional distribution at step $i$,
the sampler has access to the entropy source $\bm{\omega}$
as well as a \textit{uniform state}
$(\randS_{i-1},\randM_{i-1})$, which is a pair of random variables such that
$\randS_{i-1} \sim \Uniform[0,\randM_{i-1})$.
%
This uniform state is comprised of both random bits drawn freshly from the
entropy source as well as random bits that have been \textit{recycled} from
generating the previous random variables $X_1, X_2, \dots, X_{i-1}$.
%
After $X_i$ is generated, the global uniform state $(\randS_{i-1},\randM_{i-1})$
is updated to a new
value $(\randS_i,\randM_i)$ such that $\randS_i \sim \Uniform[0,\randM_i)$
which will be used to sample $X_{i+1}$ in the subsequent step.

The correctness of randomness recycling requires the following
invariant to be maintained $(i\ge0)$:
\begin{enumerate}[wide=15pt, leftmargin=*, label=(I\arabic*),]
\item \label{invariant} the global uniform state
  $(\randS_i,\randM_i)$ satisfies
  $\randS_i \perp (X_1, \dots, X_i) \mid \randM_i$.
\end{enumerate}
%
% The notation $X \perp Y \mid Z$ means that $X$ is conditionally
% independent of $Y$ given $Z$.
%
Maintaining this global uniform state makes it possible to substantially
reduce the number fresh coin tosses drawn from the entropy source,
by storing unused information from previous coin tosses whenever possible.
%
By induction, the invariant \labelcref{invariant} is maintained as long as
$(\randS_0, \randM_0) = (0,1)$ and the sampling function used at each step
$i$ fulfills the following contract:
\begin{enumerate}[wide=15pt, leftmargin=*, label=(C\arabic*),]
\item \label{contract} if the initial global uniform state $(\randS_{i-1},\randM_{i-1})$ satisfies
  $\randS_{i-1} \perp (X_1, \dots, X_{i-1}) \mid \randM_{i-1}$, then
  the updated global uniform state $(\randS_i,\randM_i)$ satisfies
  $\randS_i \perp (X_1, \dots, X_{i}) \mid \randM_i$.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Organization}

\Cref{sec:random-states} introduces uniform and
nonuniform random states; and describes how they can be manipulated to
create new random states through information-preserving transformations.
%
\Cref{sec:rr} shows how many existing algorithms for sampling discrete
random variables can be efficiently modified to perform randomness
recycling, while propagating a global uniform random state that maintains
the invariant \labelcref{invariant}.
%
\Cref{sec:analysis} uses the ideas developed in
\cref{sec:random-states,sec:rr} to analyze complexity of the algorithms and
prove \cref{theorem:entropy-cost}.
%
\Cref{sec:related} discusses related work.

\section{Random States}
\label{sec:random-states}

\input{fig-overview}

\begin{definition}
A \textit{uniform random state} $(\randS,\randM)$ is any pair of discrete
random variables such that $\randS \sim \Uniform[0,\randM)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Let $\mathbf{w} \defeq (w_0,\dots,w_{n-1})$ be a list of $n \ge 1$
positive integers with sum $m$.
%
A \textit{nonuniform random state} $(\randS,\mathbf{w})$
is a random variable $\randS \sim \Discrete(\mathbf{w})$,
i.e., $\Pr(X=i) = w_i/m$ for $i \in [0,n)$.
\end{definition}

Randomness recycling leverages several properties about merging and
splitting both uniform and nonuniform random states, which we describe in
\cref{sec:random-states-merge,sec:random-states-split}.
%
These two operations are information-preserving transformations that are
inverses of one another.

\subsection{Merging Random States}
\label{sec:random-states-merge}

\subsubsection{Merging Two Uniform States into a Uniform State}
\label{sec:random-states-merge-unif}

We first describe how to \textit{merge} two uniform states $(\randS,\randM)$ and
$(\randS',\randM')$ to obtain a new uniform state
over a larger range $\randM \randM'$.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:merge-uniform}
For any integers $m, m' \ge 1$,
\begin{align}
\randS \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,m), \quad
\randS' \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,m'), \quad
\randS \perp \randS'
\implies
\randS + \randS'm \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,mm').
\label{eq:merge-uniform}
\end{align}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The map
$[0,m) \times [0,m') \ni (x,x') \overset{f}{\mapsto} x+x'm \in \set{0,1,\dots,mm'-1}$
is injective,
because if $x + x'm = y + y'm$ then $x-y = m(y'-x')$.
%
But $(x-y) \in (-m,m)$ is an integer multiple of $m$ if and only if
$x-y=0$, which implies that $y'-x'=0$.
%
The assumption $\randS \perp \randS'$ implies that $(\randS,\randS')$ is uniform over
the grid $[0,m) \times [0,m')$.
%
Therefore $\Pr(\randS+\randS'm = i) = \Pr((\randS,\randS') = f^{-1}(i)) = 1/(m m')$
whenever $0 \le i < mm'$, since $f$ is 1-1.
\end{proof}

Our randomness recycling algorithms will propagate a uniform state $(\randS,\randM)$
in a way that maintains the invariant~\labelcref{invariant}.
%
To ensure that the space complexity remains bounded,
we specify an upper bound $W \ge 1$ on the size of the uniform state
so that $\Pr(\randM \in [1, 2^W)) = 1$ and in turn $\Pr(\randS \in [0, 2^W-1)) = 1$.
%
The values $(\randS, \randM)$ are stored as $W$-bit words in memory.

The \Call{Recycle}{} procedure shown in \cref{alg:recycle}
uses \cref{prop:merge-uniform}
to recycle a new uniform state $(\randS',\randM')$
into an existing global uniform state $(\randS,\randM)$.
%
The notation $\randS \setp \dots$ and $\randM \setp \dots$
on \cref{algline:refill-update-Z,algline:refill-update-m}
means that the global variables $(\randS,\randM)$
are being updated in place.
%
The only valid deterministic initialization of the global uniform state
is given by $(\randS_0,\randM_0) = (0,1)$.

\input{alg-recycle}
\input{alg-refill}

It will also be necessary
to recycle a fresh uniform state $(\randS',\randM')$ that is drawn
directly from an entropy source
into $(\randS,\randM)$, as shown in \cref{alg:refill}.
%
The interface to the entropy source is denoted $\Call{Flip}{k}$,
which returns $k$ i.i.d.~fair coin flips.
%
The number $k$ of required flips computed on \cref{algline:refill-fresh-k}
ensures that the updated uniform state $(\randS,\randM)$ does not
overflow beyond $W$-bit words.

\subsubsection{Merging a Nonuniform and Uniform State into a Uniform State}
\label{sec:random-states-merge-nonunif}

We next describe a useful generalization of \cref{prop:merge-uniform}.
%
% It could be useful if you are given discrete uniforms with known
% distribution and you want to turn htem into random bits.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:merge-nonuniform}
For any positive integers $\mathbf{w} \defeq w_0, \dots, w_{n-1}$
with sum $m$,
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{0pt}
\begin{align}
\randS \sim \mathrm{Discrete}(\mathbf{w}), \quad
\randS' \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0, w_\randS)
\implies \left(\randS' + \sum_{i=0}^{\randS-1}w_i\right) \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,m).
\end{align}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
We first prove the map $f$ defined by
$\cup_{i=0}^{n-1}(\set{i}\times[0,w_i)) \ni (x,x') \overset{f}{\mapsto} x' + \sum_{i=0}^{x-1}w_i \in [0,m)$
is a bijection.
%
Since the domain and codomain of $f$ each have $m$ elements, it is sufficient
to show that $f$ is injective.
%
For a fixed value of $x \in [0,n)$
the map $g_x$ defined by
$[0,w_x) \ni x' \overset{g_x}{\mapsto} f(x,x') \in \left[\sum_{i=0}^{x-1}w_i, \sum_{i=0}^{x}w_i\right)$
is injective.
%
Since the images $g_0([0,w_0)), g_1([0,w_1)), \dots, g_{n-1}([0,w_{n-1}))$ are disjoint,
it follows that $f$ is itself injective.
%
Now, for any $j \in [0,m)$, define $(x_j, x'_j) \defeq f^{-1}(j)$, so that
\begin{align}
\Pr\left(X' + \sum_{i=0}^{X-1}w_i = j\right)
= \Pr((X,X') = f^{-1}(j))
= \Pr(X=x_j, X'=x'_j)
= \frac{w_{x_j}}{m}\frac{1}{w_{x_j}}
= 1/m.
\end{align}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
\Cref{prop:merge-uniform} is a special case of \cref{prop:merge-nonuniform}
with $\mathbf{w} = (m', m', \dots, m')$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Splitting Random States}
\label{sec:random-states-split}

\subsubsection{Splitting a Uniform State into Two Uniform States}
\label{sec:randoms-tates-split-unif}

The inverse of \cref{prop:merge-uniform} is as follows.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:split-uniform}
For any integers $m, m' \ge 1$,
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{0pt}
\begin{align}
\randS \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,mm')
\implies
\left\lbrace\begin{aligned}
\floor{\randS / m} &\sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,m') \\
(\randS \bmod m) &\sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,m) \\
\floor{\randS / m} &\perp (\randS \bmod m).
\end{aligned}\right.
\label{eq:split-uniform}
\end{align}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the arguments in \cref{sec:rr-uniform}.
\end{proof}

%
\Cref{prop:split-uniform} shows how a uniform random state can be split
into two smaller independent uniform random states by using a single
integer division.
%
It shows that the merging operation in \cref{prop:merge-uniform}
preserves information and is perfectly reversible.

\subsubsection{Splitting a Uniform State into a Nonuniform and Uniform State}
\label{sec:random-states-split-nonunif}

The inverse of \cref{prop:merge-nonuniform} is as follows.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:split-nonuniform}
For any positive integers $\mathbf{w} \defeq w_0, \dots, w_{n-1}$ with sum $m$,
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{0pt}
\begin{align}
\randS \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,m), \quad
\randS \equiv \sum_{i=0}^{Y-1}w_i + X\ (\mbox{where } X \in [0,w_Y))
\implies
\left\lbrace\begin{aligned}
Y  &\sim \mathrm{Discrete}(\mathbf{w}) \\
X &\sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0, w_Y).
\end{aligned}\right.
\end{align}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $W_i \defeq w_0 + \dots + w_{i}$ denote the prefix sums of $\mathbf{w}$
for $i \in [0,n)$.
%
Since $Z \in [W_{Y-1}, W_Y)$ with probability one,
the event $\set{Y=i}$ holds if and only if $Z \in [W_{i-1}, W_i)$
which has probability $(W_i-W_{i-1})/m = w_i/m$ $(i\in[0,n); W_{-1}\defeq0)$.
%
Since $X = Z - W_{Y-1}$, conditioning on the event $\set{Y=i}$ gives
$Z \mid \set{Y=i} \in [W_{i-1}, W_i)$ and $X \mid \set{Y=i} \in [0, W_{i}-W_{i-1} \equiv w_i)$
which are both uniformly distributed by the uniformity of $Z$.
\end{proof}

\Cref{prop:split-nonuniform} says that a uniform random state can be split
into \begin{enumerate*}[label=(\roman*)]
\item a nonuniform random state following \textit{any} given finite discrete rational distribution; and
\item a leftover uniform random state;
\end{enumerate*}
as long as the original uniform range
matches the common denominator of the target discrete distribution.
%
It shows that the merging operation in \cref{prop:merge-nonuniform}
preserves information and is perfectly reversible.
%
\Cref{prop:split-nonuniform} forms the basis of the inversion sampling
algorithms in \cref{sec:rr-inversion}.

\subsubsection{Generating a Uniform Random State of a Given Size}
\label{sec:random-states-split-bernoulli}

Suppose the global uniform random state is $(\randS, \randM)$
and our goal is to generate
$X \sim \Uniform[0,n)$ over a smaller range $n<\randM$.
%
Applying \cref{prop:split-nonuniform} with the weights
$\mathbf{w} = (\randM-n, n)$ furnishes
a Bernoulli sample $Y \sim \Discrete(\randM-n,n)$
and a uniform sample $X \sim \Uniform[0,w_Y)$.
%
A simple rejection-sampling approach is therefore as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(R\arabic*),wide=15pt,leftmargin=*]

\item\label{step:unif-rej-r-1} If $Y=1$, then $X \sim \Uniform[0,n)$ is
  accepted and returned as the desired sample.

\item\label{step:unif-rej-r-2} If $Y=0$, then $X \sim \Uniform[0,\randM-n)$, which is not
  over the desired range, is rejected and recycled into the global uniform
  state $(\randS, \randM)$ using \cref{alg:recycle}.
\end{enumerate}

In both the accept and reject cases, we must discard the Bernoulli outcome
$Y$ and waste the corresponding $H_{\rm b}(n/\randM)$ bits of information,
because it may in general be correlated with the output sample $X$
(violating the contract \labelcref{contract}).
%
More importantly, we cannot recycle $Y$ back into the
global uniform state using \cref{prop:merge-nonuniform}
unless we recycle it in both the accept and reject cases.
%
The accept case requires consuming the desired $X \sim \Uniform[0, n)$,
which effectively undoes the sampling operation.
%
Discarding a Bernoulli outcome in this way is the only operation that we
consider which loses information and is fundamentally not reversible.

If $\randM \gtrapprox n$, the rejection-based method
\labelcrefrange{step:unif-rej-r-1}{step:unif-rej-r-2}
is a reasonably time- and entropy-efficient
sampler for $X \sim \mathrm{Uniform}[0,n)$
(cf.~\cref{alg:uniform-near-pot}).
%
On the other hand, if $\randM \gg n$ then the rejection probability
$(\randM-n)/\randM$ is very high.
%
We can instead use \cref{prop:split-uniform} to split the uniform state
into two smaller uniform states.
%
The resulting sampler is as follows:
%
\begin{enumerate}[label=(S\arabic*),wide=15pt,leftmargin=*]
\item\label{step:unif-rej-s-1}
  Generate
  $\hat{X} \sim \Uniform[0,\floor{\randM/n} n)$ using
  \labelcrefrange{step:unif-rej-r-1}{step:unif-rej-r-2}.

\item\label{step:unif-rej-s-2}
  Apply \cref{prop:split-uniform}
  (with $Z = \hat{X}$, $m=n$, $m'=\floor{\randM/n}$)
  to obtain $X \defeq (\hat{X} \bmod n) \sim \Uniform[0,n)$
  as the desired sample.
  %
  Recycle the leftover
  uniform state $\floor{\hat{X}/n} \sim \Uniform[0,\floor{\randM/n})$
  into the global uniform state using \cref{alg:recycle}.
\end{enumerate}
%
The correctness of \labelcref{step:unif-rej-s-1} follows from the fact
$\floor{\randM/n} n \le \randM$, so
\labelcrefrange{step:unif-rej-r-1}{step:unif-rej-r-2}
can be applied.
%
\Cref{sec:rr-uniform} fuses
\labelcrefrange{step:unif-rej-s-1}{step:unif-rej-s-2} together
to arrive at a simple and efficient algorithm for uniform sampling.

\section{Randomness Recycling for Discrete Distributions}
\label{sec:rr}

We now describe how randomness recycling can be used to improve
the efficiency of several algorithms for sampling discrete random
variables $X$.
%
\Cref{fig:overview} gives an overview of the ``randomness recycling''
workflow for generating a random sequence $(X_1, X_2, \dots)$.
%
The randomness recycler maintains at all times a global uniform state
$(\randS,\randM)$, each a $W$-bit integer.
%
Calls to \Call{Recycle}{}
(\cref{alg:recycle}) and \Call{Refill}{}
(\cref{alg:refill}) are used to manage the global uniform state.
%
Uniform sampling as described in \cref{sec:random-states-split-bernoulli}
serves as the basic source of randomness used by downstream sampling
algorithms.

While the sampling algorithms in this section are quite far from
entropy optimal when used to generate a single sample
(in the sense of \citet{knuth1976}), by using randomness
recycling we can make them all achieve nearly optimal amortized entropy
(in the sense of \cref{theorem:entropy-cost})
for generating random sequences.

\subsection{Uniform Sampling}
\label{sec:rr-uniform}

We first make the ideas in \cref{sec:random-states-split-bernoulli}
more precise by giving a concrete algorithm.
%
Consider the problem of generating a discrete uniform
$U \sim \Uniform[0,n)$ for some $n \ge 1$.
%
Given a uniform state $(\randS,\randM)$ with $\randM \geq n$,
consider the quotients and remainders
of $\randS = q_\randS n + r_\randS$ and $\randM = q_\randM n + r_\randM$
modulo $n$,
whose possible values as $\randS$ ranges over $[0,\randM)$ are shown below.
