Here, $\randS$ is equally likely to be any one of the elements whose
quotient is less than $q_\randM$.
%
Further, among all elements with quotient $q_\randS$,
$\randS$ is equally likely to have any remainder
$r_\randS \in \set{0,\dots,n-1}$.
%
It follows that $q_\randS \mid \set{q_\randS < q_\randM} \sim \Uniform[0,q_\randM)$
and $r_\randS \mid \set{q_\randS < q_\randM} \sim \Uniform[0,n)$.

