\subsection{Inverse Transform Sampling}
\label{sec:rr-inversion}

The Bernoulli generation algorithm can be generalized to sample
a rational discrete distribution $P \defeq (a_0,\dots,a_{n-1})/A$, where
$A \defeq a_0 + \dots + a_{n-1} > 0$.
%
This algorithm uses the (unnormalized) cumulative distribution function
$F \defeq (A_0, A_1, \dots, A_{n-1}\equiv A)$, i.e.,
an array of the prefix sums of the integers that define $P$, which
is either constructed from $P$ or given as a function $i \mapsto F_i$.
%
A uniform variate $U \sim \Uniform[0,A)$ is first generated, and
then $X \gets \min\set{ i \in [0,n) \mid U < A_i}$
is returned as the sample from $P$.
%
In this setup, the event $\set{X = i}$ is equivalent to the event
$U \in [A_{i-1}, A_i)$, where $A_{-1} \defeq 0$.
%
The uniformity of $U$ ensures that
$U \mid \set{X=i} \sim \Uniform[A_{i-1},A_i)$, which
gives the randomness recycling rule.

\begin{align*}
\NiceMatrixOptions{custom-line={letter = I, tikz = {line width=2pt, blue }}}
