The top array (which has $A_{n-1}$ elements) shows all possible values
of $U\sim \Uniform[0,A_{n-1})$.
%
The bottom array (which has $n$ elements) shows the cumulative probabilities
$F$ that determine $X$ from $U$.
%
\Cref{alg:inversion} shows a generic format for inversion samplers, which
recycle randomness from the leftover uniform random state in
\cref{prop:split-nonuniform}.
%
The search operation on \cref{algline:inversion-search} can be performed in
several different ways, depending on the usage pattern of the sampler.
\begin{itemize}
\item Using binary search on the cumulative probabilities
when they
are either given as input or computed as a preprocessing step.
This method is ideal for generating for multiple samples from the same distribution
since its space is linear and search time is logarithmic.
%
\item Using a linear scan over the array of probabilities or cumulative
probabilities.
%
This method is ideal when just a single sample from the distribution is
required (\cref{alg:inversion-scan}), because it does not require any
preprocessing.
%
\item Using lookup table sampling (\cref{sec:rr-lookup-table}).
This method is only suitable when the sum of weights $A$ is small but
delivers $O(1)$ search time.
\end{itemize}

