\subsection{Alias Sampling}
\label{sec:rr-alias-sampling}

The alias method~\citep{walker1977} is a state-of-the-art sampler that
avoids the exponential space complexity of the lookup table method while
retaining its extremely fast runtime.
%
In the preprocessing phase,
the target distribution $P = (a_0, \dots, a_{n-1}) / b$
is used to compute \begin{enumerate*}[label=(\roman*)]
\item the alias outcomes $\mathbf{z} \defeq (z_0, \dots, z_{n-1})$ where $z_i \in [0,n-1] \setminus \set{i}$;
\item the no alias odds $\mathbf{w} \defeq (w_0, \dots, w_{n-1})$ with $w_i \in [0,b]$.
\end{enumerate*}
\citep{vose1991}.
%
Equipped with these data structures, the generation phase is as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item Draw a uniform index $I \sim \Uniform[0,n)$.
\item Flip a coin $B \sim \Bernoulli(w_I/b)$;
  if $B=1$ then return $X \gets I$, else return $X \gets z_I$.
\end{itemize}
%
For any discrete distribution $P$, it is always possible to construct an
alias data structure that guarantees the return value $X$ is distributed
according to $P$ \citep[Theorem 4.1]{devroye1986}.
%
That is, for a given outcome $j \in [0,n)$, let $k_1, \dots, k_m$
be the indexes for which $j$ is an alias (i.e., $z_{k_i} = j$, for $i=1,\dots,m$).
%
The alias method guarantees that
\begin{align}
\Pr(X = i) \defeq 1/n\cdot w_j/b + \sum_{i=1}^{m} 1/n \cdot (b-w_{k_i})/b = a_j/b
&& ( 0 \le j < n).
\end{align}

A na\"ive approach to randomness recycling with the alias sampler
is to sample $I \gets \Call{Uniform}{n}$ using \cref{alg:uniform}
and $B \gets \Call{Bernoulli}{w_I, b}$ using \cref{alg:bernoulli}.
%
However, this approach does not optimally recycle randomness.
%
Instead, the optimal recycling rule is as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Sample $U \sim \Uniform[0,nb)$.
  \item Write $U = q_U b + r_U$,
    where $q_U \defeq \floor{U/b}$ and $r_U \defeq U \bmod b$.
    %
    Here $I \equiv q_U \sim \Uniform[0,n)$ furnishes a uniformly
    chosen index in $[0,n)$ and $r_U \sim \Uniform[0,b)$ furnishes the uniform
    needed to generate the $\Bernoulli(w_I/b)$ flip.
    %
  \item If $r_U < w_{I}$, then return $X \gets I$,
  else return $X \gets z_{I}$, and recycle a uniform over $n a_X$
  using an auxiliary array $\mathbf{c}$ as described in the remainder of this section.
\end{itemize}
%
Conditional on the event $\set{X=i}$, the selected cell in the alias table
is uniform over all $n a_i$ ways the label $i$ could have been achieved.

\input{fig-alias}

This idea is best visualized in terms of an example.
%
Let
\begin{equation}
P \defeq \set{
  \mathrm{A} \mapsto 4 \eqdef a_0,
  \mathrm{B} \mapsto 3 \eqdef a_1,
  \mathrm{C} \mapsto 3 \eqdef a_2,
  \mathrm{D} \mapsto 1 \eqdef a_3
  } / 11
\end{equation}
be the target distribution, where we use the symbols
$\mathrm{A}=0, \mathrm{B}=1, \mathrm{C}=2, \mathrm{D}=3$ to avoid
confusion between the outcome labels and other
integers in the alias data structures.
%
The corresponding data structures for alias sampling are shown in
\cref{fig:alias}, where $*$ denotes an arbitrary value.
%
Note that the table itself is never stored in memory.
%
In this example, consider two cases:
\begin{itemize}
\item Suppose that $(q_U, r_U) = (1,2)$ specifies the cell $(\mathrm{B}, 2)$.
%
Then conditioned on $\set{X = \mathrm{B}}$, the value $2$ is uniformly
distributed over $[0,12)$, i.e., the indexes of all other cells labeled
$\mathrm{B}$ in the table.
%
We can directly recycle $(\randS', \randM') = (2, 12)$ into the global
uniform state, where $\randS' = r_U = 2$ and $\randM'=n a_1 = 12$.

\item Suppose that $(q_U,r_U) = (1,7)$ specifies the
cell $(\mathrm{A}, 12)$.
%
Then conditioned on $\set{X = \mathrm{A}}$, the value $12$ is uniformly
distributed over $[0,16)$, i.e., the indexes of all other cells labeled
$\mathrm{A}$ in the table.
%
We can thus recycle $(\randS', \randM') = (12, 16)$ into the uniform state.
%
To compute the value $\randS' = 12$ from $(q_U, r_U) = (1,7)$, we first add
the number of $\mathrm{A}$ cells to the left of the current column
($11$), then subtract the number of $\mathrm{B}$ cells in the current
column $(6)$ to obtain the offset $c_1=11-6=5$, and finally add $r_U = 7$ to obtain
$\randS'=c_1+r_U=5+7=12$.
%
The bound $\randM'=16 = n a_{z_1}$ is available as in the previous case.
\end{itemize}

To enhance the alias sampler with this randomness recycling rule, we
construct an array $\mathbf{c} \defeq (c_0, \dots, c_{n-1})$ of the offsets
needed to perform recycling as described above, which can be done in linear
time.
%
\Cref{alg:alias} shows the resulting alias sampler with randomness recycling.

\input{alg-alias}

\subsection{Discrete Distribution Generating Tree Sampling}
\label{sec:rr-ddg}

A discrete distribution generating (DDG) tree is a universal computational
model introduced by \citet{knuth1976} for describing any computable
sampling algorithm that maps random bits to discrete outcomes.
%
A DDG tree $G$ is a complete, rooted binary tree where each leaf node
has an outcome label $i \in \Nat$.
%
DDG sampling operates as follows, starting from the root node of $G$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(D\arabic*), wide=15pt, leftmargin=*,]
  \item\label{ddg:flip} Obtain a fair coin flip $B \gets \Call{Flip}{1}$. If $B=0$,
    then visit the left child of the current node; else if $B=1$, then
    visit the right child.
  \item\label{ddg:decide} If the visited child node is a leaf node, then
  return its label; else go to \labelcref{ddg:flip}.
\end{enumerate}
%
The set of leaf nodes in a DDG tree $G$ is denoted $\mathcal{L}(G)$.
The depth and label of any leaf $l \in \mathcal{L}(G)$,
are denoted $d(l) \ge 0$ and $\ell(l) \in \Nat$, respectively.
%
With these notations, a random variable $X \sim G$ obtained
by DDG sampling~\labelcrefrange{ddg:flip}{ddg:decide}
has the following distribution:
\begin{align}
\Pr(X = i) = \sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}(G)} 2^{-d(l)} \cdot \mathbb{I}[\ell(l) = i].
\label{eq:ddg-output-dist}
\end{align}
The expected number of coin tosses used to sample $X$ is equal to the
average depth of a leaf:
\begin{align}
\expect{T_G} = \sum_{l \in \mathcal{L}(G)} 2^{-d(l)} \cdot d(l).
\label{eq:ddg-entropy-cost}
\end{align}

As discussed in \cref{sec:intro-existing}, \citet{knuth1976} show how to
construct an entropy-optimal DDG tree $G^*$ for any distribution distribution
$P \defeq (a_0, \dots, a_{n-1}) / A$ whose expected number of coin flips
\cref{eq:ddg-entropy-cost} is the least possible.
%
However, explicitly constructing $G^*$ can require exponential space in the
number of bits needed to encode $P$ \citep[Theorem 3.6]{saad2020popl}.
%
An alternative approach is to incrementally traverse $G^*$ without
explicitly constructing the tree
(e.g., \citet[p.~384]{knuth1976}, \citet[Algorithm 1]{saad2025}),
although the steps \labelcref{ddg:flip,ddg:decide} become more complicated
as the leaves and labels must be now created during sampling.

The Fast and Amplified Loaded Dice Roller~\citep{saad2020fldr,draper2025}
algorithms are near entropy-optimal DDG samplers that use rejection
sampling to reduce the space complexity of explicitly stored DDG trees.
%
These methods can be interpreted as a compression of the lookup table method
from \cref{sec:rr-lookup-table} using power-of-two block sizes.
%
The key idea is to build an entropy-optimal DDG tree $G'$ for
$P' \defeq (a_0, \dots, a_{n-1}, a_n) / 2^k$
where $2^k$ is a power of two that is larger than the sum of weights $A$ and
$a_n = 2^k - A$ is a ``reject'' outcome.
%
\Citet{saad2020popl} and \citet{draper2025} show that with a linearithmic
sized tree $G'$ it is possible to achieve expected costs less than $H(P)+6$
and $H(P)+2$, respectively, where the latter coincides with the entropy-optimal
\citet{knuth1976} range $[H(P), H(P)+2)$.
%
\Cref{fig:recycle-ddg-naive} shows an example DDG tree $G$, where the
colors of the leaf nodes are suggestive of the recycling strategy.

\input{fig-recycle-ddg-naive}
\input{fig-recycle-ddg}
\input{alg-ddg}

\paragraph{Recycling Rule}

Unlike the randomness recycling perspective from the previous sections
which extract a uniform state from a uniform state,
the recycling rule for DDG sampling extracts
a uniform state $(\randS', \randM')$ from a \textit{nonuniform state},
in the sense of \cref{sec:random-states-merge-nonunif}.
%
In particular, we perfectly recycle the distribution of \textit{depth}
of the leaf nodes conditioned on the
\textit{label} of the visited leaf node, which gives a nonuniform distribution
$\Discrete(\mathbf{w})$ as in \cref{prop:merge-nonuniform} whose weights
$w_i$ are distinct powers of two corresponding to the set bits in the
binary expansion of the label probability.

More specifically, consider any entropy-optimal DDG tree $G$ whose output
distribution is $P = (a_0, \dots, a_{n-1}, a_n)/A$, where $A$ is a power of two
and $a_n$ is a
``reject'' outcome that implicitly denotes a back-edge to the root.
%
Each leaf node in $G$ has a label $i \in [0,n]$.
%
Let $c_i \ge 1$ denote the (finite)
number of leaves in $G$ with label $i$.
%
Further, let $0 \le d_{i1} < d_{i2} < \dots < d_{ic_i}$ denote the
depths of the leaves with label $i$, which are distinct since $G$
is entropy optimal.
%
Conditioned on the event $\set{X=i}$, the distribution over
the possible leaves with this label is given by
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{w}_i = (w_{i1}, w_{i2}, \dots, w_{ic_i})
= (2^{d_{i c_i}-d_{i1}}, 2^{d_{i c_i}-d_{i2}} \dots, 1)
\propto
(2^{-d_{i1}}, 2^{-d_{i2}} \dots, 2^{-d_{ic_i}})
\end{equation}
%
Conditioned on returning $\set{X=i}$, DDG sampling selects \textit{one}
of these $c_i$ leaves as an exact nonuniform draw $X' \sim \Discrete(\mathbf{w}_i)$.
%
For example, in \cref{fig:recycle-ddg-naive}, the (unnormalized)
distribution over depths given $\set{X = \mathrm{A}}$ is given by
$\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{A}} = (4, 1) \propto (2^{-2}, 2^{-4})$.
%
The randomness recycling rule is immediate from
\cref{prop:merge-nonuniform}, which shows how to recycle
the nonuniform state $X'$ using a fresh draw $U \sim \Uniform[0,w_{X'})$.
%
The challenge with this approach is that it is difficult to guarantee
the global uniform state will not overflow the $W$-bit buffer size.

\paragraph{Efficient Implementation without Overflow}
%
To avoid explicitly merging a nonuniform random state, it is more convenient to
implement randomness recycling on a (hypothetical) DDG tree $G'$
whose leaves are all at the same depth, as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item Replace each leaf node in $G$ at depth $d$ with a subtree that
  terminates at the maximum depth of $G$; and set all
  the labels of the new leaf nodes to be the same label as the original leaf.
\item Pack all the non-reject outcomes to the left of the resulting tree.
\item Use $U \sim \Uniform[0,A)$ to select one of the $A$ leaves at the final level.
\end{itemize}
%
This approach produces a tree $G'$ as shown in \cref{fig:recycle-ddg}.
%
\Cref{alg:ddg} shows the corresponding algorithm for sampling a
``left-packed'' DDG tree of this form whose leaves are all at the same level.
%
Because the leaves in \cref{fig:recycle-ddg} are all at the same level,
the recycling rule reverts to the typical uniform case as in the inversion,
lookup, and alias sampler implementations from the previous sections, rather than
the nonuniform case using \cref{prop:merge-nonuniform} if the tree was
of the form in \cref{fig:recycle-ddg-naive}.

\Cref{alg:ddg} uses fast bit operations to identify the sampled leaf $X$
and implement the recycling rule without actually
constructing the hypothetical tree $G'$.
%
In particular, when a leaf node with label $X$ is encountered (\cref{algline:fldr-hit-leaf}),
a uniform $\randS' \sim \Uniform[0,\randM')$ with $\randM' \defeq a_X$ is extracted.
%
The value of $\randS'$ is computed by first choosing one of the ``hypothetical''
leaf nodes below the current leaf (dashed edges in \cref{fig:recycle-ddg})
using $U \bmod 2^{k-d}$, and then computing the offset
$a_X - (a_X \bmod 2^{k+1-d})$ which is the sum of all hypothetical
leaves with label $X$ to the left.

\begin{remark}
For a distribution whose sum $A$ of weights is very close to a power of two,
as for ALDR \citep{draper2025} or any dyadic distribution,
using the division-optimized uniform sampler in
\cref{alg:uniform-near-pot} to generate $U \gets \Call{Uniform}{A}$
on \cref{algline:fldr-uniform} of \cref{alg:ddg} may improve the runtime.
\end{remark}

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

This section analyzes the entropy complexity of the randomness recycling
algorithms from \cref{sec:rr}.
%
The correctness of these algorithms when used to generate a random sequence
rests on the fact that they maintain the invariant \labelcref{invariant} by
adhering to the contract \labelcref{contract}, as guaranteed by the operations
of merging and splitting random states from \cref{sec:random-states}.
%
It is always straightforward to verify that the space and time complexity
of the randomness recycling algorithms from \cref{sec:rr} match the complexity
of the original sampling algorithms without randomness recycling, provided
that the global buffer size $W$ is small enough that the call to
$\Call{Uniform}{}$ does not change the complexity by requiring operations
on substantially larger integers than are used in the original algorithms.

\subsection{Efficiency of Uniform Sampling}
\label{sec:analysis-uniform}

For any invocation of $\Call{Uniform}{n}$ (\cref{alg:uniform}), the call to $\Call{Refill}{}$
on \cref{algline:uniform-refill}
ensures that the global uniform state $(\randS, \randM)$ satisfies
$\randM \geq \randMin \defeq 2^{W-1}$.
%
The probability of rejection is thus $(\randM \bmod n) / \randM \leq (n-1)/\randM \leq (n-1)/\randMin$.
%
As discussed in \cref{sec:random-states-split-bernoulli},
the only entropy loss is from
discarding the information of a $\Bernoulli((\randM \bmod n) / \randM)$
flip from the accept/reject decision.
%
The total entropy loss from a given invocation is then the expected number
of trials times the expected entropy of the Bernoulli variable.
%
We now make this intuition precise.

Consider a sequence of $k$ invocations to $\Call{Uniform}{}$
to obtain samples $U_1, \dots, U_k$.
%
Denote the $i$th call as $\Call{Uniform}{n_i}$, where
$n_i \in [1,\randMin]$.
%
Let $(\randS_{i}, \randM_{i})$ be the value of the global uniform
state at the end of the call $\Call{Uniform}{n_i}$.
%
Let $T_i$ denote the (random) number of coin flips $\omega_j$
requested from the entropy source, across all invocations of \Call{Flip}{}
(through \Call{Refill}{}) made by the call $\Call{Uniform}{n_i}$.
%
With these notations, the expected \textit{entropy loss}
of the $i$th call is given by
\begin{align}
\expect{(\log(\randM_{i-1}) + T_i) - (\log(n_i) + \log(\randM_i))},
\label{eq:entropy-loss-uniform}
\end{align}
where $\expect{\log(\randM_{i-1}) + T_i}$ is the entropy consumed
and $\expect{\log(n_i) + \log(\randM_i)}$ is the entropy produced.
%
The expectation in \cref{eq:entropy-loss-uniform} is taken over
all the coin flips $\bm{\omega} \sim \Uniform(\set{0,1}^\infty)$
from the entropy source $\Call{Flip}{}$:
the random variables $(T_i, U_i, \randS_i, \randM_i)$ are all
deterministic functions of these flips.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:entropy-loss-uniform}
The expected entropy loss of any invocation
$\Call{Uniform}{n_i}$ of \cref{alg:uniform} satisfies
\setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{0pt}
\begin{align}
0
\leq
\expect{(\log(\randM_{i-1}) + T_i) - (\log(n_i) + \log(\randM_i))}
\leq
\frac{\randMin}{\randMin - n_i + 1} H_{\rm b}\left( \frac{n_i-1}{\randMin} \right).
\label{eq:entropy-loss-uniform-bound}
\end{align}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\newcommand{\randMi}[1][j]{\randM^-_{#1}}
\newcommand{\randMm}[1][j]{\randM^\star_{#1}}
\newcommand{\randMo}[1][j]{\randM^+_{#1}}
\newcommand{\randSi}[1][j]{\randS^-_{#1}}
\newcommand{\randSm}[1][j]{\randS^\star_{#1}}
\newcommand{\randSo}[1][j]{\randS^+_{#1}}
This entropy loss bound is justified informally
in \cref{sec:random-states-split-bernoulli}.
%
Here, we prove the bound using a direct analysis of
\cref{alg:uniform}.
%
Let $J \ge 1$ denote the random number of loops in the invocation
$\Call{Uniform}{n_i}$ until a sample is returned.
%
In each loop iteration $j$,
the random state is manipulated in two places:
the call to $\Call{Refill}{}$ on \cref{algline:uniform-refill}
at the start of the loop; and
the recycling on
\cref{algline:uniform-recycle-accept} or \cref{algline:uniform-recycle-reject}
at the end the of the loop.
%
To track the state, we define for $j \in [1,J]$
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
\item $(\randSi, \randMi)$ is the global random state at the start of iteration $j$;
\item $(\randSm, \randMm)$ is the global random state after the call to $\Call{Refill}{}$;
\item $(\randSo, \randMo)$ is the global random state at the end of iteration $j$.
\end{itemize}
%
These quantities satisfy
\begin{align}
\randMi[1] = \randM_{i-1}
&&
\randMi[j+1] = \randMo[j] (j \in [1,J-1])
&&
\randMo[J] = \randM_{i}.
\label{eq:entropy-loop-properties}
\end{align}
%
Let $T_{i,j}$ for $j \in [1,J]$ denote the number of fresh coin tosses
consumed in \cref{algline:refill-fresh-k} of $\Call{Refill}{}$.
%
\Cref{prop:merge-uniform} implies that
\begin{align}
\log(\randMm) = \log(\randMi) + T_{i,j} && (j \in [1,J]).
\label{eq:entropy-mid-in-toss}
\end{align}

The accept-reject step at iteration $j$ is
\begin{enumerate}[wide=15pt,widest={(Accept)},leftmargin=*]
\item[(Accept)] If $\randSm < n_i \floor{\randMm/n_i}$: then
$\randMo = \floor{\randMm/n_i}$; and the loop terminates
and a sample from $\Uniform[0,n_i)$ is successfully returned.
%
\item[(Reject)] Else: $\randMo = \randMm \bmod n_i$; and the loop continues.
\end{enumerate}
%
It follows that the output entropy $O_j$ at the end of iteration $j$
\begin{equation}
O_j = \left\lbrace\begin{array}{lll}
  \log(\floor{\randMm/n_i}) + \log(n_i)
    & \mbox{with probability } \displaystyle \frac{n_i \floor{\randMm/n_i}}{\randMm}
    &\mbox{(accept case)}
  \\
  \log(\randMm \bmod n_i)
    & \mbox{with probability } \displaystyle 1-\frac{n_i \floor{\randMm/n_i}}{\randMm}
    & \mbox{(reject case).}
\end{array}\right.
\label{eq:expected-output-entropy-step-j}
\end{equation}

The expected entropy loss at iteration $j$ is then
\begin{align}
\expect{ (\log(\randMi) + T_{i,j}) - O_j}
&= \expect{ (\log(\randMi) + T_{i,j} - \log(\randMm)) + (\log(\randMm) - O_j)} \\
&= \expect{(\log(\randMm) - O_j)}
\end{align}
via \cref{eq:entropy-mid-in-toss}.
%
Applying \cref{eq:expected-output-entropy-step-j} gives
\begin{align}
&\expect{\log(\randMm) - O_j}
\notag
\\
&=
\expect{
  \log(\randMm)
  -
  \frac{n_i \floor{\randMm/n_i}}{\randMm}
  \left(\log(\floor{\randMm/n_i})+ \log(n_i) \right)
-
  \frac{\randMm \bmod n_i}{\randMm}
  \left(\log(\randMm \bmod n_i)\right)
  }
\\
&=
\expect{
\frac{n_i \floor{\randMm/n_i}}{\randMm}
  \left(\log\left(\frac{\randMm}{n_i\floor{\randMm/n_i}} \right)\right)
  +
  \frac{\randMm \bmod n_i}{\randMm}
  \left(\log\left(\frac{\randMm}{\randMm \bmod n_i} \right)\right)
  }
\\
&=
\expect{H_{\rm b} \left( \frac{\randMm \bmod n_i}{\randMm} \right)}
\\
&\le
H_{\rm b} \left( \frac{(n_i-1)}{\randMin} \right).
\label{eq:iteration-bound-entorpy-loss}
\end{align}

We now argue that the sum of these entropy losses over the $J$
random iterations precisely corresponds
to the total entropy loss \cref{eq:entropy-loss-uniform}, as follows:
\begin{align}
&\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left(\left(\log(\randMi) + T_{i,j}\right) - O_j\right) \notag \\
&= \randMi[1] + \left(T_{i,1} + \dots T_{i,J}\right) + \sum_{j=2}^J(\randMi-\randMo[j-1]) - (\log(n_i) + \log(\randMo[J])) \\
&= \left(\randM_{i-1} + T_{i}\right) - (\log(n_i) + \log(\randM_{i})),
\end{align}
where we have used the properties in \cref{eq:entropy-loop-properties}
and the fact that $O_j$ is precisely in the \textit{reject case}
of \cref{eq:expected-output-entropy-step-j} for $j \in [1,J-1]$
(i.e., $\randMo = \randMm \bmod n_i = \randMi[j+1]$)
by the definition of $J$.
%

Finally, since the acceptance probability in \cref{eq:expected-output-entropy-step-j}
is at least $(\randMin -n_i + 1) / \randMin$, the expected number of iterations
$\expect{J} \le \randMin/(\randMin -n_i + 1)$, which combined with
\cref{eq:iteration-bound-entorpy-loss} gives the result:
\begin{align}
\expect{\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left( \log(\randMi) + T_{i,j} - O_j\right)}
&\le
\expect{\sum_{j=1}^{J}H_{\rm b} \left( \frac{(n_i-1)}{\randMin} \right)}
\\
&=
H_{\rm b} \left( \frac{(n_i-1)}{\randMin} \right) \expect{J}
\\
&\le
\frac{\randMin}{(\randMin -n_i + 1)} H_{\rm b} \left( \frac{(n_i-1)}{\randMin} \right).
\end{align}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Since \cref{eq:entropy-loss-uniform-bound} holds for any
integers $n_1, \dots, n_k$, it also holds in expectation
when $N_i=f_i(U_1,\dots,U_{i-1})$ is a random variable
that is an arbitrary function of the previous uniforms.
%
It also holds if there is arbitrary external randomness or nondeterminism
in the determination of the $N_i$, as long these entropy sources are
independent of the coin flips $\bm{\omega}$ returned by $\Call{Flip}{}$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
In practice, a typical setting to minimize entropy loss
is letting $\randMin=2^{63}$
(i.e, $W=64$; guaranteed to fit in a \texttt{long long int} word)
and $n \le 2^{32}$ (guaranteed to fit in a \texttt{long int} word).
%
From \cref{prop:entropy-loss-uniform}, these values
guarantee an expected entropy loss that is less than
$2 \times 10^{-8}$ bits,
and a similar bound parametric in $n$ is
$5 \times 10^{-10} \log(n)$ bits.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Efficiency of Nonuniform Sampling}
\label{sec:analysis-nonuniform}

We now analyze the entropy cost of randomness recycling samplers for
a sequence $(X_i)_{i\ge1}$ of general discrete random variables,
as described in the problem formulation from \cref{sec:intro-problem}.
%
All the algorithms in \crefrange{sec:rr-bernoulli}{sec:rr-ddg}
for nonuniform sampling perform exactly one call to
$U \gets \Call{Uniform}{}$ and then reversibly decompose $U$
into a sample $X$ from the desired target distribution together with a
leftover uniform state $(\randS', \randM')$ that is recycled into
the global uniform state using the techniques from \cref{sec:random-states}.
%
Therefore, the expected entropy loss of generating $X_i$,
\begin{equation}
\expect{(\log(\randM_{i-1}) + T_i) - (H(P_i(x_i \mid X_1, \dots, X_{i-1})) + \log(\randM_i))},
\label{eq:entropy-loss-nonuniform}
\end{equation}
corresponds precisely to the entropy loss of generating the corresponding
uniform $U_i$.
%
In \cref{eq:entropy-loss-nonuniform},
the expectation
$\expect{H(P_i(x_i \mid X_1, \dots, X_{i-1}))}$
of the entropy of the random distribution of $X_i$
is taken over $(X_1, \dots, X_{i-1})$, which is
precisely the definition of the conditional entropy
$H(X_i \mid X_1,\dots,X_{i-1})$.

\begin{corollary}
\label{prop:entropy-loss-nonuniform}
The expected entropy loss of generating $X_i$ when
using a sequence of calls to any randomness recycling sampler satisfies
% \setlength{\belowdisplayskip}{0pt}
\begin{align}
\begin{aligned}[t]
0
&\leq \expect{(\log(\randM_{i-1}) + T_i) - (H(P_i(x_i \mid X_1, \dots, X_{i-1})) + \log(\randM_i))}
\\
&\leq
\frac{\randMin}{\randMin - n_i + 1} H_{\rm b}\left( \frac{n_i-1}{\randMin} \right),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:entropy-waste-nonuniform}
\end{align}
where $n_i$ is the number of outcomes of the uniform used
to sample $X_i$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Efficiency of Generating a Random Sequence}
\label{sec:analysis-seq}

To analyze the overall entropy cost of generating $(X_1, \dots, X_k)$
using a randomness recycling sampler $S$,
we can combine \cref{eq:entropy-waste-nonuniform} for $i =1,\dots,k$.
%
Recall that the global random state is initialized
in \cref{alg:recycle} as $(\randS_0, \randM_0) = (0,1)$,
and at every step the uniform state
satisfies $\randM_i < 2^\randW$ with probability one.
%
Let $T_S(k) \defeq T_1 + \dots + T_k$ denote the total number of coin flips
requested from the entropy source across all invocations of \Call{Flip}{}.
%
Applying linearity of expectation to \cref{prop:entropy-loss-nonuniform},
the chain rule of entropy, the facts $\log(M_0) = 1$ and
$\expect{\log(M_{k})} < \randW$, and cancelling the intermediate
expressions $\log(M_i)$ for $i\in[1,k)$ from the telescoping sum gives the
following result.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:entropy-waste-total}
The expected entropy loss of generating $(X_1, \dots, X_k)$ when using
a sequence of calls to any randomness recycling sampler $S$ satisfies
\begin{align}
0 \leq \expect{T_S(k) - H(X_1, \ldots, X_k)}
< k \frac{\randMin}{\randMin-d+1} H_{\rm b}\left( \frac{d-1}{\randMin} \right) + W,
\end{align}
where $d \in [1, \randMin]$ is an upper bound on the argument
$n$ in any invocation of $\Call{Uniform}{n}$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection[Proof of \crefnameof{theorem}~\ref{theorem:entropy-cost}]{Proof of \cref{theorem:entropy-cost}}
\label{sec:analysis-theorem}

This section proves in \cref{theorem:entropy-cost}, which is restated below.

\EntropyCost*

\begin{proof}
\Cref{alg:loop} is a witness to the sampler $S$ from
the theorem statement, which makes repeated use of the linear-scan inversion
sampler (\cref{alg:inversion-scan}) to generate a random sequence $(X_i)_{i\ge1}$.

\input{alg-loop}

The $\Call{Recycle}{\randS', \randM'}$, $\Call{Refill}{{}}$,
and $\Call{Uniform}{n}$ methods each use $O(W)$ auxiliary space.
%
The global uniform state $(\randS, \randM)$,
which is the only state carried over between samples, uses $2W$ bits.

To arrive at the entropy bound in \cref{theorem:entropy-cost},
by \cref{prop:entropy-waste-total} it suffices to find $W \ge 1$ such that
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{1-(d-1)/2^{W-1}} H_{\rm b}\left( \frac{d-1}{2^{W-1}} \right)
\leq \varepsilon.
\end{align}
%
Let $f(\delta) \defeq H_{\rm b}(\delta) / (1 - \delta)$.
%
Then $f(\delta) \sim \delta \log(1/\delta)$ as $\delta \to 0$,
so $W(d, \varepsilon) \defeq 1 + \ceil{\log((d-1) / f^{-1}(\varepsilon))}$
satisfies $W(d, \varepsilon) \sim \log(d / \varepsilon)$
as $d / \varepsilon \to \infty$,
which matches the claimed entropy-space tradeoff.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The exact inverse of $f(\delta)$ requires solving a transcendental equation,
which is generally not possible in closed form, but it can be approximated
with a series expansion, which may look roughly like
$W(d,\varepsilon) \approx 1+\ceil{\log(d) + \sum_{m=1}^{\log^*(1/\varepsilon)} \log^{\circ{m}}(1/\varepsilon)}$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
\Cref{alg:loop} is specialized for the case where the different distributions
$x_i \mapsto P_i(x_i \mid X_1, \ldots, X_{i-1})$ are arbitrary and given in
the form of an array of weights, where no sublinear-time sampler is possible.
%
If instead the conditional distribution of $X_i$
is given as a cumulative distribution function
$x_i \mapsto P_i(X_i \le x_i \mid X_1=x1,\dots,X_{i-1}=x_{i-1})$ then the
binary-search implementation of \cref{alg:inversion} is more efficient
in time and space.
%
A detailed discussion of the complexity properties of random sampling under
different specifications of the input distribution (e.g., as pointwise
probabilities or cumulative probabilities; and possibly as computable
reals) is available in \citet{Trevisan2010,yamakami1999}.
\end{remark}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\Cref{sec:intro-existing} describes existing approaches to generating
a random sequence as described in the problem statement from \cref{sec:intro-problem}.
%
In this section, we discuss related work in the broader context of
algorithms that recycle random bits.

The term ``randomness recycler'' appears to have originated in
\citet{fill2000}, who introduce the concept as an exact (perfect) sampling
technique that stands in contrast to approximate sampling using Markov
chains.
%
\Citeauthor{fill2000} show how to apply randomness recycling in challenging
combinatorial settings such as generating random independent sets, random
graph colorings, Ising models, random cluster models, and self-organizing
lists in expected linear time.
%
These randomness recycling algorithms have better time complexity
than exact rejection sampling and are more accurate than approximate
sampling using Markov chains.
%
The authors also note that randomness recycling is not universally applicable
in all scenarios where Markov chains are used, but it can efficiently generate
perfect samples in linear time for challenging combinatorial problems.
%
Our work applies randomness recycling ideas for the problem statement
in \cref{sec:intro-problem}, to obtain improvements in space, time,
and entropy trade-offs as compared to the existing approaches
described in \cref{sec:intro-existing}.

The randomness recycling method for uniform sampling
described in \cref{sec:random-states-split-bernoulli,sec:rr-uniform}
corresponds to a method described by \citet{jacques2004}
on the Dr.~Math Forum.
%
\Citet{mennucci2010} empirically analyzes this method for scaling uniform random
number generators, and performs extensive numerical tests with specialized
optimization using bitwise operations on different machine architectures.
%
Our contributions build on these works by
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item extending randomness recycling techniques to algorithms
  that sample \textit{nonuniform} distributions; including
  Bernoulli sampling, inversion sampling, lookup table sampling, alias
  sampling, and DDG tree sampling (\crefrange{sec:rr-bernoulli}{sec:rr-ddg});
%
\item theoretically analyzing the expected entropy loss of these methods in \cref{sec:analysis};
%
\item using these algorithms and analyses
  to establish a new exact sampler for generating a random sequence whose
  expected amortized entropy cost is arbitrary close to the optimal
  \citeauthor{shannon1948} entropy rate (\cref{theorem:entropy-cost}).
\end{enumerate}

\Citet{impagliazzo1989} describe randomness recycling techniques
in the context of bounded-error probabilistic polynomial time (BPP)
algorithms.
%
In this context, the recycled random state does not satisfy the
independence invariant \labelcref{invariant} and the sampler is allowed to
be approximate.
%
The authors show how to run a BPP algorithm multiple times while using
(approximately independent) recycled random bits from applying hash
functions to more efficiently amplify the correctness probability as
compared to using fresh random bits on each trial.

\section{Implementation Details}
\label{sec:implementation}

A C library containing the algorithms described in this paper is available
at \url{https://github.com/probsys/randomness-recycling}.
%
We briefly describe some practical details about the implementation
of these algorithms.

\subsection{Buffering, Bits, Bytes, and Words}

A common software interface to the entropy source is $\Call{Flip$_s$}{b}$,
which returns $b \ge 0$ blocks each with size $s \ge 1$ bits, for a total
number of $sb$ bits.
%
For example, the \texttt{getrandom} system call on Linux
returns $b \ge 0$ random \textit{bytes} ($s=8$ bits).
%
\Cref{alg:refill-blocks} shows how to modify $\Call{Refill}{}$
(\cref{alg:refill}) for entropy sources that return blocks of random bits.
%
\input{alg-refill-blocks}

\begin{remark}
The \texttt{getrandom} system call in Linux
is guaranteed to succeed whenever the number of requested
bytes $b \le b_{\rm max} \defeq 256$.
%
To make most judicious use of the entropy source, it is advantageous
to create a PRNG buffer $B' = \set{0,1}^{s\cdot b_{\rm max}}$.
%
For example, in the C programming language,
using $\texttt{uint8\_t prng\_buffer[256]}$ is sufficient
to store the $2048$ bits returned by \texttt{getrandom}.

Given such a buffer of bytes, there are several optimizations that can
be made to the $\Call{Refill}{}$ and $\Call{Uniform}{}$ algorithms.
%
If the global uniform state uses $W$ bits and the hardware natively supports
widening multiplication (from two $W$-bit integers to a $2W$-bit integer),
then it is unnecessary to use the value of $\randS$ directly in
$\Call{Uniform}{}$ to avoid overflowing the uniform state.
%
Instead, $\Call{Refill}{}$ can be modified to use the widening multiplication,
and feed extra bytes from the result into the PRNG buffer in the case that
the result uses more than $W$ bits.
%
Extracting these bytes requires only bitwise operations to perform the split
from \cref{prop:split-uniform} as the divisor is a power of two.
%
With this optimization, $\Call{Uniform}{}$ can directly use uniforms over $2^{W}$
from the PRNG buffer as its randomness source.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Minimizing Divisions}

Integer division instructions are relatively expensive compared to
multiplication, and can create performance bottlenecks in
practical applications~\citep{lemire2019,brackett2025}.
%
There are several ways to reduce the number of integer divisions.

\paragraph{Bernoulli Sampling}

\Cref{alg:bernoulli-opt} is an optimized version of \cref{alg:bernoulli}
that eliminates one division.
%
Because the call to $\Call{Uniform}{b}$ has been inlined for further optimization,
the algorithm now also requires direct read and write access to the global
variables $(\randS, \randM)$ from \cref{alg:recycle}.

\input{alg-bernoulli-opt}

\paragraph{Uniform Sampling near a Power of Two}

When the PRNG is reasonably fast, the runtime of \cref{alg:uniform} is
dominated by the $\Call{DivMod}{}$ operation.
%
If $n$ is a power of $2$, then this expensive division can be replaced with
fast bitwise operations.
%
It is also possible to draw $\log{n}$ random bits directly from the entropy
source in this case, but then recycling would in general overflow the
$W$-bit buffer size of the global random state.
%
If $n$ is slightly smaller than a power of two, it can similarly be faster
to use a uniform over the next power of two to avoid division, with small
additional rejection as shown in \cref{alg:uniform-near-pot}.
%
This variant eliminates a division at the expense of a slightly higher
entropy consumption than \cref{alg:uniform}.

