\textbf{Failure‐Recovery Techniques}
Erasure coding (EC) significantly lowers repair bandwidth compared to full replication~\cite{10.1007/3-540-45748-8_31}.
To further reduce recovery latency, schemes such as proactive repair rebuild lost chunks immediately upon failure detection, or even preemptively when failures are predicted~\cite{9721141,10.1145/3708994}. These techniques focus solely on the repair phase, and can be layered on top of our chunking and placement model without any modifications. Checkpoint-restart is another actively researched area. Several efforts leverage multiple storage levels to asynchronously hide the I/O overhead of persisting checkpoints~\cite{VeloCIPDPS19, VELOC-SuperCheck21, VELOC-FGCS24}. EC is used to protect the node-local checkpoints against node failures, as an alternative to storing checkpoints to a parallel file system. 
