To show the effectiveness of the \drex{} algorithms, we conduct real experiments with a wide-area storage system called DynoStore (DS) \cite{dynostore,sanchez2025dynostore}. DynoStore implements a federated storage overlay across distributed storage nodes using common interfaces called data containers. DynoStore can be configured to use different algorithms to map data items to storage nodes. Thus, while DynoStore manages the access to data and storage resources, we use \drex{} to control the chunking and placement of data items.
We deploy DynoStore with \drex{} using ten storage nodes distributed across three Chameleon Cloud sites: CHI@UC, CHI@TACC, and CHI@NRP~\cite{keahey2020lessons}. To achieve a heterogeneous setup, we selected nodes of various types and purposes. The hardware details are presented in Table \ref{tb:chainf}.
