%-------------------------------- ABSTRACT 
\begin{abstract}
We provide a rough sets semantics for the three-valued extension of first-order Priest's da Costa logic, which we studied in a previous paper. 
The semantics follows the usual pattern of the semantics for first-order classical logic.
\end{abstract}
------------------------------------%

\section{Introduction}

In \cite{MCTN} we studied a three-valued first-order logic given in the signature $\{\land, \lor, \neg, D, \bot \}$. 
We use the symbol $D$ for the dual of intuitionistic negation, using the notion of duality in the sense already present in \cite{ES}. 
The logic under consideration will be called {\bf{ID$_3$}}. 
In this paper we present a rough sets semantics for the mentioned logic.

We start Section 2 presenting {\bf{ID$_3$}}. 
Afterwards, using $\Box$ instead of $D$, we give a logic that is equivalent by translation to {\bf{ID$_3$}}. 
As expected, the formula $\Box \alpha$ may be read as ``$\alpha$ is necessary".

Section 3 deals with the algebraic semantics and Kripke models for {\bf{ID$_3$}}, notions that will be used in the next sections.

For the reader not acquainted with rough sets, Section 4 begins stating the basic information in order to render this paper self-contained. 
Afterwards, we introduce the announced rough set semantics for ID$_3$. 

Relating the rough set semantics with the Kripke models, in Section 5 we prove soundness and completeness of ID$_3$ using the results of soundness and completeness proved in \cite{MCTN}.

Finally, there is an Appendix where we consider the univocity of the unary connectives used.


