We will use the symbol $\vdash$ in the context $\Gamma \vdash \alpha$ (where $\Gamma$ is a set of formulas and $\alpha$ is a formula) with the usual meaning, that is, indicating the existence of at least one derivation of $\alpha$ from $\Gamma$. 
We will not add a subscript to the symbol $\vdash$ as the context will make clear what logic is being meant.

\begin{lem}
Let $\alpha$ be any formula.
Then,

$\neg \neg D \alpha \dashv \vdash D\alpha$.
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}
The right-to-left direction is immediate just using the $\neg$-rules. 
For the other direction, by (cS) it holds that $DD\alpha \vdash \neg D\alpha$ whence by antimonotonicity of $\neg$ it follows that $\neg \neg D \alpha \vdash \neg DD \alpha$ whence by ($D$I) it follows that $\neg \neg D \alpha \vdash D\alpha$.
\end{proof}

In general, it holds that if $M$ is an even string of modalities, then $M\neg \alpha \dashv \vdash \neg \alpha$ and  $MD \alpha \dashv \vdash D\alpha$ and if $M$ is an odd string of modalities, then $M\neg \alpha \dashv \vdash \neg \neg \alpha$ and  $MD \alpha \dashv \vdash DD\alpha$. 
As a consequence, the modalities are as in the following figure.

