Note that the intuitionistic conditional $\alpha \to \beta$ may be defined as $\neg(\alpha \land \neg \beta) \land (D\alpha \lor \beta)$ and so also the biconditional $\alpha \leftrightarrow \beta$ as $(\alpha \to \beta) \land (\beta \to \alpha)$.

\begin{prop} Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be any formulas. Then, 

(i) If $\alpha \vdash \beta$, then $D\beta \vdash D\alpha$,

(ii) If $\alpha \dashv \vdash \beta$, then $D\alpha \dashv \vdash D\beta$,

(iii) If $\alpha \dashv \vdash \beta$, then $\vdash \delta^\beta_\alpha \dashv \vdash \delta$, for any formula $\delta$.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
Supposing the hypothesis of part (i) by ($D$I) it follows that $\vdash \beta \lor D\alpha$ whence supposing $D\beta$ by ($D$E) it follows that $D\alpha$. 

 Part (ii) follows by part (i).

 Part (iii) follows by part (ii) and similar properties in the case of the intuitionistic connectives.
\end{proof}

Note that by algebraic soundness it may be easily seen in the three-element chain that neither $\alpha \to \beta \vdash D\beta \to D\alpha$ nor $\alpha \leftrightarrow \beta \vdash D\alpha \leftrightarrow D\beta$ are the case.

Finally, the usual Gentzen quantifier rules are also included. 
As stated in the Introduction, our logic will be called {\bf{ID$_3$}}.


