We call this two-element Kripke model, \emph{the Kripke model associated to the rough interpretation $\mathcal{I}$}. 

It is possible to prove the following fact.

\begin{prop} \label{ItoK}
For every formula $\alpha$ it holds that

$v_{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha) = 1$ iff $1 \Vdash_e \alpha$ \ and \ 
$\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha)$ iff $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_e \alpha$. 
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
We check the cases of the atomic formulas, some connectives and the universal quantifier, leaving the rest for the reader.

$1 \Vdash_e R(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ iff 
$(e(x_1),\dots, e(x_n)) \in R^\rho_1$ if and only if  
$(f(x_1),\dots, f(x_n)) \in \underline{R^\sigma}$ if and only if $v_{_\mathcal{I}}(R(x_1,\dots,x_n)) = 1$.

$\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_e R(x_1,\dots, x_n)$ iff 
$(e(x_1),\dots, e(x_n)) \in R^\rho_\frac{1}{2}$ if and only if 
$(f(x_1),\dots, f(x_n)) \in \overline{R^\sigma}$ if and only if
$\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{_\mathcal{I}}(R(x_1,\dots,x_n))$.

Let us now suppose that the proposition holds for $\alpha$ and $\beta$. 
We have to prove that it holds for $\alpha \land \beta$. 
If $1 \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha \land \beta)$, then $1 \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha) \land v{_\mathcal{I}}(\beta)$ iff $v{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha) = 1$ and $v{_\mathcal{I}}(\beta) = 1$.

If $\frac{1}{2} \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha \land \beta)$, then

Let us now suppose that the proposition holds for $\alpha$ and let us prove that it holds for $\neg \alpha$.

If $1 \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(\neg \alpha)$, then $v{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha) = 0$. 
So, by the inductive hypothesis $1 \nVdash_e \alpha$ and $\frac{1}{2} \nVdash_e \alpha$ and so $1 \Vdash_e \neg \alpha$.

If $\frac{1}{2} \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(\neg \alpha)$, then we proceed as in the previous case.

If $1 \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(D \alpha)$, then $v{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha) = 0$ or $v{_\mathcal{I}}(\alpha) = \frac{1}{2}$. 
In the first case, by the inductive hypothesis $1 \nVdash_e \alpha$ and $\frac{1}{2} \nVdash_e \alpha$ and so $1 \Vdash_e D\alpha$. 
In the second case, by the inductive hypothesis $1 \nVdash_e \alpha$ and $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_e \alpha$. 
So, $1 \Vdash_e D\alpha$.

If $\frac{1}{2} \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(D \alpha)$, then we proceed as in the previous case.

Let us now suppose that the proposition holds for $\alpha$ and prove that it holds for $\forall x \alpha$.

Firstly, $v{_\mathcal{I}}(\forall x\alpha) = 1$ iff 
$min\{v_{_{\mathcal{I}^{x/a}}}(\alpha): a \in U \} =1$ iff 
for all $a \in U$, it holds that $v_{_{\mathcal{I}^{x/a}}}(\alpha) = 1$ iff 
for all $a \in U$, it holds that $v_{_{\mathcal{I}^{x/a}}}(\alpha) = 1$ and $\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{_{\mathcal{I}^{x/a}}}(\alpha)$ iff (by the inductive hypothesis) 
for all $a \in U$ it holds that $1 \Vdash_{e^{a/x}}\alpha$ and $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_{e^{a/x}}\alpha$ iff
$1 \Vdash_e \forall x \alpha$.

Secondly, $\frac{1}{2} \leq v{_\mathcal{I}}(\forall x\alpha)$ iff 
$\frac{1}{2} \leq min\{v_{_{\mathcal{I}^{x/a}}}(\alpha): a \in U \}$ iff 
for all $a \in U$, it holds that $\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{_{\mathcal{I}^{x/a}}}(\alpha)$ iff (by the inductive hypothesis)
for all $a \in U$ it holds that $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_{e^{a/x}}\alpha$ iff 
$\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_e \forall x \alpha$.
\end{proof}

Reciprocally, given a Kripke model ${\bf{K}} = (K, \leq, U, \rho, e)$, with $K = \{1 < \frac{1}{2} \}$ and of the form of those studied in \cite{MCTN}, we define a rough-interpretation $\mathcal{I}_{\bf{K}}$ of $L$ as follows.
\begin{enumerate}
