	\end{eqnarray*}
\end{enumerate}

We can now prove the reciprocal of Proposition \ref{ItoK}, that is, the following fact.

\begin{prop} \label{KtoI}
For every formula $\alpha$, it holds that 

$1 \Vdash \alpha$ iff $v_{_{\mathcal{I}_{\bf{K}}}}(\alpha) = 1$  and $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash \alpha$ iff $\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{_{\mathcal{I}_{\bf{K}}}}(\alpha)$.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
We check the cases of the atomic formulas and the universal quantifier, leaving the rest for the reader.

Let us check it for the case that $\alpha = R(x_1,\dots, x_n)$. 

Firstly, it holds that $1 \Vdash R(x_1,\dots, x_n)$ if and only if
$(e(x_1), \dots, e(x_n)) \in R^{\rho}_1$ if and only if  
$((e(x_1),\varepsilon_1), \dots, (e(x_n),\varepsilon_n)) \in R^\sigma$, for all $\varepsilon_i \in \{0,1\}$ iff 
$(f(x_1), \dots, f(x_n)) \in \underline{R^\sigma}$ if and only if
$v_{_{\mathcal{I}_{\bf{K}}}}(R(x_1,\dots,x_n)) = 1$. 

Secondly, $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash R(x_1,\dots, x_n)$ iff 
$(e(x_1), \dots, e(x_n)) \in R^{\rho}_\frac{1}{2}$ iff 
$(f(x_1), \dots, f(x_n)) \in R^{\sigma}$ if and only if 
$(f(x_1), \dots, f(x_n)) \in \overline{R^{\sigma}}$ iff 
$\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{_{\mathcal{I}_{\bf{K}}}}(R(x_1,\dots,x_n))$.

Let us now suppose that the proposition holds for $\alpha$ and prove that it holds for $\forall x \alpha$.

Firstly, $1 \Vdash_e \forall x \alpha$ iff 
for all $a \in U$ we have that $1 \Vdash_{e^{a/x}} \alpha$ and $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_{e^{a/x}} \alpha$ iff (by the inductive hypothesis) 
for all $a \in U$, it holds that $v_{I_K^{a/x}} (\alpha) = 1$ and $\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{I_K^{a/x}} (\alpha) $ iff 
for all $a \in U$, it holds that $v_{I_K^{a/x}} (\alpha) = 1$ iff 
min $\{ v_{I_K^{a/x}}(\alpha) : a \in U \} = 1$ iff $v_{_{\mathcal{I}_{\bf{K}}}}(\forall x \alpha) = 1$.

On the other hand, $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_e \forall x \alpha$ iff 
for all $a \in U$ we have that $\frac{1}{2} \Vdash_{e^{a/x}} \alpha$ iff (by the inductive hypothesis)
for all $a \in U$, it holds that $\frac{1}{2} \leq v_{I_K^{a/x}} (\alpha) $ iff 
$\frac{1}{2} \leq$ min $\{ v_{I_K^{a/x}}(\alpha) : a \in U \}$ if and only if $v_{_{\mathcal{I}_{\bf{K}}}}(\forall x \alpha) = \frac{1}{2}$.
\end{proof}

Finally, we get the following result.

\begin{theo} \label{BT}
$\Gamma \Vdash \alpha$ if and only if $\Gamma \vDash \alpha$.
\end{theo}

\begin{cor}
The logic $ID_3$ is sound and complete relative to the rough sets semantics.
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem \ref{BT} and the fact that in \cite{MCTN} we proved that $ID_3$ is sound and complete relative to the two-element Kripke models considered above.
\end{proof}


\section{Appendix on univocity}

All the unary coonectives used in this paper are univocal. 
In the case of $\neg$ and $D$ we easily find a derivation to prove that $\neg \alpha \vdash \neg'\alpha$ and $D\alpha \vdash D'\alpha$ \ supposing that $\neg'$ and $D'$ have similar rules to the rules of $\neg$ and $D$, respectively. 
Indeed, check the following derivations:

