\begin{abstract}
\noindent Let $G=(V,E)$ be a connected graph, and let $T \subseteq V$ be a subset of vertices. An orientation of $G$ is called $T$-odd if any vertex $v \in V$ has odd in-degree if and only if it is in $T$. Finding a $T$-odd orientation of $G$ can be solved in polynomial time as shown by Chevalier, Jaeger, Payan and Xuong (1983). Since then, $T$-odd orientations have continued to attract interest, particularly in the context of global constraints on the orientation. For instance, Frank and Kir\'aly (2002)  investigated $k$-connected $T$-odd orientations and raised questions about acyclic $T$-odd orientations. This problem is now recognized as an Egres problem and is known as the "Acyclic orientation with parity constraints" problem. Szegedy (2005) proposed a randomized polynomial algorithm to address this problem. An easy consequence of his work provides a polynomial time algorithm for planar graphs whenever $|T| = |V|-1$. Nevertheless, it remains unknown whether it exists in general. In this paper we contribute to the understanding of the complexity of this problem by studying a more general one. We prove that finding a $T$-odd acyclic orientation on graphs having some directed edges is NP-complete.
\end{abstract}
\end{frontmatter}

\begin{keyword}
parity orientation, acyclic orientation, planar graph
\end{keyword}


\section{Introduction} \label{sec:introduction}

\noindent The study of constrained graph orientations and parity related problems have wield significant interest in recent years. A notable problem crossing these two domains is the acyclic orientation with parity constraints problem, which has been the subject of various research efforts. The first local constraint considered in this problem involves the parity of the in-degree of vertices in an orientation. For a graph $G = (V,E)$ and a subset $T \subseteq V$, an orientation of $G$ is called $T$-odd if any vertex $v \in V$ has odd in-degree if and only if it is in $T$.

\noindent Chevalier, Jaeger, Payan and Xuong (1983) \cite{chevalierOddRootedOrientations1983} demonstrated that finding a $T$-odd orientation of an undirected graph $G$ can be solved in polynomial time. Specifically, a $T$-odd orientation exists if and only if the size of $T$ has the same parity as the number of edges, i.e., $|E| + |T| \equiv_2 0$. This necessary condition shows in particular that any bridge in the graph has a forced orientation determined by the parity of the number of edges and vertices in $T$ within each of the connected components. Also, vertices of degree two in $T$, can be contracted into a single edge as they must have exactly one inward-edge and one outward-edge. These observations can be used to consider $T$-odd orientations only in 2-connected graphs, where vertices of $T$ have degree at least three and vertices of $V-T$ have degree at least $2$.\newline

\noindent Further advancements were made by Frank and Kir\'aly (2002) \cite{frankGraphOrientationsEdgeconnection2002} when adding a $k$-connectivity global constraint. They gave a characterization of undirected multigraphs $G = (V, E)$ having a $k$-edge-connected $T$-odd orientation for every subset $T \subseteq V$ with $|E| + |T| \equiv_2 0$.
It also raised questions on other global constraints such as acyclicity which can be seen as an other kind of connectivity constraint. Which leads to the current formulation of Problem \ref{prob:AOP}. 
\begin{problem}[Acyclic Orientation with parity constraints]\label{prob:AOP}\ \newline
	\textbf{Instance:} A pair $(G,T)$ with $G$ an undirected graph and $T \subset V$ a subset of its vertex set.\newline
	\textbf{Question:} Does $G$ have an acyclic $T$-odd orientation ?\newline
\end{problem}
\noindent This existence problem is now recognized as an Egres problem \cite{AcyclicOrientationParity} and is known as the "Acyclic orientation with parity constraints" problem. It is easy to check it is in NP. Indeed, given an orientation, one can verify in polynomial time whether it satisfies the in-degree constraints for each vertex and whether the orientation is acyclic. Still, it is not known whether Problem \ref{prob:AOP} is in Co-NP. Note also that the acyclicity constraint allows to reduce the natural generalization to multi-graphs. Indeed, every edge in a multi-edge must be directed the same way to preserve acyclicity and we could replace equivalently any odd multi-edge by a single edge and remove even multi-edges.\newline

\noindent Szegedy (2005) \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005} addressed Problem \ref{prob:AOP} by proposing a randomized polynomial algorithm. We expose in Section \ref{sec:Szegedy} an easy consequence of his work which provides a polynomial time algorithm whenever $G$ is planar and $|T| = \vert V\vert-1$. However, a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm remains unknown.\newline

\noindent In this paper we study a generalization of Problem \ref{prob:AOP} to partially directed graphs (see Problem \ref{prob:PDAOP}), which could be seen as an intermediate step that appears when attempting to build a graph orientation with a greedy approach.


\begin{problem}[Acyclic Orientation with parity constraints on partially directed graphs]\label{prob:PDAOP}\ \newline
	\textbf{Instance:} A pair $(G,T)$ where $G$ is a graph having a subset $A$ of edges with a fixed orientation and $T \subset V$ a subset of its vertex set.\newline
	\textbf{Question:} Does $G$ have an acyclic $T$-odd orientation preserving the fixed orientation of $A$ the pre-directed edges ?
\end{problem}


\noindent Our main result is to show that:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main}
    Problem \ref{prob:PDAOP} is NP-complete even restricted to planar graph of max degree $3$ and such that any vertex not in $T$ has degree $2$.
\end{theorem}

\noindent This class of instances is of interest. Indeed, thanks to Szegedy and Oxley's results \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005, szegedySymplecticSpacesEarDecomposition2006, oxleyMatroidTheory2011}, we exposed a polynomial algorithm for instances of Problem \ref{prob:AOP} where $G$ is planar and $|T| = |V|-1$. Szegedy \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005} gives a randomized polynomial algorithm to address Problem \ref{prob:AOP}. We show that the generalization of the problem \ref{prob:AOP} to \ref{prob:PDAOP} for instances where $G$ is a planar graph of max degree $3$ and vertices not in $T$ have degree $2$ is NP-complete. These result emphasizes the interest of this class of instances for Problem \ref{prob:AOP} and could lead to further results.


\section{Basic definitions} \label{sec:preliminaries}
\noindent A partially directed graph $G$ consists of a non-empty set $V(G)$ of vertices, a set $E(G)$ of edges (unordered pairs of vertices), and a set $A(G)$ of arcs (ordered pairs of vertices).  An edge will be denoted independently $uv$ or $vu$ with $u, v\in V$. However, an arc will be denoted $\dir{uv}$ or $\dir{vu}$ according to the ordering of the vertices $u$ and $v$. A graph $G$ is said to be undirected if $A(G)$ is empty, and directed if $E(G)$ is empty.\newline

\noindent Given a graph $G$ and a set $S\subset E(G) \cup A(G)$, an \textbf{orientation} $O$ of $S$ is a set of arcs such that $S\cap A(G)\subseteq O$ and for any edge $uv\in S \cap E(G)$ we have that either $\dir{uv} \in O$ or $\dir{vu} \in O$. Given a subset $S'\subseteq S$ and an orientation $O$ of $S$, we denote $O(S')$ the orientation of $S'$ such that $\dir{uv}\in O(S')$ if and only if $\dir{uv}\in O$ for any edge $uv\in S' \cap E(G)$. An {\bf orientation $O$ of a graph $G$} is an orientation of $E(G)\cup A(G)$ and it will be abusively denoted by $O(G)$ instead of $O(E(G)\cup A(G))$.


\noindent A set of arcs $A$ is said \textbf{acyclic} if the subgraph induced by $A$ does not contain any directed cycle. Similarly, a graph $G$ is acyclic if its arc set $A(G)$ is acyclic.


\noindent Given $X \subset V$, we define $\delta^\circ(X)$ to be the set of edges having exactly one vertex in $X$ and $\delta^+(X)$, $\delta^-(X)$ respectively to be the set of arcs directed outward and inward from $X$. Then we define the \textbf{boundary} of $X$ to be $\delta(X) \overset{def}{=} \delta^\circ(X) \cup \delta^+(X) \cup \delta^-(X)$. We say that $\delta(X)$ is {\bf uniform} if $\delta(X)\neq\emptyset$ and ($\delta(X)=\delta^-(X)$ or $\delta(X)=\delta^+(X)$).  We define also the set $\delta(X,Y) \overset{def}{=} \delta(X)\cap \delta(Y)$. For any vertex $v \in V(G)$, we define the \textbf{out-degree} $d^+(v) = |\delta^+(v)|$, the \textbf{in-degree} $d^-(v) = |\delta^-(v)|$ and the \textbf{edge-degree} $d^\circ(v) = |\delta^\circ(v)|$. The degree $d(v)$ is $|\delta(v)|$.\newline

\noindent We extend abusively those notations to subgraphs. Let $H$ be a subgraph of a graph $G$, the \textbf{boundary} of $H$ will abusively be noted $\delta(H)$ instead of $\delta(V(H))$. Similarly, $\delta^\circ(H)$, $\delta^+(H)$, $\delta^-(H)$ abusively denote $\delta^\circ(V(H))$, $\delta^+(V(H))$, $\delta^-(V(H))$ respectively and for two subgraphs $H_1,H_2$ of a graph $G$, $\delta(H_1,H_2)$ denotes $\delta(V(H_1),V(H_2))$. We will further need to consider subgraphs along with their boundary, thus, we define for any subgraph $H$ of $G$, the subgraph induced by the set of edges and arcs $\delta(H)\cup E(H)\cup A(H)$ noted as $\Gamma_H$.\newline

\noindent Let $I=(G,T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP}. We note $G(I) = G$ and $T(I) = T$. For simplicity, we note also $V(I) = V(G), E(I) = E(G)$ and $A(I) = A(G)$.
For the figures, we will refer to vertices in $T(I)$ as \textbf{black} vertices and vertices in $V(I) \bs T(I)$ as \textbf{white} vertices. It will also prove useful in the proofs to work with a slight generalization of $T$-odd orientations~: given a graph $G$, a subgraph $S$ and a subset $T \subset V(G)$, an orientation $O$ is $T$-\textbf{odd on }$S$ if vertices of $V(S)$ have odd in-degree in $O(G)$ if and only if they belong to $T$. Note that in this definition, no constraints are imposed on the in-degree of the vertices in $V(G)\backslash V(S)$.\newline

%\noindent A \textbf{planar embedding} of a graph $G$ is a drawing of $G$ in the Euclidean plane so that the vertices of $G$ correspond to distinct points of the plane; each edge of $G$ corresponds to a simple curve that connects the ends of the edge but meets no other vertices; and each point of intersection of two such simple curves is an end of both edges. We call a graph \textbf{planar} if it has a planar embedding. A \textbf{plane} graph consists of a graph $G$ together with a planar embedding of $G$. Such a planar embedding $G'$ of $G$ can itself be regarded as an abstract graph and, as such, is isomorphic to $G$.\newline

\noindent A \textbf{planar embedding} is a drawing of a graph in the plane such that no edges cross each other. A graph that admits a planar embedding is called \textbf{planar}.

\section{A polynomial case on undirected graphs}\label{sec:Szegedy}

%Let $(G, T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP}. 
\noindent We present a polynomial time algorithm which solves Problem \ref{prob:AOP} whenever $G$ is planar and $|T| = |V(G)|-1$. We need additional definitions to describe the algorithm.\newline

\begin{comment}
\noindent A {\bf cut-decomposition} of $G$ is a set family $\cD$ on $G$ satisfying the following conditions :
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\emptyset \not \in \cD$
    \item $V \in \cD$
    \item $\{v\} \in \cD$ for each $v \in V$
    \item For each non singleton $S \in \cD$, there are $S_1, S_2 \in \cD$ partitioning $S$.
    \item For each $S,T \in \cD$, they are either disjoint or one is included in the other (ie. laminarity)
\end{enumerate}

\noindent We call two disjoint sets $S_1, S_2$ of a cut-decomposition \textbf{neighbouring} if their union is also in the decomposition. For a pair of neighbouring sets $S_1, S_2 \in \cD$, the set $\delta(S_1,S_2)$ is called a cut of $\cD$. We say that $\cD$ is an {\bf odd-cut decomposition} if all cuts occurring in $\cD$ are odd.
\noindent Szegedy established the following :

\begin{lemma}[Szegedy 2005 \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005}] \label{lem:cut-decomposition}
    Let $(G, T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP} such that $|T| = |V(G)|-1$\newline
    $G$ has an acyclic $T$-odd orientation if and only if it has an odd-cut decomposition.
\end{lemma}
\end{comment}

\noindent The \textbf{geometric dual} of a planar embedding of $G$ is the graph where the faces of this embedding are its vertex set and two faces are adjacents whenever they share an edge of $G$. A graph is said \textbf{factor-critical} if it has a perfect matching after removing any vertex of the graph. The following Lemma is a direct application of results due to Szegedy  \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005, szegedySymplecticSpacesEarDecomposition2006} and Oxley 2011 \cite{oxleyMatroidTheory2011}.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:dual}
    Let $G$ be a connected planar graph with a subset $T\subset V(G)$ such that $|T| = |V(G)|-1$.\newline
    $G$ has an acyclic $T$-odd orientation if and only if the geometric dual of any planar embedding of $G$ is factor critical.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
	Let $G$ be a connected planar graph with a subset $T\subset V(G)$ such that $|T| = |V(G|-1$.\newline
	\noindent The mentionned works of Szegedy and Oxley concern Matroid Theory. For our purpose, we don't need precise definitions since the proof is by direct applications of their results. Given a graph $H$, we denote by $M(H)$ the graphic matroid induced by $H$. The (algebraic) dual of a matroid $M$ will be denoted by $M^*$. For more detailled definition of matroids, we refer to Oxley's book \cite{oxleyMatroidTheory2011}.

	\noindent By corollary 2.11.6 in \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005}~:\newline

	\begin{minipage}{15cm}
		\centering
	    $G$ has an $T$-acyclic orientation if and only if $M^*(G)$ can be represented by an alternating projection matrix over some field of characteristic $2$.
	\end{minipage}
	\begin{minipage}{1.8cm}
		\centering
		(A)
	\end{minipage}\newline

	\noindent In \cite{oxleyMatroidTheory2011}, Oxley shown (Lemma 2.3.7) that~:\newline

	\begin{minipage}{15cm}
		\centering
		If $H^*$ is the geometric dual of a planar embedding of a connected graph $H$, then  $M(H^*)$ is isomorphic to $M^*(H)$.
	\end{minipage}
	\begin{minipage}{1.8cm}
		\centering
		(B)
	\end{minipage}\newline

	\noindent By (A) and (B), we get~:
	\begin{center}
		$G$ has an $T$-acyclic orientation if and only if $M(G^*)$ is representable over a field of characteristic $2$ by an alternating projection matrix.
	\end{center}

	\noindent To achieve the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:dual}, it is enough to apply Corollary 2.10.1 in \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005} claiming that~:
	\begin{center}
		A graph $H$ is factor-critical if and only if $M(H)$ can be represented by an alternating projection matrix over some field of characteristic $2$.
	\end{center}

\end{proof}

\noindent The following theorem follows :

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:planarAlgo}
    Let $(G, T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP} such that $G$ is planar and $|T| = |V(G)|-1$.\newline
    There exists a polynomial time algorithm to decide if $G$ has an acyclic $T$-odd orientation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
     Let $(G, T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP} such that $G$ is planar and $|T| = |V(G)|-1$. 

    \noindent By Lemma \ref{lem:dual} we know that $G$ has an acyclic $T$-odd orientation if and only if the dual graph $G^*$ of a plane embedding of $G$ is factor-critical. Thanks to Korte and Vygen (2007) \cite{korteCombinatorialOptimizationTheory2007}, we know there exists a polynomial time algorithm to decide if a graph has a perfect matching. Thus, we can decide in polynomial time if a graph is factor-critical by removing each vertex of the graph and checking if the resulting graph has a perfect matching. 

    \noindent Hence, to achieve the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:planarAlgo}, it is enough to check that getting $G^*$ is a polynomial task.  P. Rosenstiehl and R. E. Tarjan \cite{rosenstiehlRectilinearPlanarLayouts1986} give a linear time algorithm to compute a planar embedding as well as its geometric dual. 
\end{proof}

\noindent The next remark shows that the condition $\vert T\vert =\vert V(G)\vert -1$ is not as specific as it seems. Indeed, let $(G, T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP}. We define $G^T$ from $G$ by adding a vertex $v^T$ linked to every vertex of $V(G)-T$. As observed in \cite{szegedyApplicationsWeightedCombinatorial2005}, we have~ :

\begin{claim}\label{lem:addApex}
	Let $(G, T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP}. \newline
	$G$ has an acyclic $T$-odd orientation if and only if $G^T$ has an acyclic ($V(G)-v^T$)-odd orientation.\qed 
\end{claim}

\noindent This Claim allows us to extend our algorithm as follows~:

\begin{corollary}
    Let $(G, T)$ be an instance of Problem \ref{prob:AOP} such that $G$ is planar and there exists a planar embedding of $G$ such that $V(G)-T$ is included in a face.\newline
    There exists a polynomial time algorithm to decide if $G$ has an acyclic $T$-odd orientation.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
	We first aim to show $G^T$ is planar. Given a planar embedding of $G$ such that $V(G)-T$ is included in a face, we add the vertex $v^T$ in the face containing $V(G)-T$ and we link it to every vertex of $V(G)-T$. This operation builds a planar embedding of $G^T$, hence $G^T$ is planar. From Claim \ref{lem:addApex}, $G$ has an acyclic $T$-odd orientation if and only if $G^T$ has an acyclic $(V(G)-v^T)$-odd orientation, and notice it satisfies $|V(G)-v^T| = |V(G)| -1$. By applying Theorem \ref{thm:planarAlgo} on $G^T$, we obtain the desired result.
\end{proof}


\section{NP-completeness on partially directed graphs} \label{sec:partiallyDirectedGraphs}

\noindent In this section, we establish the NP-completeness of the problem of finding an acyclic $T$-odd orientation of a partially directed graph. To demonstrate this, we employ a reduction from the well-known NP-complete problem planar-3-SAT.

\begin{problem}[3-SAT] \label{prob:3SAT}
	\ \\
	\textbf{Instance:} A pair $(\cX, \cC)$ where $\cX = \{x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n\}$ is a set of boolean variables and $\cC = \{c^1, c^2, \ldots, c^m\}$ is a set of clauses. Each clause $c^j$ is a disjunction of three literals, where a literal is either a variable from $\cX$ or its negation. \newline
	\textbf{Question:} Is there an assignment of the variables in $\cX$ such that all clauses in $\cC$ are satisfied?
\end{problem}

\noindent For any 3-SAT instance, we can construct what is known as its incidence graph. Let $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{C})$ be a 3-SAT instance. Its \textbf{incidence graph} $\cB_{\cX, \cC}$ is a bipartite graph with vertices corresponding to the variables and clauses of $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{C})$. An edge exists between a variable vertex $x_i$ and a clause vertex $c^j$ if and only if $x_i$ appears in $c^j$. Thus, for any $j$, $d(c^j) = 3$, and for any $i$, $d(x_i)$ corresponds to the number of clauses containing the variable $x_i$.

\begin{problem}[Planar-3-SAT] \label{prob:planar3SAT}
	\ \\ 
	\textbf{Instance:} An instance $(\cX, \cC)$ of 3-SAT and a planar embedding of $\cB_{\cX, \cC}$.\newline
	\textbf{Question:} Is there an assignment of the variables in $\cX$ such that all clauses in $\cC$ are satisfied?
\end{problem}

\begin{theorem}[Lichtenstein 1982 \cite{lichtensteinPlanarFormulaeTheir1982}]\label{thm:P3SAT}
    Planar 3–SAT is NP–complete. \qed
\end{theorem}

\noindent Given a planar graph and one of its planar embedding, let $\sigma_{v}$ be a clockwise cyclic permutation of the neighbors of $v$ according to the planar embedding. Thus $\sigma_{v}(i)$ returns the i-est neighbor of $v$ and $\sigma_{v}^{-1}(u)$ returns the index of the neighbor $u$ in the cyclic order.
For example, in Figure \ref{fig:incidence_graph}, we could define $\sigma_{c^4} = (x_2, x_5, x_4)$ and $\sigma_{x_5} = (c^5, c^3, c^4, c^2)$.
