\section{Introduction}
%\redbf{To add: plagiarism, class redesign, stronger emphasis on code review and early intervention as code review in CS207 shows they don't always understand their code}

Students in introductory programming classes are generally able to write working code. However, they may not have a full understanding or comprehension of how the code works. %There are a variety of reasons why this might be the case. 
% There are a variety of reasons why students of introductory programming classes are able to submit passing code without having an understanding of how or why it works. 
%Fundamentally incorrect approaches might happen to pass the provided unit tests, students may continually tweak failing solutions or snippets of example code until they pass by fluke, or of course other less charitable explanations are available. 
In the experience of Lehtinen et al., around a third of their class of 125 students had difficulties explaining their code~\cite{Lehtinen21}. At this introductory stage, this can have serious long term consequences to the student's ability to become a skilled programmer, as these shaky foundations become increasingly strained by problems of greater scale and complexity. Code comprehension questions are an effective solution to this problem~\cite{schulte2010programComprehension}, forcing students to critically examine their own code, as well as challenging any incorrect assumptions.
%, and helping identify whether students wrote their code in the first place.
Unfortunately, the bespoke nature - manually creating questions based on a student's own code, makes for a process which is difficult to scale to larger classes.%, such as [REDACTED 2nd year programming class], the 200 student class that inspired this work. 

% Trimmed down in second draft
%Scalable alternatives such as banks of prepared questions have shortcomings, such as being shared between students. 

