\section{Mean-Field Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization}\label{sec:main-algo}



% \subsection{Proximal Policy Optimization}
% Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) \cite{schulman2017proximal} is an on-policy reinforcement learning algorithm with theoretical guarantees similar to trust region policy optimization \cite{schulman2015trust}, but is simpler to implement using first-order optimization. 
%
Motivated by Theorem~\ref{thm:performance-guarantees}, we present an algorithm to learn the optimal identical team policy. 
%
We build our algorithm based on the proximal policy optimization (PPO) framework due to its simplicity and effectiveness. 
%
While PPO has shown promising performance in cooperative tasks including mean-field control problems~\cite{yu2022surprising, cui2023learning}, its application in mixed competitive-collaborative scenarios is less studied, especially in the MFTG settings. 
%
In the sequel, we introduce our key contribution: the MFTG learning algorithm,
which we refer to as Mean-Field Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization (MF-MAPPO). 
%

We initialize two pairs of actor-critic networks, one for each team, deployed to learn the identical policy used by each team (see Figure~\ref{fig:actor-critic-network}).
%
Specifically, we introduce a \emph{minimally-informed critic} network by exploiting the shared mean-field information.
%
The key point to note here is that we only require the common information for the critic network in order to learn the value function (Proposition \ref{thm:minimal-critic}). 
%
Furthermore, the private information available to each agent only \emph{individually} enters the actor during training.
%
This results in neural networks that scale well with the number of agents.
%thereby mitigating the curse of dimensionality due to number of agents. 


