\subsubsection{Task: Lunar Lander}

At present, the Lunar Lander stands as a more challenging environment for the dNL-RL agents. As the previous control problem environment has demonstrated that SAC is the best performing algorithm, only SAC was tested on the Lunar Lander environments. In setting up the experiments, it was found that both the discretisation scheme and the initial high and low values for the state features had a significant impact. In the context of the Lunar Lander, a reward of $200$ indicates the lander successfully landed on the platform. The current binning scheme being deployed is $[3,3,3,3,3,3,-,-]$ for each feature, where ($-$) indicates no binning performed as it is a discrete/Boolean feature. In order to evaluate the consistency and performance of the dNLRLc and dNLRLnlc agents, multiple trials were conducted using different random seeds. We ran each experiment 5 times and the resulting policies were analysed. While only one representative policy is presented for each agent, any variations or discrepancies across the trials are discussed. The non-linear change was again a $sine$ transformation on the state feature angular orientation in space $\theta$ associated with the transformation predicate \textit{AngleSine}. 


