\begin{abstract}
% What is resolution cross-over
In adaptive bitrate streaming, resolution cross-over refers to the point on the convex hull where the encoding resolution should switch to achieve better quality.
% Accurately predicting the cross-over is crucial for streaming providers to determine the most suitable resolution at a given network bandwidth to achieve the best quality for a specific video content.
Accurate cross-over prediction is crucial for streaming providers to optimize resolution at given bandwidths.
% Existing work
Most existing works rely on objective Video Quality Metrics (VQM), particularly VMAF, to determine the resolution cross-over. However, these metrics have limitations in accurately predicting resolution cross-overs. Furthermore, widely used VQMs are often trained on subjective datasets collected using the Absolute Category Rating (ACR) methodologies, which we demonstrate introduces significant uncertainty and errors in resolution cross-over predictions.
% What we do
To address these problems, we first investigate different subjective methodologies and demonstrate that Pairwise Comparison (PC) achieves better cross-over accuracy than ACR. We then propose a novel metric, Resolution Cross-over Quality Loss (RCQL), to measure the quality loss caused by resolution cross-over errors. 
% To the best of our knowledge, RCQL is the first metric designed to evaluate the accuracy of resolution cross-overs. 
Furthermore, we collected a new subjective dataset (LSCO) focusing on live streaming scenarios and evaluated widely used VQMs, by benchmarking their resolution cross-over accuracy. 
% Furthermore, we collected a new subjective dataset, Live Sport Cross-Over (LSCO) and proposed a method to assess inter-subject inconsistency for incomplete PC data screening. Finally, we evaluated widely used VQMs, focusing on live streaming scenarios, by benchmarking their resolution cross-over accuracy using our dataset.

\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Adaptive Bitrate Streaming, Video Quality Metric (VQM), Quality of Experience (QoE)
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
% What is adaptive streaming
% \textbf{\textit{Motivation:}} 
Video streaming has experienced significant growth, driven by the widespread availability of high-speed Internet and the increasing use of mobile devices. It has become an essential part of daily life, serving purposes in entertainment, education, business, and more.
To accommodate varying bandwidth and device types among end-users, Adaptive BitRate streaming (ABR) methods~\cite{bentaleb_survey_2019} are widely used. In ABR, video content is encoded at multiple bitrate-resolution pairs, referred to as representations. These representations form a bitrate ladder~\cite{amirpour_pstr_2021}, allowing video quality to be dynamically adjusted based on the viewer's available bandwidth and device capabilities.

