\section{Observer screening}
\label{sec:obs_screen}
% motivation of dataset screening
% Observer screening is essential for collecting reliable QoE datasets. For ACR or DCR, well-established methods for screening observers have been developed~\cite{itu2021BT913, zhu2023zrec, tiotsop2024modeling}, whereas observer screening methods for PC data are less mature. Cohen’s Kappa coefficient~\cite{cohen1960coefficient} and RT distance~\cite{rogers1960computer} are two widely applied methods for PC data. Ak \textit{et al.} proposed a method to weight the RT distance based on the ambiguity of different pairs~\cite{ak2022rv}. However, all these methods are only applicable to complete PC data. Due to the use of active sampling, different observers rate different pairs, and the number of ratings per pair varies, making these methods unsuitable.
Observer screening is crucial for reliable QoE datasets. While well-established methods exist for ACR and DCR~\cite{itu2022BT910, zhu2023zrec}, screening for PC data is less developed. Methods like Cohen’s Kappa~\cite{cohen1960coefficient} and RT distance~\cite{rogers1960computer}, and Ak \textit{et al.}'s ambiguity-weighted RT distance~\cite{ak2022rv}, are limited to complete PC data.
% existing works: Cohen's Kappa and Rogers-Tanimoto distance
% We propose a new approach to compute inter-observer consistency for incomplete PCM collecte by PC with active sampling. This measure can help identify and filter outliers in the collected dataset. The proposed inter-observer consistency metric considers: 1) the ambiguity of each pair, 2) the agreement between each observer and others, and 3) the number of ratings for each pair.
We propose a new approach to compute inter-observer consistency for incomplete PCMs collected via active sampling. This metric helps identify and filter outliers by considering: 1) pair ambiguity, 2) observer agreement, and 3) the number of ratings per pair.
\subsection{Ambiguity}
For each observer $o_i$, assume that he/she voted on $N$ pairs of videos, with each pair denoted as $p_n$. The number of ratings (i.e., how many people voted on this pair) for $p_n$ is represented as $r_n$.
For pair $p_n$, the number of ratings for $A$ is denoted as $a_n$, the number of ratings for $B$ is denoted as $b_n$, and the number of ties is denoted as $t_n$. We know that:
\begin{equation}
\small
    a_n+b_n+t_n=r_n
\end{equation}
The ambiguity of pair $p_n$ can be calculate as follow:
\begin{equation}
\small
    Ambiguity_{p_n}=\frac{|a_n - b_n|}{r_n}=\frac{|a_n - b_n|}{a_n+b_n+t_n}
\end{equation}
It is straightforward to observe that the ambiguity of $p_n$ lies between $0$ and $1$:\textbf{Ambiguity = 0:} This pair is highly ambiguous, with equal votes for $A$ and $B$, or all participants voting for a tie. \textbf{Ambiguity = 1:} This pair is not ambiguous at all, with all participants voting exclusively for $A$ or $B$.
% \begin{itemize}
% \small
% \item \textbf{Ambiguity = 0:} This pair is highly ambiguous; there is an equal number of votes for $A$ and $B$, or all participants voted for a tie.
% \item \textbf{Ambiguity = 1:} This pair is not ambiguous at all; all participants voted exclusively for $A$ or $B$.
% \end{itemize}

\subsection{Agreement}
The agreement of observer $o_i$ with all other observers for $p_n$ can be computed with weighted agreement:
\begin{equation}
\small
    W_i(p_n) = \begin{cases}  P(A)_n=a_n/r_n & \text{if } v_{i} = A \\ P(B)_n=b_n/r_n & \text{if } v_i = B \\ P(T)_n=t_n/r_n & \text{if } v_i = T \end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $v_i$ is the vote of observer $o_i$ for video pair $p_n$.

\subsection{Inter-observer Consistency}
For a given observer $o_i$, the basic idea to compute his/her consistency is to calculate the average of the agreement between him/her and other observers across all the pairs he/she voted on. The higher this agreement, the more consistent this observer is with others.
% However, computing consistency solely based on agreement is not entirely fair, particularly for videos with high ambiguity. To address this, we weight the agreement based on the ambiguity:
% \begin{itemize}
% \item For video pairs with less ambiguity (easy pairs), larger weight is assigned to the agreement.
% \item For video pairs with more ambiguity (difficult pairs), smaller weight is assigned to the agreement.
% \end{itemize}
However, computing consistency solely based on agreement is not entirely fair, particularly for videos with high ambiguity. To address this, we weight the agreement based on ambiguity, assigning larger weights to less ambiguous pairs and smaller weights to more ambiguous pairs.
% Considering that in active sampling, different paris are voted with different number of observers, we should also consider the number of ratings per pair. The more a pair has been voted on, the higher it should be weighted. If a pair has been rated only once, it should not be considered in the consistency calculation.
In active sampling, pairs are rated by varying numbers of observers, so the number of ratings per pair must be considered. Pairs with more votes should be weighted higher, while those rated only once should be excluded from consistency calculations.
The inter-observer consistency $C_i$ can be calculated as:
% \begin{equation}
% \begin{aligned}
%     C_{i} &= \frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}(r_n-1)\times\frac{|a_n - b_n|}{r_n}\times W_i(p_n)}{\sum_{n=1}^{N}(r_n-1)} \\
%            &= \frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N} \left( \frac{(r_n - 1) \times |a_n - b_n|}{r_n} \times W_i(p_n) \right)}{\sum_{n=1}^{N} (r_n - 1)}
% \end{aligned}
% \end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\small
    C_{i} = \frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}(r_n-1)\times\frac{|a_n - b_n|}{r_n}\times W_i(p_n)}{\sum_{n=1}^{N}(r_n-1)}
\end{equation}
The larger $C_i$ is, the more consistent this observer is with others. To further validate the proposed method, we injected synthetic spammers, and detailed results can be found in Sec.~\ref{subsec:screening}.

