\paragraph{RepuNet sustains cooperation, effectively avoiding the `tragedy of the commons'.} Our key finding across both scenarios is that agents with RepuNet consistently exhibit cooperative behaviors across all five independent runs. In Scenario 1 (Figure \ref{fig:results-1}a), the evolution of agent participation rates highlights the proportion of agents who prioritize collective rationality and cooperate for better public goods. We observe that the proportion of cooperative agents with RepuNet steadily increased and remained high over time. In Scenario 2 (Figure \ref{fig:results-1}c), the evolution of investment success rates similarly reflects agents' willingness to prioritize long-term cooperation over short-term self-interest. We note that the proportion of cooperative agents with RepuNet gradually increased, eventually reaching nearly 100\% of agents adherence to the agreement to allocate gains for long-term cooperation. In contrast, cooperation in treatments without RepuNet exhibits a generally decreasing trend in both scenarios, ultimately leading to the `tragedy'. In summary, RepuNet effectively incentivizes agents to sustain cooperation, with most agents prioritizing collective rationality---even at the expense of personal interests (e.g. convenience in energy use)---to avoid the `tragedy of the commons'.

\paragraph{Cooperative behavior fosters good reputations, which in turn sustains long-term cooperation, creating a positive feedback loop in generative MASs.} As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:results-1}(b) and (d), our analysis reveals a strong positive correlation between cooperative behavior and reputation. In Scenario 1, agents who frequently participate in voluntary programs---cooperating to enhance public goods---tend to have significantly higher average reputations among others. In Scenario 2, agents who consistently allocate gains in accordance with prior agreements---prioritizing long-term cooperation over short-term self-interest---achieve higher average reputations. This finding demonstrates that cooperative behaviors directly enhance an agent's standing within the system. In turn, agents are motivated to maintain their reputations through appropriate actions, reinforcing long-term cooperation and perpetuating a self-sustaining cycle.

\paragraph{RepuNet drives network evolution by clustering cooperative agents with high reputations while isolating those with low reputations and defective behavior.} Figure \ref{fig:network} illustrates network dynamics across scenarios with and without RepuNet, using snapshots from one of five experimental runs. Initially, agents in both treatments are unconnected. Over time, in Scenario 1, agents without RepuNet form indiscriminate connections, interacting broadly without effective selection (Figure \ref{fig:network}a). In contrast, RepuNet fosters selective connections, clustering high-reputation agents while isolating defectors (Figure \ref{fig:network}b). Similarly, in Scenario 2, RepuNet gradually forms clusters of reputable agents, eventually uniting all agents with high reputations (Figure \ref{fig:network}d). This emergent structure---where cooperative agents cluster and defectors are excluded---prevents exploitation, stabilizes cooperation, and effectively avoid the `tragedy of the commons'.

