\paragraph{More Finding in Scenario 1.}\textit{Unlike humans~\cite{foster2004research}, generative agents tend to share positive gossip rather than negative.} Figure \ref{fig:results-3} illustrates this through two key analyses. First, our correlation study reveals that frequently discussed agents tend to gain higher reputations---an inverse pattern to typical human social dynamics~\cite{foster2004research}. To explore the reason, we conducted sentiment analysis using \texttt{twitter-roberta-base-sentiment}, classifying gossip as positive, neutral, or negative, with confidence scores. Sentiment values were mapped to $+1$ (positive), $0$ (neutral), and $-1$ (negative), then weighted by confidence and averaged. Results show that 90\% of gossip is positive, exemplified by statements like, ``I want to gossip about Isabella ... her perspective on the program as an empowering opportunity for our community is just remarkable.''

