\subsection{Ablation Study on the Reputation System}
\label{sec:4.3 ablation study}
We conducted an ablation study to answer the question of how effectively does each component of our RepuNet. Since RepuNet is an event-driven system reliant on direct social encounters within an embedded network, we focused on the impact of reputation at the agent level and the role of indirect gossip (Reputation and Gossip for short). Our ablation study included four conditions: (i) with RepuNet, (ii) without Gossip, (iii) without Reputation, and (iv) without RepuNet. In all cases, agents interacted within the network, and each experiment was repeated five times under identical settings.

As shown in Table \ref{tab:ablation study}, we averaged the performance over the final five rounds in both scenarios. RepuNet achieved the highest participation rate in Scenario 1 and the highest investment success rate in Scenario 2, confirming its effectiveness in preventing the `tragedy of the commons'. We also note that removing gossip led to a slight decrease in performance, suggesting that direct encounters have a greater impact than indirect gossip. However, removing the Reputation or the entire RepuNet caused a sharp drop in rates (below 20\%), suggesting that agents without RepuNet prioritized individual rationality, leading to non-cooperation and the `tragedy of the commons'.
