\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-0.2cm}
The study of reputation-based MASs has been a well-established area of AI for decades; on the other hand, generative AI technologies have recently captivated the world~\cite{ren2025ai}. In this paper, we bridge these two fields together to establish a reputation-based generative MAS, sustaining cooperation and avoiding the `tragedy of the commons'. Specifically, we proposed RepuNet, a novel reputation system operating at two levels: reputation dynamics at the agent level and network dynamics at the system level, both driven by direct encounters and indirect gossip. These dynamics, in turn, influence agents' behavior in future interactions. To evaluate RepuNet's effectiveness, we conducted two experimental scenarios: one assessing agents' cooperative behavior in a public goods dilemma and another measuring their trustworthiness in a sequential investment dilemma. Extensive results show that reputation-enabled agents not only self-regulate their behavior effectively but also select reputable partners for cooperation, thereby preventing the `tragedy of the commons'.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec: limitations}
Although we explored the effectiveness of reputation and emergent phenomena at both the agent and system levels, our study has certain limitations. Firstly, due to cost constraints, we did not examine the impact of multiple agents using different LLMs. Since LLMs are trained on distinct pre-trained datasets, agents powered by different models may develop varying reputations for the same behavior. Investigating this aspect could provide deeper insights into model-specific biases in reputation formation. Furthermore, our RepuNet focuses on individual reputation---both an agent's self-perception and its evaluation of others. However, in real-world scenarios, reputation is often more complex, as individuals can represent not only themselves but also their  groups they belong to. Consequently, individual reputation can shape group reputation, and conversely, group reputation can influence individual reputation. We envision that reputation-based generative MASs would be a fruitful avenue for future research, and these limitations present some promising directions.




