\subsection{State-of-the-Art for RL-based Multi-Task Learning and Their Limitations}
\label{Sec_Intro_SOTA}

In ANN domain, multi-task RL-based methods primarily explore \textit{replay-based} and \textit{architectural-based methods}~\cite{Minhas_SurveyNCL_arXiv24}. 
Specifically, replay-based methods revisit data from previous tasks~\cite{Replay1}\cite{Replay2}, hence incurring significant storage costs. 
Meanwhile, architectural-based methods dynamically expand the network, leading to high latency and uncontrolled model growth~\cite{Minhas_SurveyNCL_arXiv24}. 
While these methods have shown promising results, they often require substantial computational resources and struggle with efficiency and scalability~\cite{Minhas_SurveyNCL_arXiv24}.
Another method learns from a shared initialization, that helps the networks to quickly adapt to new tasks with minimal fine-tuning~\cite{finn2017modelagnosticmetalearningfastadaptation}. 
However, this method requires learning an effective initialization that can generalize well across multiple tasks, which is non-trivial. 
Currently, the prominent work in ANN domain employs DQN, which performs well in a single-task setting, but still struggles in multi-task settings, as shown by \circled{1} in Fig.~\ref{Fig_Observation}. 
This shows the difficulty of applying traditional RL models to more complex, multi-task scenarios.

In the SNN domain, several methods have been proposed.
First is DSQN~\cite{DSQN}, which provides an SNN equivalent to the DQN, and it only excels in a single-task setting. 
In multi-task settings, DSQN also suffers from low performance, as indicated by \circled{2} of Fig.~\ref{Fig_Observation}. 
Recently, MTSpark~\cite{MTSpark} outperforms other RL-based methods from both ANN and SNN domains achieving state-of-the-art results in tasks such as Atari games and image classification. 
Therefore, MTSpark represents the current state-of-the-art for multi-task RL-based methods.  

Despite performance improvements, most of the existing RL-based methods, including MTSpark, rely on \textit{``fixed task-switching interval''} during their training phase, thereby limiting their adaptability and efficiency in real-world dynamic environments. 
The reason is that, fixed task-switching intervals can lead to inefficient resource utilization if a task has already plateaued or if a task requires more time.           

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Associated Research Challenges}
\label{Sec_Intro_Challenges}

To solve the limitations posed by the fixed task-switching intervals and improve the effectiveness of multi-task learning, the following \textbf{key research challenges} need to be addressed.
%
\begin{itemize}
    \item The network model should include an adaptive mechanism to monitor task-specific learning progress and decide when to switch tasks. 
    This will help the network to have scalable multi-task learning capabilities.
    \item The task-switching strategy should avoid both premature and delayed transitions, hence ensuring each task receives adequate training time.
    %
    \item The network model should maintain its complexity level (e.g., network size) when performing the algorithms for multi-task learning. 
\end{itemize}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Our Novel Contributions}
\label{Sec_Intro_Novelty}

To address the associated research challenges, we propose \textbf{SwitchMT}, \textit{a novel methodology to enable adaptive task-switching in RL-based multi-task learning with SNNs for intelligent autonomous agents}; see an overview in Fig.~\ref{Fig_SwitchMT}. 
To achieve this, we employ following key contributions.
%
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Network architecture selection (Section~\ref{Sec_SwitchMT_Network}):} 
    It aims to choose the suitable network architecture that can facilitate RL-based multi-task learning.
    %
    \item \textbf{Training strategy with an adaptive task-switching policy (Section~\ref{Sec_SwitchMT_Training}):} 
    It aims to automatically adjust task-switching decisions based on real-time performance monitoring by leveraging both rewards and internal dynamics of network parameters, which lead to more effective training.
    %
    \item \textbf{Comprehensive evaluation (Section~\ref{Sec_Eval}):} 
    We perform extensive evaluation including three Atari games (i.e., Pong, Breakout, and Enduro), and compare the performance with several prominent and state-of-the-art works (i.e., DQN, DSQN, and MTSpark). 
\end{itemize}
%
\textbf{Key Results:}
We evaluate the SwitchMT methodology using a Python implementation, and then run it on the Nvidia GeForce RTX 4090-based multi-GPU machines. 
It achieves competitive scores across multiple Atari games (i.e., Pong: -8.6, Breakout: 4.8, and Enduro: 341.0) compared to state-of-the-art MTSpark (i.e., Pong: -5.4, Breakout: 0.6, and Enduro: 371.2) and human-level performance (i.e., Pong: -3, Breakout: 31, and Enduro: 368).
Moreover, SwitchMT also shows better learning progress over time than state-of-the-art. 

