\section{Evaluation Methodology}
\label{Sec_Eval}

We evaluate SwitchMT methodology through a Python-based implementation, and then run it on NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 multi-GPU machines. 
For comparisons partners, we consider MTSpark\_ADD~\cite{MTSpark}, DQN~\cite{Playing_Atari}, DSQN~\cite{DSQN}, DQN with dueling structure (DQN\_D), and DSQN with dueling structure (DSQN\_D).
DQN and DSQN represent the baseline methods, DQN\_D and DSQN\_D represent the enhanced baseline methods, while MTSpark\_ADD represents the state-of-the-art.
All these methods consider fixed task-switching interval (i.e., 25 episodes in each environment).

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\smallskip
\textbf{Environments:}
We train and test all investigated methods on three Atari games (i.e., Pong, Breakout, and Enduro) as the target tasks/environments, and utilize an action space of 18 possible actions. 
This approach ensures that we have consistency in the action space across all environments. 
The state observations are also represented as 210$\times$160$\times$3 RGB images, identical to the frames presented to human players.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\smallskip
\textbf{Experimental Settings:}
We train all networks with a static learning of 1e-4, a batch size of 512, and the Adam optimizer over 4 million frames in each environment. 
In line with the widely-used settings in Deep RL research, we apply a discount factor $\gamma$ of 0.99 to stabilize learning, use a replay buffer of size $2^{20}$ for each environment to store transitions for experience replay, and decay exploration from $\epsilon$ = 1.00 to $\epsilon$ = 0.10 over 1 million total frames. The details of the parameter settings are provided in Table \ref{table:exp_setup}.

