To prevent this class of attacks, organizations employ
  technical controls (\eg spam filters, attachment analyzers)
  complemented by cybersecurity awareness training~\cite{mengesContrastingSynergizingCISOs2024, hoUnderstandingEfficacyPhishing2025}. %-warranted by failures in deployed technical controls. 
%Despite organizational deployment of technological safeguards including  endpoint detection tools, spam filters, and attachment analyzers~\cite{aleroudPhishingEnvironmentsTechniques2017}, these technical controls alone prove insufficient as attackers continuously adapt their tactics, leading organizations to complement anti-phishing technology with cybersecurity awareness training programs~\cite{mengesContrastingSynergizingCISOs2024, hoUnderstandingEfficacyPhishing2025}.

Although these anti-phishing trainings are costly, their effectiveness is in question.
Organizational security awareness programs often focus on compliance metrics rather than meaningful behavioral change~\cite{haneyComplianceImpactTracing2023}.
Meanwhile, recent large-scale evidence suggests that current approaches have limited impact~\cite{hoUnderstandingEfficacyPhishing2025}.
%Recent research has documented the challenges organizations face in transforming from compliance-focused training completion rates to programs that actually impact workforce attitudes and behaviors. This transformation requires sustained organizational commitment, leadership support, and outcome-focused evaluation—elements that are often missing from traditional training approaches.
%A primary challenge in the use of security awareness training for preventing phising is the lack of a standardized difficulty assessment for emails complexity and phishing simulations.
To better evaluate the effectiveness of anti-phishing training, two questions arise.
First, are some kinds of training more effective than others?
Second, how does their effectiveness vary with the difficulty of the phishing lure?

% Email social engineering, or "phishing", is a longstanding cybersecurity hazard~\cite{kumaraguruProtectingPeoplePhishing2007}, and it remains one of the most prevalent cybersecurity threats to modern organizations~\cite{lainPhishingOrganizationsFindings2022}. According to multiple industry studies, it is the primary attack vector used to breach organizations~\cite{alkhalilPhishingAttacksRecent2021}. The Federal Bureau of Investigation reports that phishing is among the most detrimental frauds targeting enterprises~\cite{greitzerExperimentalInvestigationTechnical2021}. Such attacks critically impact organizations by damaging their reputations and their financial bottom lines. Although organizations have technological safeguards, such as spam detectors and attachment analysis~\cite{aleroudPhishingEnvironmentsTechniques2017}, attackers continue to evade them. Therefore, organizations complement their anti-phishing technology~\cite{mengesContrastingSynergizingCISOs2024} with cybersecurity training programs~\cite{hoUnderstandingEfficacyPhishing2025}. However, evaluating the efficacy of awareness training continues to pose significant challenges~\cite{reinheimerInvestigationPhishingAwareness2020}, with recent large-scale studies revealing surprisingly limited effectiveness of current approaches~\cite{hoUnderstandingEfficacyPhishing2025}.
% \KC{We can merge the above 2 paragraphs to have a concise context}

In this paper, we report the results of a measurement study to address these questions.
We situate the study in an operational environment~\cite{abroshanPhishingHappensTechnology2021}, set at a US-based international fintech firm with \textbf{12,511 employees} across multiple business units.
We compared three experimental conditions: a control group (no training), traditional lecture-based training, and lectures augmented with interactive phishing exercises.
Subjects in each condition received lures of varying complexity, which we assigned difficulty levels using the NIST Phish Scale.
This study design enables rigorous evaluation of training effectiveness across standardized difficulty levels on a large population, improving on previous works that had either been small-scale or lacking in systematic difficulty assessment~\cite{sumnerExaminingFactorsImpacting2022, schoniYouKnowWhat2024}.

Our findings were unfavorable for the conventional anti-phishing trainings available from cybersecurity training vendors.
Neither mode of training produced statistically significant improvements in phish click reduction (average: 10.4\%) or phish reporting behavior (average: 9.6\%).
However, the NIST Phish Scale did predict user behavior, with phishing lure difficulty having a highly significant effect on click-through rates (F(2, 12086) = 41.415, p $<$ 0.001, $\eta^2$ = 0.007).
Thus our study shows that, while the NIST Phish Scale provides a means of measuring the difficulty of different lures, the trainings we applied did not help subjects perform better on those lures.

We summarize our main contributions below:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Large-Scale Validation of the NIST Phish Scale.} We provide the most comprehensive empirical validation of the NIST Phish Scale to date, demonstrating its predictive utility across 12,511 participants in an operational environment, showing strong correlation between difficulty ratings and user susceptibility.

\item \textbf{Realistic Assessment of Training Effectiveness.}
Our results challenge optimistic assumptions about the effectiveness of phishing training.
We found no statistically significant impact of training on either click rates or reporting behavior. Although these findings align with recent large-scale studies from the healthcare~\cite{hoUnderstandingEfficacyPhishing2025} and banking sectors~\cite{doingAnalysisPhishingReporting2024}, our study addresses explicit calls from the research community for large-scale ecologically valid studies combining standardized assessments of difficulty inside of an operational context~\cite{franzWeShouldConsider2021,abroshanPhishingHappensTechnology2021}.
\end{enumerate}

\myparagraph{Significance}
Our findings have important implications for researchers, organizations, and regulators.
For the research community, we provide empirical assessment of training effectiveness using rigorous methodology and standardized difficulty measures.
For organizations, our results suggest the need for realistic expectations about training outcomes and highlight the importance of complementing awareness programs with robust technical controls rather than relying primarily on human-centered defenses.
Our validation of the NIST Phish Scale's predictive utility enables organizations to use this tool for benchmarking the difficulty of their phishing simulations.
For regulators, our work contributes to the growing body of evidence that organizational cybersecurity strategy should emphasize technical defenses~\cite{alkhalilPhishingAttacksRecent2021} rather than placing the burden on humans --- requiring anti-phishing training is costly and has minimal effect.

\section{Background and Related Work}

In a phishing attack,
  attackers send messages (\textit{phishing lures})
  to targets whom they wish to persuade to act against their own interests.
Attackers seek results such as sensitive information, account credentials, or the ability to control the victim's machine~\cite{alkhalilPhishingAttacksRecent2021, ferreiraPersuasionHowPhishing2019}. 
Modern phishing campaigns target individuals and organizations across multiple attack vectors, applying conventional email phishing as well as SMS-based attacks (``smishing'') and voice-based deception (``vishing'')~\cite{dasSoKComprehensiveReexamination2020, desoldaHumanFactorsPhishing2022}.
Recent attacks have begun to incorporate Generative AI tools to produce tailored and realistic messages.
The severity, persistence, and increasing sophistication of these attacks has established phishing detection and prevention as a critical focus within both academic cybersecurity research and industry practice~\cite{aleroudPhishingEnvironmentsTechniques2017, abroshanPhishingHappensTechnology2021}.

In this section, we
  summarize current defenses against phishing (\cref{subsec:phishing-defenses}),
  discuss metrics for evaluating phishing effectiveness (\cref{subsec:measuring-effectiveness}), and
  review prior empirical studies (\cref{subsec:empirical-studies}) and knowledge gaps (\cref{sec:Background-Gaps}).

\subsection{Phishing Defenses}
\label{subsec:phishing-defenses}

Phishing poses substantial risks to organizations, who therefore erect multiple layers of defenses against these attacks.
We distinguish automated defenses from human-focused ones.

\subsubsection{Automated Defenses}
\label{subsec:tech-defenses}

Organizations employ a range of technological defenses to mitigate phishing risks. These include spam filters, attachment analyzers, and URL checkers~\cite{aassalSpearsShieldsAre2019, aleroudPhishingEnvironmentsTechniques2017, alkhalilPhishingAttacksRecent2021}.
More recently, machine learning approaches have been applied to detect phishing content.
Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) models and optimized Random Forest algorithms~\cite{bikkuOptimizedMachineLearning2022} have demonstrated high accuracy in detecting phishing attempts.
As with everything in cybersecurity, phishing is a cat-and-mouse game.
Adversaries have begun to leverage artificial intelligence to create more convincing lures.
Large language models (LLMs) can generate personalized and context-aware phishing emails that are difficult for traditional filters to detect~\cite{mengesContrastingSynergizingCISOs2024}.
Techniques like typosquatting, domain spoofing, and watering hole attacks exacerbate this issue~\cite{alkhalilPhishingAttacksRecent2021, aleroudPhishingEnvironmentsTechniques2017}.

\subsubsection{Human-Focused Defenses: Training and Awareness}
\label{subsec:human-focus}

Defending against phishing is understood not as solely a technical problem, but rather as a socio-technical one~\cite{abroshanPhishingHappensTechnology2021, desoldaHumanFactorsPhishing2022}.
Spear phishing and emerging phishing attacks present sophisticated threats that overcome automated defenses~\cite{butaviciusBreachingHumanFirewall2015, burnsSpearPhishingBarrel2019}, so human awareness of phishing remains necessary to successful defense~\cite{downsDecisionStrategiesSusceptibility2006, canfieldBetterWatchOut2019}.

There are many approaches to training humans to be resilient to phishing attacks.
Methods range from lecture-style training involving passive videos and readings~\cite{marshallExploringEvidenceEmail2024} to simulation feedback providing immediate feedback after interaction with phishing simulations~\cite{carellaImpactSecurityAwareness2017, caputoGoingSpearPhishing2014}. 
Interactive training uses gamification or scenario-based learning to engage participants~\cite{bitrianGamificationWorkforceTraining2024, hartRiskioSeriousGame2020}, while just-in-time training alerts users in real time during risky behavior~\cite{franklinOptimisingNudgesBoosts2019}.

Anti-phishing trainings are frequently included within the cybersecurity awareness trainings ubiquitous in organizations.
%Cybersecurity awareness training has emerged as a necessary complement.
Legal mandates drive the widespread adoption of such training programs, with
  HIPAA requiring security awareness training for healthcare organizations~\cite{hipaa-security-rule},
  GDPR encouraging staff training on cybersecurity practices~\cite{gdpr-article39},
  and
  PCI DSS mandating training for those handling cardholder data~\cite{pci-dss}. 
Industry standards and frameworks further reinforce these requirements, with
  ISO 27001 calling for human resource security controls including awareness programs~\cite{iso-27001}
  and
  the NIST Cybersecurity Framework advising phishing simulations under the ``Identify'' function~\cite{nist-csf}.


\subsection{Measuring Effectiveness of Phishing Training}
\label{subsec:measuring-effectiveness} 

The effectiveness of phishing training can be assessed based on how resilient a target becomes to phishing lures.
\cref{sec:Background-PhishScale} describes how lures can be categorized by their complexity, and then~\cref{subsec:training-metrics} gives the standard metrics for target resilience.

\subsubsection{Measuring Training Lure Difficulty}
\label{sec:Background-PhishScale}
