To assess phishing lure difficulty, the NIST Phish Scale categorizes them along two dimensions as depicted in~\cref{tab:phish-scale-matrix}.
\textit{Phishing Cues} represent observable errors or inconsistencies in the email that might alert users to its fraudulent nature, such as spelling mistakes, suspicious URLs, or formatting irregularities~\cite{canhamNotAllVictims2024}. \textit{Premise Alignment} measures the relevance and familiarity of the email's subject matter to the recipient's typical email environment and organizational context~\cite{antoniouSystematicMethodExecute2015}. A high alignment, messages that closely resemble emails users receive daily, combined with few obvious cues yields the most deceptive and challenging-to-detect phishing emails~\cite{butaviciusBreachingHumanFirewall2015}. The scale allows organizations to benchmark lure complexity and contextualize simulation outcomes, though it requires sufficiently large sample sizes for reliable use and consistent application~\cite{canhamPhishDerbyShoring2022}.


\subsubsection{Training Outcome Metrics}
\label{subsec:training-metrics}

The effectiveness of anti-phishing training is commonly measured in three ways:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Open Rate} captures the percentage of emails opened, though this measurement can be deceptive since different email clients and security settings can affect whether an email is recorded as opened~\cite{antoniouSystematicMethodExecute2015} we therefore largely ignore this measure. 
\item \textit{Click-Through Rate (CTR)} measures the percentage of recipients who click on malicious links, serving as a primary indicator of phishing susceptibility~\cite{carellaImpactSecurityAwareness2017, caputoGoingSpearPhishing2014}. 
\item \textit{Reporting Rate} tracks phishing incidents reported to security teams, representing users' proactive security behaviors and ability to recognize suspicious content~\cite{burnsSpearPhishingBarrel2019, debonaRealWorldStudy2020}.
\end{itemize}

