The results demonstrate that fairness-optimized runs, which employ the Jain, Gini, or Atkinson indices, consistently achieve low inequity values (ranging from approximately 0.89\% to 4.77\%), indicating that the allocation closely approximates the ideal fair distribution. In contrast, runs driven by revenue maximization exhibit significantly higher inequity levels (from around 21.25\% to 35.57\%), highlighting the inherent trade-off between economic performance and equitable resource distribution. These findings confirm that the optimal hyperparameter settings (\(\alpha = 25\) for Jain and Atkinson, and \(\alpha = 10\) for Gini) are effective in enhancing the sensitivity of the fairness metrics, ensuring that even subtle deviations from an equal allocation are captured. Overall, the table underscores the robustness of the fairness-oriented optimization in achieving balanced service allocation under uniform capacity constraints.
