\section{Introduction}
\label{sec1}
Dynamic Low-Rank Approximation (DLRA) of large-scale matrices has demonstrated significant value in various fields, including image compressing, machine learning, natural language processing, bioinformatics, and mathematical finance. In this context, we concentrate on the efficient solution of low-rank approximation solutions for large-scale matrix differential equations.

In this work, we are interested in solving a class of semilinear stiff matrix equations, as follows:
\begin{equation*}
	\dot{X}(t)=AX(t)+X(t)A^{T}+F(t,X(t)),
	\quad X(t_{0})=X_{0},
\end{equation*}
where $X(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n},F:[t_{0},\infty)\times\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ is nonlinear, and $A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ is time-independent. In particular, we consider here problems stemming from PDEs. Therefore, the matrix A is typically the spatial discretization of a differential operator, which generates a stiff term. Instead, the nonlinear term $F$ is assumed to be nonstiff. The objective of this paper is to find a low-rank approximation for the solution of the given matrix differential equation.

In recent years, efficient numerical solutions for large-scale stiff matrix differential equations have garnered significant attention in the fields of physics, chemistry, and engineering. The primary challenge lies in balancing computational efficiency with robustness against stiffness and high-dimensional nonlinearity. The traditional Lie-Trotter splitting method addresses the linear part analytically by separating the stiff and nonstiff terms and employing an exponential integrator  \cite{hairer1993sp,ostermann2019convergence}. However, the low-rank approximation of the nonlinear terms still depends on the projective splitting integrator \cite{lubich2014projector}, which faces challenges related to sensitivity to small singular values and bottlenecks in computational efficiency \cite{kieri2016discretized}. Although Dynamic Low Rank Approximation (DLRA) significantly reduces dimensionality through manifold constraints \cite{koch2007dynamical}, its classical implementation is constrained by explicit step-size limitations and fixed rank assumptions \cite{kieri2019projection}. Subsequent improvements, such as the adaptive BUG method \cite{ceruti2022rank,carrel2024randomised}, enhance the stability but have limited scalability for high-dimensional nonlinear problems. The emergence of randomized numerical linear algebra introduces new ideas for low-rank decomposition, including the randomized singular value decomposition (RSVD) proposed by Halko et al. \cite{halko2011finding} and the generalized Nyström method developed by Tropp et al. \cite{tropp2017practical}. These methods accelerate matrix approximation through sketching techniques. Additionally, \cite{carrel2024randomised} extend these approaches to dynamic scenarios by developing the dynamic randomized singular value decomposition (DRSVD) and dynamic generalized Nyström (DGN) methods, which demonstrate low variance and high parallelism in time-dependent systems \cite{lam2024randomized}. However, most existing studies concentrate on static or weakly stiff scenarios, and the deep integration of the splitting framework with randomized dynamic low-rank (DLR) methods has not been thoroughly investigated.

In this work, we propose a novel method for efficiently obtaining low-rank approximate solutions to stiff matrix differential equations,  embedding randomized DLRA into a splitting strategy. To address the difficulties arising from the coupling of stiffness and nonlinearity, we first decompose the matrix differential equation into its stiff part $AX+XA^{T}$ and nonstiff, nonlinear components $F$ \cite{ostermann2019convergence}. The linear component can be solved exactly and efficiently by exponential integrators \cite{einkemmer2015overcoming}, and the rank of this solution is preserved. The nonlinear component is then stochastically approximated using the Dynamical rangefinder, which is integrated with the post-processing step to maintain the low-rank structure and achieve a low-rank approximation of the solution \cite{carrel2024randomised}. This integration not only inherits the robustness of the splitting method in handling stiffness \cite{sutti2024implicit} but also enhances the computational efficiency of the nonlinear terms through randomization techniques. Furthermore, it supports adaptive rank adjustment to balance accuracy and computational cost, providing a novel solving paradigm for high-dimensional stiff systems that offers both high efficiency and stability.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In \Cref{sec2}, we provide a detailed introduction to the collaborative solving framework based on splitting. First, we establish the Lie-Trotter and Strang splitting formats. Next, we propose the Dynamic Range Finder (DRSVD/DGN) with an adaptive rank correction mechanism, which achieves stable decoupling of the subproblems through the principle of variational splitting on the projective manifold. In \Cref{sec3}, we validate the theoretical results through a series of numerical experiments, demonstrating their excellent numerical performance. In \Cref{sec4}, we validate the numerical stability of our method by simulating the approximate solution to the Allen-Cahn equation. Finally, the paper closes with conclusions in \Cref{sec5}.

\section{Randomized Split Low-rank Integrator with Exponential Kernels}\label{sec2}

In this work, we consider the numerical solutions of the following class of matrix differential equations:
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.1}
	\dot{X}(t)=AX(t)+X(t)A^{T}+F(t,X(t)),
	\quad X(t_{0})=X_{0},
\end{equation}
where $X(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n},F:[t_{0},\infty)\times\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}\to\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ is nonlinear,and $A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ is time-independent. In this discussion, we focus on problems arising from partial differential equations (PDEs). Therefore, the matrix $A$ typically represents a spatially discretized form of the differential operator, which introduces a stiff term. In contrast, the nonlinear term $F$ is assumed to be nonstiff. By utilizing the variation-of-constants formula \cite{collegari2018linear}, the full-rank exact solution of eq.~\eqref{eq2.1} can be expressed as follows:
\begin{equation*}
	X(t)=\mathrm{e}^{(t-t_0)A}X(t_0)\mathrm{e}^{(t-t_0)A^*}+\int_{t_0}^t\mathrm{e}^{(t-s)A}F(s,X(s))\mathrm{e}^{(t-s)A^*}\mathrm{d}s.
\end{equation*}
for $t_{0}\leq t\leq T$.

However, the stiffness of eq.~\eqref{eq2.1} limits the applicability of most standard numerical integrators. Explicit methods must satisfy a CFL-like condition and require the use of tiny step sizes \cite{banerjee2017cfl}, while implicit methods can result in significant computational costs. Consequently, we propose an efficient numerical integrator based on the splitting method.

\subsection{Splitting into two subproblems}\label{sec2.1}
Taking the structure of the equation into full account, we split \cref{eq2.1} into two terms: the stiff linear component and the nonstiff nonlinearity. This division results in the following two subproblems:
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.2}
	\dot{M}(t)=AM(t)+M(t)A^{T},\quad\quad M(t_{0})=M_{0},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.3}
	\dot{N}(t)=F(t,N(t)),\quad\quad N(t_0)=N_0.
\end{equation}
where $t\in[t_{0},T]$. The principal advantage of this splitting strategy stems from its ability to precisely decouple stiffness dynamics: the stiff component is entirely encapsulated within the first subproblem \cref{eq2.2}, whereas the second subproblem  \cref{eq2.3} is a nonstiff ordinary differential equation. This separation achieves dual computational benefits: (i) it expands the admissible choices of numerical integrators for the nonstiff subsystem, and (ii) enables systematic derivation of splitting schemes with arbitrary convergence orders by reconfiguring the operator composition in \cref{eq2.1}. This is illustrated by the Lie-Trotter (first-order) and Strang (second-order) splitting frameworks.

We denote $\Phi_\tau^A(M_0)$ as the exact solution of \cref{eq2.2} at $t_0+\tau$ when the initial value is $M_{0}$ i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.4}
	\Phi_\tau^A(M_{0})=M(t_0+\tau)=\mathrm{e}^{\tau A}M_{0}\mathrm{e}^{\tau A^{T}}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, let $\Phi_\tau^F(N_0)$ represents the exact solution of \cref{eq2.3} when the initial value is $N_{0}$, that is $\Phi_{\tau}^{F}(N_{0})=N(t_{0}+\tau)$.

The numerical evaluation of \cref{eq2.4} can be computationally expensive if the dimension $m$ and $n$ is large. Therefore, we propose addressing the solution to the stiff subproblem using a low-rank flow. Note that the differential equation \cref{eq2.4} is rank preserving, i.e, the rank of the solution $M(t)$ does not depend on time (see \cite{helmke2012optimization}), providing a theoretical basis for the development of low-rank splitting algorithms. In this paper, we introduce a low-rank numerical method based on the Lie-Trotter and Strang splitting frameworks. This approach significantly reduces computational costs by integrating dynamic low-rank approximation (see in subsection \ref{sec2.2} and \ref{sec2.3}) with an efficient matrix exponent computation. We next determine the low-rank solutions of \cref{eq2.2} and \cref{eq2.3}.

\textbf{The Lie-Trotter splitting scheme}\quad We propose a Lie-Trotter splitting-based numerical integrator for \cref{eq2.2}, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.5}
	\mathcal{L}_\tau:=\Phi_\tau^A\circ\Phi_\tau^F.
\end{equation}
With this format, we can obtain an approximation $X_{\tau}^{L}$ of the solution $X(t)$ to \cref{eq2.1} at $t = t_0+\tau$, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
	X_{\tau}^{L}=\mathcal{L}_\tau(X_{0})=\Phi_\tau^A\circ\Phi_\tau^F(X_{0}).
\end{equation*}
where $X_{0} = N_{0}$.

First, apply the Dynamic low-rank approximation method to the non-stiff subproblem \cref{eq2.3} (see details in subsections \ref{sec2.2} and \ref{sec2.3}) to obtain a rank-$r$ approximate solution $N_{\tau}=USV^{T}$ to \cref{eq2.3}, where $U\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}$, $V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$ have orthonormal columns, $S\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}(r\ll\min\{m,n\})$ is a diagonal matrix. Subsequently, using this approximation for the initial data $N_{\tau}$, we obtain a rank-$r$ approximation $M_{\tau}$ to the exact solution $M(t_0+\tau)=\Phi_\tau^A(N_{\tau})$ by
\begin{equation*}
	M_{\tau}=e^{\tau A}N_{\tau}e^{\tau A^{T}}=e^{\tau A}USV^{T}e^{\tau A^{T}}.
\end{equation*}
For the matrix exponential-vector multiplications $e^{\tau A}U$ and $e^{\tau A}V$,  where $U\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}$, $V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}(r\ll\min\{m,n\})$, several high-performance algorithms exist, such as Taylor series expansion \cite{AlMohyHigham2011}, Leja point-based interpolation \cite{Caliari2016}, and Krylov subspace approximations \cite{guttel2013rational, saad1992analysis}. In order to maintain orthogonality, we will compute these actions here with the Leja method. The matrices $e^{\tau A}U$ and $e^{\tau A}V$ can undergo QR decomposition to enforce orthogonality. Specifically, we factorize $e^{\tau A}U=\widetilde{U}R$ where $\widetilde{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}$ has orthonormal columns and $R\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}$ is upper triangular. Analogously, the decomposition $e^{\tau A}V=\widetilde{V}P$ yields another orthonormal basis $\widetilde{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}$ with upper triangular $P\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}$. To preserve the rank-$r$ structure, the core matrix is updated via $\widetilde{S}=RSP^\top$, resulting in the low-rank approximation of the solution to \cref{eq2.2} $M_\tau=\widetilde{U}\widetilde{S}\widetilde{V}^\top$. 

From this, we can obtain the following steps for the Lie-Trotter splitting calculation:
\begin{itemize}
	\item[]\textbf{1. Full-step non-stiff integration:} Apply the DRSVD/DGN method (see in subsections \ref{sec2.2} and \ref{sec2.3}) to solve the nonstiff subproblem over a full step $\tau$ yielding the initial low-rank approximation $N_{\tau}=USV^{T}$.

	\item[]\textbf{2. Full-step stiff integration:} Obtain the final solution ${M}_{\tau}$ by integrating the stiff term over a time step $\tau$, i.e.,
	\begin{equation*}
		M_\tau=e^{\tau A}US\left(e^{\tau A}V\right)^\top.
	\end{equation*}
\end{itemize}

Although the method is computationally efficient, its first-order accuracy (local error $O(h^2)$) may lead to significant accumulation of errors in long-term simulations. To improve the accuracy, the Strang splitting generalizes the Lie-Trotter format to second-order accuracy by symmetrizing the time-stepping strategy.


\textbf{The Strang splitting scheme}\quad A second-order method, known as the Strang splitting method, is given by the symmetry formula:
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.6}
	S_{\tau}:=\Phi_{\tau/2}^{A}\circ\Phi_{\tau}^{F}\circ\Phi_{\tau/2}^{A}.
\end{equation}
Similarly, we can obtain an approximate $X_{\tau}^{S}$ for $X(t_0+\tau)$ once more, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
	X_{\tau}^{S}=S_\tau(X_{0})=\Phi_{\tau/2}^A\circ\Phi_\tau^F\circ\Phi_{\tau/2}^A(X_{0}).
\end{equation*}
where $X_{0} = M_{0}$.

To further enhance accuracy through symmetrization, the initial data $M_0$ is typically represented in a rank-$r$ $(r\ll\min\{m,n\})$ factorized form via a truncated singular value decomposition (SVD), i.e., $M_0=USV^\top$, where $U\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}$, $V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}\text{ (column-orthogonal)}$, $S\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}\text{ (diagonal)}.$The Strang splitting scheme proceeds as follows:

\begin{itemize}
	\item[]\textbf{1. Half-step stiff evolution:} Compute the intermediate solution $\tilde{N}_{\tau/2}$ by integrating the stiff term over half a time step $\tau/2$, i.e.,
\begin{equation*}
	\widetilde{N}_{\tau/2}=e^{\frac{\tau}{2}\mathcal{A}}US\left(e^{\frac{\tau}{2}\mathcal{A}}V\right)^\top.
\end{equation*}
	\item[]\textbf{2. Full-step non-stiff integration:} Apply the DRSVD/DGN method (see details in subsections \ref{sec2.2} and \ref{sec2.3}) to solve the nonstiff subproblem over a full step $\tau$ yielding the updated low-rank approximation $\tilde{N}_{\tau}$.

	\item[]\textbf{3. Remaining half-step stiff evolution:} Propagate $\tilde{N}_{\tau}$ through the stiff term for another half step $\tau/2$ to obtain the final solution $\tilde{M}_{\tau}$.
\end{itemize}
Since DRSVD/DGN maintains the approximate solution at a fixed rank $r$, and the stiff evolution does not increase this rank, the output of each step of the Strang splittingis consistently a rank-$r$ matrix. The global error order is elevated to $O(\tau^2) $ at the expense of only an additional half-step of stiffness computation. 

\subsection{The dynamical randomised SVD}\label{sec2.2} The low-rank approximation $N_{\tau}$  is by an integrator, which can significantly speed up computations in large-scale setting. In the following, we concentrate on the time-dependent subproblem \cref{eq2.2}
\begin{equation*}
	\dot{N}(t)=F(t,N(t)),\quad\quad N(t_0)=N_0.
\end{equation*}
and we assume that the initial values are accurately represented by the low-rank matrix $N_0\approx X_0=U_0S_0V_0^T\in\mathcal{M}_r$. We first discretize the continuous time by defining the unit time step as $h$, where $[t_{0},T]=[t_{0},t_{0}+h]\bigcup[t_{0}+h,t_{0}+2h]\bigcup\cdots\bigcup[t_{0}+(n-1)h,t_{0}+nh]$, $h=\frac{T-t_0}{n}$. To thoroughly investigate the initial response characteristics of the system, this paper focuses on analyzing the physical processes within the first time step $[t_0,t_0+h]$. The dynamic range finder (see in \ref{sec2.4}), which identifies the orthogonal matrix $Q_h$ such that $N(t_0 + h) \approx Q_h Q_h^T N(t_0 + h)$. This step effectively captures the structure of the lower-rank subspaces of $N(t)$ within the first time interval. In order to ensure that the matrix $Q$ includes foundational information along with dynamically evolving orthogonal bases, we combine the initial basis \(U_0\) with the dynamic basis \(Q_h\) to construct the extended orthogonal basis \(Q\), defined as 
\begin{equation*}
	Q = \mathrm{orth}([U_0, Q_h]).
\end{equation*}
This leads to a novel approximation $N(t) \approx Q Q^T N(t)$  for all $t\in(t_0,t_0+h)$, which aims to reduce computational complexity by projecting the original high-dimensional nonlinear equations into the lower-dimensional subspace defined by the matrix $Q$. The projection coefficient matrix $C(t)$ is defined below to derive $n \times (2r+p)$ the differential equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.7}
	\dot{C}(t) = F(t,QC(t)^T)^TQ, \quad C(t_0) = N_0^TQ.
\end{equation}
Solving the above equations using numerical integration methods (e.g., the Runge-Kutta method) yields \(C(t_0 + h)\). This is immediately followed by a singular value decomposition (SVD) of the  \(C(t_0 + h)^T\): 
\begin{equation*}
	\tilde{U}_h\Sigma_hV_h^T=\mathrm{svd}(C(t_0+h)^T).
\end{equation*}
Then, we recover the original spatial approximation: \(N(t_0 + h) \approx N_1 = Q \tilde{U}_h \Sigma_h V_h^T\). Finally, the resulting low-rank decomposition will be truncated to the target rank $N_1=\mathcal{T}_r(Q\widetilde{U}_h\Sigma_hV_h^T)$. The computational complexity can be significantly reduced through the use of low-rank decomposition techniques. In particular, as established in \cite[Lemma 3.1]{carrel2024randomised}, when the system meets the regularity condition $\operatorname{Range}(N(t))\subset\operatorname{Range}([U_0,Q_h])=\operatorname{Range}(Q)$, for all $t\in[t_0,t_0+h].$ The \cref{eq2.7} can achieve exact reconstruction of the solution under this condition.

Dynamic randomised SVD combines stochastic projection with dynamic evolution by estimating the column space through a dynamic rangefinder, combined with post-processing and truncated SVD in the row space, enabling efficient low-rank approximation of time-dependent matrix differential equations.

Further, based on the adaptive dynamic rangefinder (see in \ref{sec2.5}), we can easily obtain the adaptive dynamic singular value decomposition (ADRSVD) algorithm. Performing the adaptive dynamic rangefinder to adaptively determine the range and then truncate the singular value decomposition to the target tolerance rather than the target rank.

% \begin{algorithm}
% 	\caption{Dynamical Randomised SVD (DRSVD)} %\cite[Algorithm~3]{carrel2024randomised}}
% 	\label{alg:drsvd}

\begin{algorithm}
	\caption{Dynamical Randomised SVD (DRSVD)} %\cite[Algorithm~3]{carrel2024randomised}}
\label{alg:drsvd}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
	\REQUIRE Initial value $ N_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n} $ and $N_{0}\approx U_{0}S_{0}V_{0}^{T}\in\mathcal{M}_{r}$, step-size $ h > 0 $, target rank $ r > 0 $, oversampling $ p \geq 0 $, number of power iterations $ q \geq 0 $.

	\STATE Compute $Q_h = \text{DynamicalRangefinder}(N_0, h, r, p, q)$.

	\STATE Basis augmentation: $Q = \text{orth}([U_0, Q_h])$.

	\STATE Compute $ C(t_0+h) $ by solving the reduced $ n \times (2r + p) $ differential equation on subspace
	\begin{align*}
		\dot{C}(t) = F(t,QC(t)^T)^T Q, \quad C(t_0) = N_0^T Q.
	\end{align*}

	\STATE Conduct a SVD of $ C(t_0+h)^T = \tilde{U}_{h} \Sigma_h V_h^T $ and $N(t_{0}+h)=Q\tilde{U}_{h}\Sigma_{h}V_{h}^{T}$.
	\STATE Truncate the SVD to the target rank. $U_{1}\Sigma_{1}V_{1}^{T}=\mathcal{T}_{r}(Q\widetilde{U}_{h}\Sigma_{h}V_{h}^{T})$.
	\RETURN {$N_1 = U_1 \Sigma_1 V_1^T$}
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{The dynamical generalised Nystr\"{o}m methods}\label{sec2.3}
The Generalised  Nystr\"{o}m method, as an important alternative to randomized SVD, exhibits unique advantages in the field of low-rank matrix approximation. The classical  Nystr\"{o}m method was first proposed in \cite{nystroem1930praktische} and widely applied in \cite{williams2000using}. Its core idea is to construct a rank $r$ approximation for a positive semi-definite (PSD) matrix \(A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}\) ( \(A \succeq 0\) ):
\begin{equation*}
	A\approx AX\left(X^TAX\right)^\dagger(AX)^T,
\end{equation*}
where \(X \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times r}\) is a random sketch matrix. This method performs excellently in kernel matrix processing \cite{fowlkes2004spectral,scholkopf2002learning,williams2000using}. Notably, \cite{halko2011finding,martinsson2019randomized} discovered through theoretical analysis and numerical experiments that introducing a rangefinder can significantly improve the approximation accuracy:
\begin{equation*}
	A\approx AQ\left(Q^TAQ\right)^\dagger(AQ)^T,\quad Q=\text{rangefinder}(AX).
\end{equation*}
This improved form outperforms randomized SVD in most scenarios \cite{gittens2013revisiting}. 

To adapt to general rectangular matrices \(A \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}\), the generalised Nystrom method was proposed \cite{clarkson2009numerical,tropp2017practical,woodruff2014sketching}. Its complete description and error analysis can be found in \cite{nakatsukasa2020fast}: Given oversampling parameters $p$ and \(\ell\), generate random sketch matrices \(X \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times (r+p)}\) and \(Y \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times (r+p+\ell)}\), then the rank $r$ approximation is:
\begin{equation*}
	A\approx AX\left(Y^TAX\right)_r^\dagger Y^TA,
\end{equation*}
and by using double oversampling parameters ( \(p > 0, \ell > 0\) ) combined with a specific calculation order can significantly improve the algorithm's accuracy and numerical stability.

Further, the Generalized Nyström method was constructed  \cite{carrel2024randomised}, which is a stochastic algorithm for the low-rank approximation of rectangular matrices. This approximation is achieved by randomly sampling both the row and column spaces, which improves the accuracy and stability by double-sketching structure:
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.8}
A\approx\tilde{A}=AQ_2\left(Q_1^TAQ_2\right)_r^\dagger Q_1^TA,\quad
\begin{cases}
	Q_1=\operatorname{rangefinder}(AX) \\
	Q_2=\operatorname{rangefinder}(A^TY)& 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

Motivated by The Dynamic Generalized Nyström \cref{eq2.8}, based on the Dynamical Rangefinder \cref{eq2.9} and the proposed Dynamical Co-rangefinder \cref{eq2.10},   extends the Generalized Nyström approach to address time-dependent matrix differential equations \cref{eq2.2}
\begin{equation*}
	\dot{N}(t)=F(t,N(t)),\quad\quad N(t_0)=N_0.
\end{equation*}
Firstly, the orthogonal bases for the row space and column space are obtained using the dynamic rangefinder and co-rangefinder, respectively. Furthermore, the initial low-rank bases are combined with the dynamically estimated bases to derive the new orthogonal bases.
\begin{align*}
	\tilde{Q}_1 &= \text{DynamicalRangefinder}(A_0, h, r, p, q), \qquad\qquad Q_1 = \text{orth}([U_0, \tilde{Q}_1]), \\
	\tilde{Q}_2 &= \text{DynamicalCo-rangefinder}(A_0, h, r, p + \ell, q),\qquad Q_2 = \text{orth}([V_0, \tilde{Q}_2]).
\end{align*}
Then, the original high-dimensional problem is projected into a low-dimensional row (or column) space, resulting in the generation of three small matrices:
\begin{equation*}
	\mathcal{B}(t_{0}+h)=N(t_{0}+h)Q_2,\quad\mathcal{C}(t_{0}+h)^T=Q_1^TN(t_{0}+h),\quad\mathcal{D}(t_{0}+h)=Q_1^TN(t_{0}+h)Q_2.
\end{equation*}
Accordingly, the following three corresponding differential equations are obtained:
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{cases}
		\boldsymbol{\dot{\mathcal{B}}}(t)=F(t,N(t))Q_2, \\
		\mathcal{B}(0)=N_0Q_2, & 
	\end{cases}\quad
	\begin{cases}
		\boldsymbol{\dot{\mathcal{C}}}(t)=F(t,N(t))^TQ_1, \\
		\mathcal{C}(0)=N_0^TQ_1, & 
	\end{cases}\quad
	\begin{cases}
		\boldsymbol{\dot{\mathcal{D}}}(t)=Q_1^TF(t,N(t))Q_2, \\
		\mathcal{D}(0)=Q_1^TN_0Q_2. & 
	\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
However, since N(t) is unknown, these three equations are purely theoretical. For further calculations, we assume that $range(N(t))\subset range(Q_1)$ and $corange(N(t))\subset range(Q_{2})$ holds for all $t\in(t_0,t_0+h)$, resulting in the following:
\begin{equation*}
	N(t)=N(t)Q_2Q_2^T=\mathcal{B}(t)Q_2^T,\quad N(t)=Q_1Q_1^TN(t)=Q_1\mathcal{C}(t)^T,\quad N(t)=Q_1\mathcal{D}(t)Q_2^T.
\end{equation*}
Thus, the following three small autonomous differential equations are obtained:
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{cases}
		\dot{B}(t)=F(t,B(t)Q_2^T)Q_2, \\
		B(0)=A_0Q_2, & 
	\end{cases}\quad
	\begin{cases}
		\dot{C}(t)=F(t,Q_1C(t)^T)^TQ_1, \\
		C(0)=A_0^TQ_1, & 
	\end{cases}\quad
	\begin{cases}
		\dot{D}(t)=Q_1^TF(t,Q_1D(t)Q_2^T)Q_2, \\
		D(0)=Q_1^TA_0Q_2, & 
	\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
which are row-space evolution, column-space evolution, and alternate coupling. And these three differential equations can be solved in parallel. After the computation, the final approximate solution of the DGN is obtained through truncation and assembly
\begin{equation*}
	N(t_{0}+h)\approx N_1=B(t_{0}+h)D(t_{0}+h)_r^\dagger C(t_{0}+h).
\end{equation*}
where $\cdot_r$ represents the truncation to the target rank $r$. Additionally, while $\mathrm{range}(N(t))\subset\mathrm{range}(Q_1),\mathrm{range}(N(t)^T)\subset\mathrm{range}(Q_2),$ for all $t\in(t_0,t_0+h)$, the DGN is exact, see in \cite[Lemma 3.2]{carrel2024randomised}.

The dynamic generalized Nyström method decomposes high-dimensional differential equations into low-dimensional parallel problems by dynamically estimating the row and column spaces, combining truncated singular value decomposition (SVD) with orthogonal projection, and utilizing parallel computation. This approach achieves efficient and highly accurate low-rank approximations for large-scale, stiff and conservation-demanding physics problems.

Similar to the adaptive dynamic stochastic singular value decomposition (ADRSVD), the adaptive dynamic generalized Nystrom (ADGN) method can easily be obtained. The range and co-range finders in it are replaced by corresponding adaptive methods that perform truncated singular value decomposition based on tolerances.



\subsection{The dynamical rangefinder }\label{sec2.4}
For this time-dependent eq.~\eqref{eq2.3}
\begin{equation*}
	\dot{N}(t)=F(t,N(t)),\quad\quad N(t_0)=N_0\in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}
\end{equation*}
consider the first time step, the aim is to find the orthogonal matrix $Q_h\in \mathbb{R}^{m \times (r+p)}$, where $p\geq0$ denotes the oversampling parameter, making
\begin{equation*}
	\left\lVert N(t_0+h) - Q_h Q_h^T N(t_0+h) \right\rVert_F \overset{!}{=} \min.
\end{equation*}
inspired by the randomised rangefinder \cite{halko2011finding}, we obtain an ideal  algorithm:

1. Sketch a random Gaussian matrix $\Omega\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times(r+p)}$.

2. Compute the $\mathcal{B}(t_0+h)=N(t_0+h)\Omega$ or, equivalently, solve the differential equation:
\begin{equation*}
	\dot{\mathcal{B}}(t)=F(t,N(t))\Omega,\quad\mathcal{B}(t_0)=N_0\Omega.
\end{equation*}

3. Extracted the orthogonal basis $\mathcal{Q}_h=\operatorname{orth}(\mathcal{B}(t_0+h))$.
\begin{algorithm}[H]
	\caption{Dynamical Generalised Nyström (DGN)}
	\label{alg:dgn}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\REQUIRE Initial value \( N_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n} \) and \(N_{0}\approx U_{0}S_{0}V_{0}^{T}\in\mathcal{M}_{r}\), step-size \( h > 0 \), target rank \( r > 0 \), oversampling parameters \( p \geq 0 \) and \( \ell \geq 0 \), number of power iterations \( q \geq 1 \).

		%\Statex \textbf{Stage A: Estimation of the range and co-range}
		\STATE Compute in parallel: %\Comment{See Algorithm 2}
		\[
		\tilde{Q}_1 = \text{DynamicalRandomisedRangefinder}(N_0, h, r, p, q)
		\]
		\[
		\tilde{Q}_2 = \text{DynamicalRandomisedCorangefinder}(N_0, h, r, p+\ell, q)
		\]
		\STATE Augment the two bases: 
		\[
		Q_1 = \operatorname{orth}([U_0, \tilde{Q}_1]), \quad Q_2 = \operatorname{orth}([V_0, \tilde{Q}_2]).
		\]

		%\Statex \textbf{Stage B: Solve three small differential equations in parallel}
		\STATE Compute \( B(h) \) by solving the \( m \times (r + p + \ell) \) differential equation (row space evolution):
		\[
		\dot{B}(t) = F(t,B(t)Q_2^T)Q_2, \quad B(t_0) = A_0 Q_2.
		\]
		\STATE Compute \( C(h) \) by solving the \( n \times (r + p) \) differential equation (column space evolution):
		\[
		\dot{C}(t) = F(t,Q_1C(t)^T)^T Q_1, \quad C(t_0) = A_0^T Q_1.
		\]
		\STATE Compute \( D(h) \) by solving the \( (r + p) \times (r + p + \ell) \) differential equation (interaction coupling terms):
		\[
		\dot{D}(t) = Q_1^T F(t,Q_1D(t)Q_2^T)Q_2, \quad D(t_0) = Q_1^T A_0 Q_2.
		\]

		%\Statex \textbf{Stage C: Truncate and assemble according to Nyström's formula}
		\STATE Conduct a SVD of \( D(t_0+h) = U_hS_hV_h^T \)
		\STATE Truncate to the target rank: \( \mathcal{T}_r(D(t_0+h)) = U_rS_rV_r^T \) %\Comment{and machine precision}
		\STATE Compute QR decompositions and assemble:
		\[
		UR_{\mathrm{l}}=B(t_{0}+h)U_{r}, \quad VR_{2}=C(t_{0}+h)^{T}V_{r} \quad \leadsto \quad S = R_1 S_r R_2^T.
		\]
		\RETURN {\( N_1 = U S V^T \)}.
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
\noindent However, it should be noted that we do not know $N (t)$ for all $t>t_0$, so we cannot solve it directly. Therefore, the proposed design below relies exclusively on the evolution equation of $B(t)$, eliminating the need for the explicit use of the $N(t)$.

First, under assumption $\operatorname{rank}(N(t_0+h))=R\leq r+p$, through singular value decomposition (SVD) of $N(t_0+h)$ , we can derive $N(t_0+h)\Omega=U_{h}\Sigma_{h}V_{h}^{T}\Omega$, where $\begin{aligned} U_h\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times R} & \mathrm{~and~}V_h\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times R} \end{aligned}$ denote orthogonal matrices. Given that $\Omega$ is a Gaussian matrix, it follows that $V_{h}^{T}\Omega$ exhibits row full rank. Consequently, we establish that $\mathrm{Range}(\mathcal{B}(t_0+h))=\mathrm{Range}(A(t_0+h)\Omega)=\mathrm{Range}(A(t_0+h))$. Moreover, since $\mathcal{Q}_{h}$ constitutes an orthogonal basis within the column space of $N(t_0+h)$, the resulting projection is residue-free,
\begin{equation*}
	\left\|N(t_0+h)-P_{\mathcal{B}(t_0+h)}A(t_0+h)\right\|_F=\left\|A(t_0+h)-\mathcal{Q}_h\mathcal{Q}_h^TA(t_0+h)\right\|_F=0.
\end{equation*}
To recover the column space of $A(t)$ from $B(t)$, the pseudo-inverse operation of $(\Omega^{T}\Omega)^{-1}\Omega^{T}$ is employed. After the preceding analysis, the original equation can be transformed into a new autonomous $m\times(r+p)$ differential equation that solely depends on $B(t)$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.9}
	\dot{B}(t)=F(t,B(t)(\Omega^T\Omega)^{-1}\Omega^T)\Omega,\quad B(t_0)=A_0\Omega.
\end{equation}
This differential equation can be efficiently solved with established computational methods (e.g.,Runge-Kutta method).

Furthermore, leveraging symmetry principles allows for the straightforward derivation of \textbf{the dynamic co-rangefinder}. By defining $C(t_0+h)=A(t_0+h)^{T}\Omega$, an additional autonomous differential equation is yielded:
\begin{equation}\label{eq2.10}
	\dot{C}(t)=F(t,\Omega(\Omega^T\Omega)^{-1}C(t)^T)^T\Omega,\quad C(t_0)=N_0^T\Omega.
\end{equation}

It should be noted that a single iteration of range finding may be associated with a relatively large error. However, this error can be progressively minimized by appropriately increasing the number of power iterations $(q\geq0)$. Through an iterative process of alternately solving for $B(t)$ and $C(t)$, the orthogonal basis can be incrementally optimized, thereby enhancing the accuracy of the solution.

\subsection{The adaptive dynamical rangefinder }\label{sec2.5}
However, in dynamic low-rank approximations, the target rank is typically not known in advance. Consequently, the adaptive randomised rangefinder based on tolerance to dynamically adjust the target rank $r$ is proposed in \cite{halko2011finding}. This method relies on the following lemma from \cite{woolfe2008fast}:
\begin{lemma}
	Let $A\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$, fix a positive integer $K$ and a real number $\alpha>1$. Draw an independent family $\{\boldsymbol{\omega}^{(i)}:i=1,2,\ldots,K\}$ of standard Gaussian vectors. Then, 
\begin{equation*}
	\left\|A\right\|_2\leq\alpha\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\cdot\max_{i=1,\ldots,K}\left\|A\omega^{(i)}\right\|_2
\end{equation*}
with probability $1-\alpha^{-K}$.
\end{lemma}

Let $A=N(t_0+h)-QQ^TN(t_0+h)$ and $\alpha=10$ the following conclusion is obtained:
\begin{equation*}
	\left\|N(t_0+h)-QQ^TN(t_0+h)\right\|_2\leq10\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\cdot\max_{i=1,\ldots,K}\left\|N(t_0+h)\omega^{(i)}-QQ^TN(t_0+h)\omega^{(i)}\right\|_2
\end{equation*}
with probability $1-10^{-K}$. 

\begin{algorithm}[ht]
	\caption{Adaptive Dynamical Rangefinder}
	\label{alg:adaptive_rangefinder}
	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
		\REQUIRE Initial value \( N_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n} \), step-size \( h > 0 \), tolerance \( \tau > 0 \), max failure probability \( \beta > 0 \)

		\STATE Set \( K = -\left\lfloor \frac{\log(\beta)}{\log(10)} \right\rfloor \) and \( \varepsilon = \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}} \cdot \frac{\tau}{10} \). %\Comment{Block size and tolerance}
		\STATE Initialize a Gaussian random matrix \( \Omega \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times K} \).
		%\State Compute \( W^T = (\Omega^T \Omega)^{-1} \Omega^T \)
		\STATE Compute \( B(t_0+h) \) by solving the \( m \times K \) differential equation:
		\[
		\dot{B}(t) = F(t,B(t)\Omega^T \Omega)^{-1} \Omega^T) \Omega, \quad B(t_0) = N_0 \Omega.
		\]
		\STATE Extract the basis: \( Q_h = \text{orth}(B(h)) \). %\Comment{With pivoted QR, or else}
		\STATE Set \( E = \infty \) and \( j = 1 \)

		\WHILE{\( E > \varepsilon \)} 
		\STATE Generate a new Gaussian random matrix \( \Omega \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times K} \). %\Comment{Draw new random vectors}
		%\State Compute \( W^T = (\Omega^T \Omega)^{-1} \Omega^T \)
		\STATE Compute \( B(t_0+h) \) by solving the \( m \times K \) differential equation:
		\[
		\dot{B}(t) = F(t,B(t)\Omega^T \Omega)^{-1} \Omega^T) \Omega, \quad B(t_0) = N_0 \Omega.
		\]
		\STATE Compute residual: \(\tilde{B}=B(t_0+h)-Q_{h}Q_{h}^{T}B(t_0+h)\).
		\STATE Update \(E=\max_{i=1,...,K}\left\|\tilde{B}[:,i]\right\|\).
		%\If{\(\max_{i=1,\ldots,K} \left\| E[:, i] \right\| > \varepsilon\)} \Comment{Augment the basis}
		\STATE Set \( j = j + 1 \) and update the basis: \( Q_h = \text{orth}([Q_h, \tilde{B}]) \).
		%	\Comment{With a QR update, or else}
		%			\EndIf
		\ENDWHILE
		\RETURN {\( Q_h \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times jK} \)}.
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Recall that the dynamical range finder facilitates the approximation $B(t_0+h)\approx N(t_0+h)\Omega$ where $\Omega$ is a Gaussian matrix, which leads to $N(t_0+h)\omega^{(i)}=N(t_0+h)\Omega_{[:,i]}\approx B(t_0+h)_{[:,i]}$. In other words, this approach approximates the residual $\left\|N(t_0+h)\omega^{(i)}-QQ^TN(t_0+h)\omega^{(i)}\right\|$ almost exactly as $\left\|\tilde{B}[:,i]\right\|$. In addition, \cite{carrel2024randomised} opts for block computation (adding $K$ columns each time) to cut iterations:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep] % 主列表无缩进、无额外间距
	\item[]1. Sketch a random Gaussian matrix $\tilde{\Omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}$.

	\item[]2. Solve the \cref{eq2.8} to get $B(t_0+h)\approx N(t_0+h)\tilde{\Omega}$.

	\item[]3. Extract the basis $Q=\operatorname{orth}(B(h))$.

	\item[]4. Draw a new random sketch matrix $\Omega\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times K}$.

	\item[]5. Solve the \cref{eq2.8} to get a new $B(h)\approx A(h)\Omega$.

	\item[]6. Compute the error $\max_{i=1,\ldots,K}\left\|B(t_0+h)_{[:,i]}-QQ^TB(t_0+h)_{[:,i]}\right\|$.

	\item[]7. If the error is too large, update $Q$ with the new base element in \(B(t_0+h)\) and repeat from step 4.
\end{itemize}
Further balancing accuracy and speed. This approach offers a flexible and efficient adaptive framework, making it highly suitable for large-scale problems with unknown or time-dependent target ranks.

Similar to the dynamic range finder, it is straightforward to exploit symmetry to derive an additional autonomous differential equation \cref{eq2.9}. This equation forms the basis of the adaptive dynamic co-range finder.

\section{Convergence results}\label{sec3} The objective of this section is to verify the numerical outcomes of the method discussed in this paper in relation to the stiffness problem. Specifically, we will examine the convergence order of both Lie-splitting and Strang-splitting techniques. 

\subsection{Allen-Cahn equation}\label{sec3.1} 
The Allen-Cahn equation, a standard category of stiff differential equations, serves as a classical phase-field modeling framework predominantly employed to elucidate phase separation, interface dynamics, and the evolution of multiphase systems \cite{allen1972ground,allen1973correction}. The most basic representation of this equation includes a nonlinear term in the form of a cubic polynomial (reaction term) alongside a diffusion term \cite{kassam2005fourth}, specifically:
\begin{equation*}
	\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}=\varepsilon\Delta f+f-f^3,
\end{equation*}
In this context, we set $\varepsilon=0.1$. To address this equation, we utilize the Lie-Trotter splitting and Strang splitting techniques, respectively. The solution is obtained through the application of an exponential integrator for the stiff component and the (adaptive) DRSVD/DGN method for the nonlinear component. We designate the initial value as follows:
\begin{equation*}
	f_0(x,y)=\frac{\left[e^{-\tan^2(x)}+e^{-\tan^2(y)}\right]\sin(x)\sin(y)}{1+e^{|\csc(-x/2)|}+e^{|\csc(-y/2)|}}.
\end{equation*}
The governing equations are numerically solved on the two-dimensional computational domain $\Omega=[0,2\pi]^{2}$, which is discretized using a uniformly spaced grid with $1024\times1024$ nodal points. Periodic boundary conditions are imposed along all domain boundaries. Through implementation of a second-order centered finite difference discretization scheme, the partial differential equations are transformed into a system of ordinary differential equations (ODEs) expressed in matrix form:
\begin{equation*}
	\dot{X}(t)=AX(t)+X(t)A+X(t)-X(t)^{*3}.
\end{equation*}
The temporal domain is specified as $[0, T]$ with $T=1$. 

