Tables \ref{tab1} and \ref{tab2} present the relative errors in Frobenius norm and temporal convergence orders for the Allen-Cahn equation $(N=1024)$ using Lie-Totter and Strang splitting methods with the dynamic low-rank techniques (DRSVD and DGN).
For Lie splitting (Table \ref{tab1}), both DRSVD and DGN exhibit first-order convergence rate with negligible differences in errors. On the other hand, relative errors decrease consistently with refining $M$. 
Strang splitting (Table \ref{tab1}) achieves near second-order convergence for $r\geq16$, with errors decreasing sharply. While DRSVD and DGN yield nearly identical results, minor discrepancies in convergence rates suggest slight method-dependent variations. Overall, Strang splitting demonstrates superior accuracy and theoretical second-order behavior, whereas Lie-splitting maintains robust first-order convergence.


