\subsection{Differential Riccati Equation}\label{sec3.2} 
Another example of a stiff partial differential equation is called DREs, which plays a crucial role in control theory, filtering and estimation, and financial mathematics by solving problems such as optimal control, state estimation, and portfolio optimization \cite{abou2012matrix, anderson2007optimal, kalman1960new, zhou2000continuous}.

In this paper, we focus on differential Riccati equations (DREs) in the optimal control problem of linear quadratic regulators (LQRs) with parabolic partial differential equations over a finite time interval $T$. Thus, we construct the following linear control system:
\begin{equation*}%\label{eq2.5}
	\dot{x}=Ax+Bu,\quad x(t_0)=x_0,
\end{equation*}
where $A\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$ and $B\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}$ are the system matrices, $x\in\mathbb{R}^m$ is the state variable,$u\in\mathbb{R}^m$ is the control. The function $\mathcal{J}$ that need to be minimized is as follows: 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{J}(u,x)=\frac{1}{2}\int_{t_0}^T\left(x(t)^\mathsf{T}C^\mathsf{T}QCx(t)+u(t)^\mathsf{T}Ru(t)\right)\mathrm{d}t,
\end{equation*}
where $C\in\mathbb{R}^{q\times d}$, ${Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{\boldsymbol{q}\times\boldsymbol{q}}$ is symmetric and positive semidefinite and ${R}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}$ is symmetric and positive definite. Moreover, the optimal control is given in feedback form by $u_{\mathrm{opt}}(t)=-R^{-1}B^{\mathsf{T}}X(t)x(t)$, where $X(t)$ is the solution of the following differential Riccati equations:
\begin{equation*}
	\dot{X}(t)=A^\mathsf{T}X(t)+X(t)A+C^\mathsf{T}QC-X(t)BR^{-1}B^\mathsf{T}X(t).
\end{equation*}
To further demonstrate the applicability of our approaches, we utilize a test case based on the diffusion-convection equation, which was developed from \cite{penzl2000matlab}. Consider the following equation:
\begin{equation}\label{eq3.1}
	\partial_tw=\Delta w-10x\partial_xw-100y\partial_yw,\quad w|_{\partial\Omega}=0,
\end{equation}
defined on the domain $\Omega = (0, 1)^2$, subject to homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions. The matrix $A$ is derived from the spatial discretization of (\ref{eq3.1}) via standard centered finite differences, with \(\widetilde{d}\) uniformly spaced stiff points per dimension. Let \(x_{i}=i\delta\) (\(\delta = (\widetilde{d} + 1)^{-1}\), \(i = 1,\dots,\widetilde{d}\)) denote interior discretization points in the x direction. For matrix \(B \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times 1}\), its entries follow:
\(B_{i}=\begin{cases} 
	1 & \text{if } 0.1 < x_{i} \leq 0.3, \\
	0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}\).
For 
\(C \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times d}\), the entries are: \(C_{i}=\begin{cases} 
	1 & \text{if } 0.7 < x_{i} \leq 0.9, \\
	0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}\).
Additionally, \(R = I\) and \(Q = 100I\) are selected.

In the following numerical experiments, we take the initial value \(X_0 = I\), the final time \(T = 0.1\), and the reference solution calculated with dop 54. We show the results for \(N = 400\).


