\section{Numerical results for the simulation}\label{sec4}
In this section, to validate the numerical stability of the Lie splitting scheme and the Strang splitting scheme combined with adaptive low-rank methods (ADRSVD/ADGN) in long-term simulations, we focus on the following AC equation \ref{eq4.1}. The equation system is expressed as:
\begin{equation}\label{eq4.1}
   \begin{cases}
   	\partial_tu=\varepsilon^2\Delta u-f(u),\quad(t,\mathbf{x})\in(0,\infty)\times\Omega \\
   	F(u)=\theta\left[u\mathrm{ln}u+(1-u)\mathrm{ln}(1-u)\right]+2\theta_cu(1-u),\mathrm{~for}\quad0<u<1 \\
   	\left.u\right|_{t=0}=u^0, & 
   \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $u$ represents a real-valued function, ${\mathbf{\varepsilon}}$ is defined as the mobility coefficient, \(F(u)\) signifies the logarithmic Flory-Huggins potential, with \(f(u) = F^\prime(u)\). $\theta$ and $\theta_{c}$ are the absolute temperature and critical temperature respectively. Here we set ${\mathbf{\varepsilon}} = 0.1$,  $\theta = 0.8$ and $\theta_c = 1$.

Besides, we selects two-dimensional benchmark problems featuring high-curvature interfaces:

(1)~\textbf{Star-shaped interface:} This example is borrowed from \cite{mu2016new}, where 

\begin{equation*}
	u(x,y,0) = \tanh \left( \frac{0.25 + 0.1 \cos(6 \theta) -
		\sqrt{(x - 0.5)^2 + (y - 0.5^2)}}{\varepsilon \sqrt{2}} \right),
\end{equation*}
where $(x,y) \in [0,1]^2$ and
\begin{equation*}
	\theta =
	\begin{cases}
		\tan^{-1} \left( \frac{y - 0.5}{x - 0.5} \right), & x > 0.5, \\
		\pi + \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{y - 0.5}{x - 0.5} \right), & \mathrm{others}.
	\end{cases}	
\end{equation*}

(2)~\textbf{Butterﬂy-shaped interface:} This example employs the high-curvature, butterfly-shaped interface construction method proposed in \cite{mu2016new}, which is parameterized by the following equation:
\begin{equation*}
	\begin{cases}
		x(\theta)=k(a+b\cos(c\theta)\sin(d\theta))\cos(\theta), \\
		y(\theta)=k(a+b\cos(c\theta)\sin(d\theta))\sin(\theta), & 
	\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
where $\theta\in[0,2\pi]$. In this case, we set $k = 1.8$, $a = 0.40178$, $b = 0.30178$, $c = 6.0$, and $d = 3.0$.

The temporal evolution of these two initial shapes are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig5} and Fig.~\ref{fig6} respectively. 
Fig.~\ref{fig5} illustrates the dynamic evolution of numerical solutions for eq.~\eqref{eq4.1} with star-shaped initial conditions under spatial discretization parameters $N = 128$ and the time step size $h$ is $0.01$, where the Lie splitting scheme (first two rows) and Strang splitting scheme (last two rows) are combined with adaptive low-rank methods (ADRSVD and ADGN). Each column corresponds to the phase-field distribution at $T = 0,10,50,100$, respectively. The initially star-shaped interface exhibits a gradual morphological evolution: the tip regions (high-curvature areas) contract inward, while the gap regions (low-curvature areas) expand outward, ultimately approaching a circular morphology-a behavior consistent with curvature-driven flow dynamics. Numerical results demonstrate that both splitting schemes yield solutions with high consistency in interface positioning, curvature distribution, and energy dissipation characteristics.

Fig.~\ref{fig6} presents numerical simulations of the dynamic evolution for Equation \ref{eq4.1} with butterfly-shaped initial conditions under spatial discretization parameters $N= 128$ comparing the Lie splitting scheme (first two rows) and Strang splitting scheme (bottom tworows) combined with adaptive low-rank methods (ADRSVD and ADGN). Each column correspondsto phase-field distributions at $T = 0$, $T = 1$ ,$T = 5$, and $T = 10$. The initial butterfly-shaped interface evolves with high-curvature regions contracting inward and low-curvature regions expanding outward, leading to a smoothed morphology over time. Results demonstrate consistent accuracy and stability in capturing interface evolution for both splitting schemes, while the adaptive low-rank methods effectively preserve phase-field quality without significant numerical dissipation or interface distortion, validating their robustness in handling complex geometric initial-value problems.

\section{Concluding remarks}
\label{sec5}

In this study, we propose a hybrid numerical integrator based on the operator splitting strategy to efficiently solve stiff and semilinear matrix equations. We thoroughly consider the characteristics of the equation \ref{eq2.1}, dividing the problem into a stiff linear component and a non-stiff nonlinear component. We employ exponential integrators for the stiff part and dynamic low-rank methods for the nonlinear part to achieve efficient solutions. We validate the error and convergence order of our method using the Allen-Cahn equation and the Differential Riccati Equation, and the numerical results are highly promising.


